/** Blog article text (English only; see README, Blog). */
export const blogArticles = {
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.title': 'How to Compress a PDF Without Losing Quality',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.description': 'Why PDFs get so large and how to shrink them without ruining text or photos, using a free method that runs in your browser and never uploads your file.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.excerpt': 'Find out what makes a PDF heavy, which kinds shrink well, and how to cut the size while keeping text sharp and selectable.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.imageAlt': 'A large PDF document being squeezed into a much smaller file, illustrated on a teal background',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.intro.1': 'A PDF that is too big to email, or too slow to upload to a portal with a size limit, is one of the most common small annoyances in office life. The usual advice is to run it through an online compressor, but many of those services upload your document to a server, and the results are often disappointing because nobody explains what is actually making the file large.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.intro.2': 'This guide explains where the weight in a PDF comes from, what can and cannot be removed, and how to compress a file in your own browser so that text stays sharp and only the images pay the price.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.sum.1': 'Images, not text, make most PDFs large, so only the images need to be compressed.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.sum.2': 'Photo-heavy and scanned PDFs shrink well; plain text PDFs have little to remove.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.sum.3': 'Start with Recommended compression and check the result at 100% zoom before you send it.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.sum.4': 'Compress once, at the end, because every round of recompression costs a little image quality.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s1.h': 'Why PDF files get so large',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s1.p1': 'Most of the weight in a PDF is images. A single phone photo can add several megabytes, a scanned page is a full-page picture, and a document exported from a slide deck may embed the same artwork many times. Text is tiny by comparison: a page of typed text is usually a few kilobytes. Embedded fonts add some weight, but far less than most people expect.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s1.p2': 'That is why the first question is not “which tool should I use?” but “what is inside this file?”. A report with twenty photos behaves very differently from a contract made of typed text.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s2.h': 'Three kinds of PDF, three different results',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s2.l1.1': 'Text-heavy PDFs (contracts, invoices, articles) are usually small already. There is little to remove, so do not expect dramatic savings.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s2.l1.2': 'Photo-heavy PDFs (brochures, reports, exported presentations) shrink well, because the images can be recompressed and scaled down while the text is left alone.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s2.l1.3': 'Scanned PDFs, where every page is a picture, are large by nature. They can shrink the most, but only by lowering the quality of those pictures.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s3.h': 'How to compress a PDF in your browser',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s3.p1': '<tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> works on your own device, so the file is not uploaded anywhere. The steps are short:',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). If it is password-protected, unlock it first.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.2': 'The tool recompresses the embedded JPEG images and leaves text, links and fonts as they are.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.3': 'Pick a level: Less compression keeps images almost unchanged, Recommended compression balances size and quality, and Extreme compression gives the smallest file with visibly softer images.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.4': 'Run the compression, compare the sizes before and after, optionally rename the file, preview it, and download the result.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s3.p2': 'If the file could not be made smaller, the tool tells you so and gives you your original file back instead of a copy that is no better. That normally means the PDF is mostly text, or its images are already small.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s4.h': 'Choosing the right level',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s4.p1': 'Start with Recommended compression and judge the result at the size you will actually use it. For a document read on a screen, small losses of photo detail are rarely noticeable. For something you will print, or that has small print inside images, start with Less compression and compare.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s4.n1': 'Tip: zoom to 100% on a page with a photo before you decide. Compression artefacts that are invisible when the page is fitted to the window can appear when you zoom in.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s5.h': 'What about scans?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s5.p1': 'Scanned PDFs are the largest because every page is a picture. They respond well to Extreme compression, which scales the pictures down and lowers their quality more. The text of a scan is part of the picture, so it stays unselectable either way. If you need searchable text, run <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf>. It adds an invisible text layer on top of the pages (English only). Our guide to <post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>making a scanned PDF searchable</post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable> explains how.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s6.h': 'Other ways to make a PDF smaller',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s6.l1.1': 'Split it. If the recipient only needs some pages, <tool_split_pdf>Split PDF</tool_split_pdf> extracts just those.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s6.l1.2': 'Shrink the pictures before they go into the PDF. Compress or resize photos with <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor>, then build the file with <tool_images_to_pdf>Images to PDF</tool_images_to_pdf>. Our guide to <post_convert-images-to-pdf>converting images to PDF</post_convert-images-to-pdf> covers the details.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s6.l1.3': 'Prefer JPG over PNG for photographs. PNG is lossless and much larger for photos, and the tool only recompresses JPEG images.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s6.l1.4': 'Compress once, at the end. When you combine files with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>, compress the merged file a single time, because every round of recompression costs a little more image quality.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s7.h': 'How small does it need to be?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s7.p1': 'Most limits you will meet are set by the place that receives the file, not by the PDF format. Many email services cap attachments at roughly 20 to 25 MB in total, and job portals, tax sites, university systems and government forms often allow far less, sometimes only a few megabytes. Read the limit before you start so you can stop compressing as soon as the file fits.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s7.p2': 'If you are close to the limit, remember that an email adds a little overhead to every attachment, so aim a comfortable margin under the number rather than exactly on it. If you are far above it, compression alone may not be enough. Combine it with the other options above: remove pages nobody needs, or send two smaller files instead of one large one.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s8.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s8.l1.1': 'Compressing a file that is already compressed. A PDF that has been through a compressor, or exported at low quality, has little left to give, and another pass only adds artefacts.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s8.l1.2': 'Judging quality at the wrong zoom level. A page that looks fine fitted to the window can show blocky photos at 100%.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s8.l1.3': 'Choosing Extreme compression for photos that must stay sharp, such as a portfolio or a print job. Start gentler and compare.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s8.l1.4': 'Forgetting to keep the original. Always save the compressed copy under a new name so you can go back to the full-quality file.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s8.l1.5': 'Expecting a miracle from a text-only PDF. If the file is 200 KB of text, there is nothing worth removing.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s9.h': 'What happens to your file',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s9.p1': 'Everything described here runs inside your browser tab. The PDF is read from your device, processed locally and offered back as a download. Nothing is sent to our servers, which is also why very large files depend on your computer’s memory. Close the tab and the file is gone.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.1': 'Work out what is in the file: photos, scans or plain text.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.2': 'Try Recommended compression first.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.3': 'Compare the result at 100% zoom.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.4': 'Use Less compression if quality matters more than size, and Extreme compression if size matters more.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.5': 'For scans, run OCR afterwards if you need searchable text.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.1.q': 'Does compressing a PDF reduce its quality?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.1.a': 'Only the images are affected: they lose some detail in exchange for a smaller size. Text and vector graphics are not changed. How much you notice depends on the level you choose and how closely you zoom in.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.2.q': 'Why did my PDF barely get smaller?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.2.a': 'PDFs that are mostly text, or whose images are already small, have little to remove. Only embedded JPEG images are recompressed, so a PDF built from PNG images or vector artwork gains little. In that case the tool gives you your original file back.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.3.q': 'Is it safe to compress a confidential PDF this way?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.3.a': 'The compression runs in your browser and the file is not uploaded, so it does not leave your device. As with any document, avoid working on it on a shared or public computer.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.4.q': 'What is the difference between compressing a PDF and resizing its pages?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.4.a': 'Compression changes how the images inside the file are stored. Resizing pages changes the paper size and does not make the pictures lighter. For a smaller file, compression is the tool you want.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.5.q': 'Can I compress several PDFs at once?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.5.a': 'Compress them one at a time, or merge them first and compress the combined file once. Merging first also avoids paying the quality cost of compression twice.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.6.q': 'Will compression remove my bookmarks, links or form fields?',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.faq.6.a': 'No. The text, links and fonts are left alone, so they keep working. Only the embedded images are recompressed.',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.compress-pdf-without-losing-quality.cta.p': 'Open <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.title': 'How to Convert Images to PDF: JPG, PNG and Mixed Photos',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.description': 'Turn photos, screenshots and scans into one tidy PDF. Choose the page order, page size and margins, keep full image quality, and do it all in your browser.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.excerpt': 'Combine JPG and PNG images into a single PDF with the page order, size and margins you want, without uploading anything.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.imageAlt': 'Several photos and screenshots stacking into a single PDF document on a teal background',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.intro.1': 'Forms, receipts, homework, ID copies and product photos often arrive as a pile of image files, while the person asking for them wants one PDF. Converting images to PDF is simple, but a few choices decide whether the result looks professional or sloppy: the order of the pages, the page size, the margins and what happens to image quality along the way.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.intro.2': 'This guide walks through those choices and shows how to build the PDF in your browser, without installing software and without sending your pictures to a server.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.sum.1': 'One tool turns JPG, PNG and mixed images into a single PDF, with one image per page.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.sum.2': 'Choose the page size (A4, Letter or Fit to image), the order and the margins before you create the file.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.sum.3': 'Photographing paper pages? Scan to PDF adds clean-up that makes text easier to read.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.sum.4': 'Resize or compress very large photos first, so the PDF stays small enough to send.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s1.h': 'Pick the right tool for your images',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s1.p1': 'There are three converters, and the right one depends on what you have:',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s1.l1.1': '<tool_jpg_to_pdf>JPG to PDF</tool_jpg_to_pdf> when every file is a JPG. The JPEG data is embedded as it is, without re-compression, so quality does not drop.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s1.l1.2': '<tool_png_to_pdf>PNG to PDF</tool_png_to_pdf> for screenshots and graphics. Transparency is kept; transparent areas show the white page behind them.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s1.l1.3': '<tool_images_to_pdf>Images to PDF</tool_images_to_pdf> for a mix. JPG and PNG are embedded directly, and WebP, GIF or BMP files are converted to PNG first if your browser can read them.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s2.h': 'Step by step',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Add your images. You can drop several at once; each image becomes one page.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Put them in order by dragging the rows or using the arrows. The order shown is the page order.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Choose a page size: A4, US Letter, or Fit to image, which sizes each page to its picture.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Set the orientation and margin. Automatic orientation follows each image, and margins range from none to large.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s2.o1.5': 'Create the PDF and download it.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s3.h': 'A4, Letter or Fit to image?',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s3.p1': 'Use A4 or US Letter when the PDF will be printed, emailed as a document or uploaded to a portal that expects standard pages. Each image is scaled to fit inside the page, keeps its proportions and is centred, so a wide photo on a portrait page gets bands of white above and below rather than being stretched.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s3.p2': 'Choose Fit to image when the pictures are the content: a set of screenshots, a comic, a photo album. Every page takes the size of its image, so nothing is scaled or padded and nothing is cropped.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s4.h': 'Getting the order and orientation right',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s4.p1': 'File names rarely sort the way you want, especially when photos come from a phone, so check the list before creating the PDF. If some pictures are sideways, fix them first with the image tools and add them again. With A4 or Letter, Automatic orientation chooses landscape for wide images and portrait for tall ones, which is usually what you want for a mixed set. Pick a fixed orientation only when every page must match.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s4.n1': 'Tip: for documents such as receipts, take the photo straight on, in good light and with the page filling the frame. A cleaner picture gives a cleaner PDF, and a scanned page is far easier to read later with <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf>.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s5.h': 'Keeping the file size reasonable',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s5.p1': 'A modern phone photo is often 3 to 8 MB, so twenty of them make a PDF too big for most email limits. Because JPEG data is embedded without re-compression, the PDF is about as large as the pictures combined. Two fixes work well: reduce the pictures before converting with <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> or <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer>, or compress the finished PDF with <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>. We compare both in <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>how to compress a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s6.h': 'After the PDF exists',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s6.l1.1': 'Need this PDF inside a longer document? Combine them with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s6.l1.2': 'Only some pages needed? Extract them with <tool_split_pdf>Split PDF</tool_split_pdf>.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s6.l1.3': 'Going the other way? <tool_pdf_to_jpg>PDF to JPG</tool_pdf_to_jpg> turns pages back into pictures.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s7.h': 'Photographing paper pages: use Scan to PDF',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s7.p1': 'A photo of a printed page is not the same as a scan. It has uneven light, soft shadows from your hand or the phone, and a slightly grey background, and all of that shows up when someone reads or prints the PDF. <tool_scan_to_pdf>Scan to PDF</tool_scan_to_pdf> is built for that case. You add the page photos, put them in order, and choose a clean-up style: Original keeps the colours, Grayscale removes them, and Black and white document evens out shadows and turns the page into dark text on white paper.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s7.p2': 'The black and white mode suits printed pages and handwritten notes. It is not meant for photographs, where it would throw away tone and detail. Scan to PDF does not recognise the text, so the result is still a set of pictures. If you need to search or copy the words, run <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf> afterwards, as described in our guide to <post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>making a scanned PDF searchable</post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s7.p3': 'On a phone or tablet whose browser supports it, a Take a photo button opens the camera directly. Otherwise choose pictures that are already saved on the device.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s8.h': 'Tips for sharper, better-looking pages',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'Photograph pages in daylight or under an even lamp, with the phone directly above the paper instead of at an angle.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'Fill the frame with the page and keep the edges straight; crop out the table around it before building the PDF.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'Hold the camera steady or use a timer, because a slightly blurred photo cannot be repaired afterwards.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'Use the same orientation for every page. If one page is sideways, rotate the picture first so the PDF opens the right way up.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s8.l1.5': 'Name your files in the order you want them, for example page-01, page-02, so the list is already sorted when you add them.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s9.h': 'Limits worth knowing',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s9.l1.1': 'Up to 25 MB per image and 100 images per PDF.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s9.l1.2': 'JPG to PDF accepts JPG files only; use Images to PDF for mixed formats.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.s9.l1.3': 'Everything runs in your browser, so speed and the number of images you can handle depend on your device’s memory.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Does converting a JPG to PDF reduce its quality?',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.1.a': 'No. JPG files are embedded in the PDF as they are, without re-compression, so the picture is identical to the original.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Can I put different image formats in one PDF?',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.2.a': 'Yes, with Images to PDF. JPG and PNG are embedded directly, and WebP, GIF and BMP files are converted to PNG first if your browser can decode them.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.3.q': 'What is the difference between Images to PDF and Scan to PDF?',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.3.a': 'Images to PDF places your pictures on pages as they are. Scan to PDF does the same and can also clean up photographed paper pages, for example by evening out shadows and turning them into black and white. Neither tool recognises text.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.4.q': 'Why does my iPhone photo not appear in the list?',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.4.a': 'Some phone formats, such as HEIC, cannot be read by a browser. Save or export the picture as a JPG first, or change the camera format to the most compatible option, and add it again.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.5.q': 'How many images can I put in one PDF?',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Images to PDF handles many pictures in one go, within the size limits shown on the tool page. If you have far more, build several PDFs and join them with Merge PDF.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.6.q': 'Are my pictures uploaded when I convert them?',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.faq.6.a': 'No. The PDF is built inside your browser from files on your device, and nothing is sent to our servers.',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.convert-images-to-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_images_to_pdf>Images to PDF</tool_images_to_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.title': 'HEX, RGB and HSL Colors Explained With a Contrast Check',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.description': 'Learn how HEX, RGB, HSL and HSV colours work, convert between them, pick exact colours from an image and check text contrast against accessibility rules.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.excerpt': 'A clear guide to colour codes: what HEX, RGB, HSL and HSV mean, how to convert between them, how to sample a colour from a picture and how to check contrast for readable text.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.imageAlt': 'Colour swatches with hexadecimal codes next to a colour wheel on a teal background',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.intro.1': 'Every colour on a screen is written down as numbers. Designers hand developers a code such as 0f766e. A style guide lists a colour as rgb with three values. A design tool shows hue, saturation and lightness. All of them describe the same colour, but each form is useful for a different job.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.intro.2': 'This guide explains the most common colour formats, shows how to convert between them, how to take an exact colour from a picture and how to check that text on a coloured background is easy to read.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.sum.1': 'HEX, RGB, HSL and HSV are different ways to write the same colour on a screen.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.sum.2': 'HEX and RGB describe how much red, green and blue light to mix. HSL and HSV describe hue, strength and brightness, which is easier for people to adjust.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.sum.3': 'The contrast ratio between text and background decides whether text is readable. WCAG asks for at least 4.5 to 1 for normal text.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.sum.4': 'You can take exact colours from any picture with a colour picker.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.sum.5': 'Screens use the sRGB colour space for most web work, and print uses a different system.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s1.h': 'How screens make colours',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s1.p1': 'A screen mixes red, green and blue light. Each of the three channels has a strength from 0, which is off, to 255, which is full. Mixing the three gives about 16.7 million possible colours.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s1.p2': 'All the common formats describe this mix. They only differ in the way the numbers are written. Learning to read two or three of them lets you move between design tools, style sheets and code without guessing.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s2.h': 'HEX codes',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s2.p1': 'A HEX code is a hash sign followed by six characters. The first two characters give red, the next two green and the last two blue. Each pair is a number from 00 to ff written in base 16, where ff is 255.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s2.p2': 'So the code for pure red is ff0000, pure green is 00ff00, white is ffffff and black is 000000. The teal used on this site is 0f766e: red 0f is 15, green 76 is 118 and blue 6e is 110. A short form with three characters, such as fff for white, exists when each pair repeats.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s2.p3': 'HEX is compact and everywhere in web design, which is why designers paste it into chats and documents.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s3.h': 'RGB',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s3.p1': 'RGB writes the same three numbers in plain decimal. The teal above is rgb(15, 118, 110). It is easy to read when you want to see the mix and to compute with, for example to lighten a colour by adding to each channel.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s3.p2': 'Some systems add a fourth value for opacity. The converter accepts such values but ignores the opacity, because it works with solid colours.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s4.h': 'HSL and HSV',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s4.p1': 'HSL stands for hue, saturation and lightness. HSV stands for hue, saturation and value, and it is used by many colour pickers.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s4.l1.1': 'Hue is the position on the colour wheel, in degrees from 0 to 360. Red is 0, green is about 120 and blue is about 240.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s4.l1.2': 'Saturation is how strong the colour is, from grey at 0 percent to full colour at 100 percent.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s4.l1.3': 'Lightness in HSL, or value in HSV, is how bright it is.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s4.p2': 'These are the friendliest formats for people, because they match how we think. To make a colour lighter, raise the lightness. To make it duller, lower the saturation. To build a palette of matching shades, keep the hue and change the lightness in steps. The orange with HEX ff8000 is hsl(30, 100%, 50%) and hsv(30, 100%, 100%), and the converter shows all of these at once.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s5.h': 'Converting between formats',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s5.p1': '<tool_color_converter>Color Converter</tool_color_converter> accepts a colour in any of these forms, or from a picker, and updates every other format at the same time. It works in your browser tab, so nothing leaves your device.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s5.o1.1': 'Type a colour such as a HEX code, an rgb value or an hsl value, or choose it with the picker.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s5.o1.2': 'Read the HEX, RGB, HSL and HSV values and the live preview.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s5.o1.3': 'Copy the one you need.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s5.p2': 'Colour spaces beyond sRGB, such as LAB, LCH and Display-P3, are not supported. For ordinary web and app work, sRGB is the right choice.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s6.h': 'Picking a colour from a picture',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s6.p1': 'Sometimes the colour you need is not written down anywhere, only visible in a logo, a photo or a screenshot. <tool_image_color_picker>Pick Color from Image</tool_image_color_picker> lets you click on any point of a picture, or move with the arrow keys for fine control, and shows its HEX, RGB and HSL values with a copy button for each. It can also pick a colour from anywhere on your screen when your browser supports it.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s6.p2': 'The tool also extracts a palette of dominant colours, which is a quick start for matching a page to a photo or a logo. It samples the colours that are shown, in sRGB, and ignores embedded colour profiles, so a result can differ slightly from what a design program with colour management shows.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s6.p3': 'To improve a picture before you take colours from it, <tool_photo_editor>Photo Editor</tool_photo_editor> adjusts brightness and colour, and our guide to <post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser>editing a photo in your browser</post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser> explains the steps.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s7.h': 'Checking contrast for readable text',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s7.p1': 'A colour that looks good can still make text hard to read. The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, known as WCAG, measure this with a contrast ratio between the text colour and the background colour, from 1 to 1 for no difference up to 21 to 1 for black on white.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s7.p2': 'The common targets are:',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s7.l1.1': 'Normal text: at least 4.5 to 1 for level AA, and 7 to 1 for the stricter level AAA.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s7.l1.2': 'Large text, which means roughly 18 points or 14 points bold: at least 3 to 1 for AA and 4.5 to 1 for AAA.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s7.p3': 'The converter shows the ratio of your colour against white text and against black text, with a pass or fail for each level. The orange ff8000, for example, has a ratio of about 2.5 to 1 against white, which fails, and about 8.3 to 1 against black, which passes. So black text is the right choice on that orange, even though white text looks tempting.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s7.p4': 'Check every combination that carries information: buttons, links, labels and warnings. Do not rely on colour alone to tell something, since some people cannot tell certain colours apart.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s8.h': 'Colours in other places',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s8.p1': 'Colours matter outside web pages too.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s8.l1.1': 'In exported graphics. When you turn a logo into a picture, our guide to <post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png>converting SVG to PNG</post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png> explains how to keep its colours and transparency.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s8.l1.2': 'In QR codes. Scanners need strong contrast between dark and light modules, and <tool_qr_code_generator>QR Code Generator</tool_qr_code_generator> lets you choose colours, so check the contrast before you print. Our guide to <post_how-to-create-a-qr-code>creating a QR code</post_how-to-create-a-qr-code> covers it.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s8.l1.3': 'In documents written in Markdown, where <tool_markdown_previewer>Markdown Previewer</tool_markdown_previewer> shows how colours and styles look when rendered.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s9.h': 'Screens and print differ',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s9.p1': 'Screens mix light, and printers mix ink, usually cyan, magenta, yellow and black. Colours that glow on a screen, such as a bright electric blue, often cannot be reproduced in print and come out duller. If something will be printed, ask the printer which colour system and profile to use, and check a proof.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s10.l1.1': 'Forgetting the hash sign in a HEX code in a style sheet.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s10.l1.2': 'Choosing text colours by eye without checking contrast.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s10.l1.3': 'Relying on colour alone to show errors or status.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s10.l1.4': 'Expecting the same colour on every screen. Brightness, settings and colour profiles change what you see.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s10.l1.5': 'Using a screen colour for print without checking a proof.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s11.o1.1': 'Choose a base colour and note its HEX value.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s11.o1.2': 'Convert it to HSL, then create lighter and darker shades by changing lightness.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s11.o1.3': 'Check the contrast of every text and background pair.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s11.o1.4': 'Take colours from pictures with a picker instead of guessing.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.s11.o1.5': 'Test the result on more than one screen.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.1.q': 'What does the contrast check show?',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.1.a': 'It shows the WCAG contrast ratio of the colour against white and black text, which helps you choose readable combinations.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.2.q': 'Which colour formats does the converter understand?',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.2.a': 'HEX, RGB, HSL and HSV, and you can also choose a colour with the picker.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.3.q': 'Are transparency values supported?',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.3.a': 'Transparency (alpha) values are accepted but ignored.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.4.q': 'Does the converter support colour spaces such as LAB, LCH or Display-P3?',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.4.a': 'No. It works with sRGB only.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.5.q': 'Can I pick a colour from a photo?',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.faq.5.a': 'Yes. Use the image colour picker to click on any point and copy its HEX, RGB or HSL value.',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained.cta.p': 'Open <tool_color_converter>Color Converter</tool_color_converter>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.title': 'How to Add a Watermark to a PDF: Text, Logo and Opacity',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.description': 'Stamp a text or image watermark across your PDF pages, set size, opacity and angle, and learn what a watermark can and cannot protect.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.excerpt': 'Add DRAFT, CONFIDENTIAL or your logo to every page of a PDF, choose opacity and angle so the page stays readable, and understand the limits of watermarks.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.imageAlt': 'A PDF page with a diagonal translucent DRAFT stamp across it on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.intro.1': 'A watermark is the faint word or logo that sits across a page: DRAFT on a contract under review, CONFIDENTIAL on an internal report, a company logo on a price list, a name on a document you are sending to a specific person. It tells the reader something about the status of the document without changing the text underneath.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.intro.2': 'This guide shows how to add a text or image watermark to a PDF in your browser, how to choose size, opacity and angle so the page stays readable, and how to think about what a watermark can and cannot protect.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.sum.1': 'A watermark is drawn on top of the existing page content, with adjustable opacity and angle.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.sum.2': 'Use low opacity so the page remains easy to read.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.sum.3': 'Text watermarks use Latin letters; for other alphabets, use a transparent PNG image instead.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.sum.4': 'A watermark is a signal, not a lock. Anyone with a PDF editor can remove it.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.sum.5': 'Combine it with password protection if you need real restrictions.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.h': 'What watermarks are good for',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.p1': 'Watermarks communicate status and ownership:',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.1': 'DRAFT or FOR REVIEW tells readers a document is not final.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.2': 'CONFIDENTIAL or INTERNAL reminds people not to forward a file.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.3': 'COPY marks duplicates of original documents.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.4': 'A logo or name makes the source obvious on a handout or price list.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.5': 'A recipient’s name on a shared file discourages casual forwarding.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s1.p2': 'What they do not do is stop determined copying. Treat a watermark as a label, not as security.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s2.h': 'How to add a watermark in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s2.p1': '<tool_watermark_pdf>Watermark PDF</tool_watermark_pdf> works in your browser tab, so the PDF is read from your device. The steps:',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it has a password.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Choose a text watermark or an image watermark.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Set the size, opacity, angle and layout.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Apply it to all pages or to a selection of pages.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.5': 'Download the new PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s3.h': 'Text or image?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s3.p1': 'A text watermark is the quickest choice: type the word, pick a size and you are done. Text watermarks use the built-in PDF fonts, which cover Latin letters, digits and common symbols. If your word is in Urdu, Arabic, Chinese or another alphabet, typed text will not work. Instead, create a transparent PNG of the word, or use your logo, and choose the image option. Images can be PNG or JPG, up to 5 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s3.p2': 'An image watermark is better for logos, company stamps or any design that is more than a word. A PNG with a transparent background keeps the page readable around the shape.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s4.h': 'Opacity: the setting that matters most',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s4.p1': 'If the watermark is too strong, readers cannot read the page; if it is too weak, nobody notices it. Aim for a mark that is clearly visible when you look for it but does not fight with the text:',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s4.l1.1': '10 to 20 percent opacity works for large diagonal words.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s4.l1.2': '30 to 50 percent suits small logos in a corner.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s4.l1.3': 'Dark text on a white page tolerates a stronger mark than a page full of photos.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s4.p2': 'The watermark sits in front of the page text, so even a light mark covers a little of what is beneath. Test with a dense page, not only with the title page, and zoom in to check that small print is still readable.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s5.h': 'Angle, size and layout',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s5.p1': 'A diagonal word of about 45 degrees across the middle of the page is the classic DRAFT look. It is hard to ignore and does not cover any single line completely. Horizontal marks are more formal and work well as a header, footer or corner logo. A tiled layout repeats the mark across the page, which makes it harder to crop out but also more intrusive.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s5.p2': 'Choose a size that spans a good part of the page for status words, and a small size for logos. Always preview a representative page.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s6.h': 'Which pages to mark',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s6.p1': 'You can put the watermark on every page or only on a selection. Common patterns:',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s6.l1.1': 'Every page for DRAFT or CONFIDENTIAL.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s6.l1.2': 'Only the first page for a logo.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s6.l1.3': 'All pages except the cover for a legal notice.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s6.p2': 'If you need different marks on different parts of a document, apply them in separate runs, one for each range of pages.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s7.h': 'The limits of a watermark',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s7.p1': 'The watermark is drawn on top of the page. It is not a security feature: anyone with a PDF editor can remove or cover it. This matters in two situations:',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s7.l1.1': 'If a document contains information that must not be seen, a watermark does not hide it. Use <tool_redact_pdf>Redact PDF</tool_redact_pdf>, which removes the covered content instead of painting over it.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s7.l1.2': 'If you need to stop people from opening a file, combine the watermark with <tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf>, which encrypts the file with a password. Our guide to <post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf>protecting and unlocking a PDF</post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf> explains what a password does and does not enforce.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.h': 'Working with other tools',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.p1': 'Watermarks work well together with other changes, as long as you do them in a sensible order:',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.o1.1': 'Merge or extract pages first, so you know the final page order. <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>Merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> describes how.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.o1.2': 'Add page numbers with <tool_add_page_numbers>Add Page Numbers</tool_add_page_numbers>; <post_how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf>our numbering guide</post_how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf> explains position and format.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.o1.3': 'Apply the watermark last, so it sits above everything else.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.o1.4': 'Compress the result with <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> if the file is too large to send.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.p2': 'If the document is a form, fill it in and flatten it before watermarking; <post_how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form>filling out a PDF form</post_how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form> explains how.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s8.p3': 'Keep an unwatermarked copy. The mark is part of the page in the new file and cannot be taken out with this tool.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s9.l1.1': 'Choosing an opacity so high that the text underneath is hard to read.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s9.l1.2': 'Testing only the cover page, then discovering the mark hides a table on page 12.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s9.l1.3': 'Typing a word in a non-Latin alphabet instead of using an image.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s9.l1.4': 'Treating a watermark as protection for sensitive content.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s9.l1.5': 'Overwriting the original file.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s10.o1.1': 'Decide what the watermark should say: status, owner or recipient.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s10.o1.2': 'Choose text for a word and an image for a logo or a non-Latin word.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s10.o1.3': 'Start with low opacity and increase it only if needed.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s10.o1.4': 'Check a dense page, a photo page and the last page.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the original without the watermark.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Can the watermark be removed again?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.1.a': 'It is drawn on top of the page and is not a security feature, so anyone with a PDF editor can remove it. For stronger protection, combine it with a password.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Can I use Urdu, Arabic or other alphabets in a text watermark?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.2.a': 'Not as typed text, because the built-in PDF fonts cover only Latin letters. Make a transparent PNG of your text and use the image option.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Is the watermark in front of or behind the page text?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.3.a': 'In front. Lower the opacity so the page stays readable.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.4.q': 'What image formats are accepted for image watermarks?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.4.a': 'PNG or JPG, up to 5 MB. A PNG with a transparent background gives the cleanest result.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.5.q': 'Can I watermark only some pages?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Yes. You can apply the mark to all pages or to a selection.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.6.q': 'Is my PDF uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.faq.6.a': 'No. The tool runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_watermark_pdf>Watermark PDF</tool_watermark_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.title': 'How to Add a Watermark to Photos in Bulk, Text or Logo',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.description': 'Stamp a text or logo watermark on many photos at once, choose size, opacity, position and tiling, and learn what a watermark can and cannot protect.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.excerpt': 'A practical guide to watermarking photos: text or logo, single mark or tiled pattern, how to choose opacity and position, how to process a whole folder and what watermarks do not prevent.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.imageAlt': 'A photo with a faint logo watermark in the corner on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.intro.1': 'A watermark is a name, a logo or a short line of text placed on a picture. Photographers use it to show who made the picture, shops use it on product photos so that others do not pass the photos off as theirs, and designers use it on previews that are not yet paid for.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.intro.2': 'A watermark is not a lock, and it does not stop a determined person. Its job is to credit the author and to discourage casual copying. This guide shows how to add a watermark to a whole set of photos in your browser, how to design one that looks good, and what it can and cannot do.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.sum.1': 'You can stamp text or a logo on many pictures at once and download the results or a ZIP.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.sum.2': 'A single mark in a corner is discreet. A tiled pattern is harder to remove but more intrusive.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.sum.3': 'Choose the opacity and size so the mark is visible but does not spoil the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.sum.4': 'Marked copies are saved as new files, so your originals stay clean.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.sum.5': 'A watermark deters casual copying only. It can be cropped or edited out.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s1.h': 'Why add a watermark',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s1.p1': 'There are three common reasons.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s1.o1.1': 'Credit. A small name or website address on a photo tells viewers who made it, even when the picture is shared far away from the original page.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s1.o1.2': 'Protection of previews. A strong tiled mark on a preview makes the picture useless for final use, until the buyer receives the clean version.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s1.o1.3': 'Branding. Product photos with a consistent logo look like they belong together.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s1.p2': 'Think about which of these you need, because it decides the style. Credit calls for a small, quiet mark. Protecting a preview calls for a large, repeated mark.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s2.h': 'Text or logo',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s2.p1': '<tool_image_watermark>Watermark Image</tool_image_watermark> can stamp either kind.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s2.l1.1': 'Text is quick. Type a name, a website or a line such as Sample, and set its size, colour and opacity. Because the tool uses your device\'s fonts, any alphabet your system can show works, including Urdu, Arabic and Chinese.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s2.l1.2': 'A logo is a picture. It can be a PNG, JPG or WebP file up to 25 MB. A PNG with a transparent background looks best, because only the shape of the logo is stamped and not a coloured box around it.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s2.p2': 'If your logo is an SVG file, export it as a PNG first. Our guide to <post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png>converting SVG to PNG</post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png> explains how to do it at the right size while keeping transparency.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s3.h': 'How to add a watermark',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s3.p1': 'The work happens inside your browser tab, so your photos stay on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your photos. You can add up to 20 files in one batch, each up to 25 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s3.o1.2': 'Choose text or a logo, then set its size, opacity, position and layout.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s3.o1.3': 'Apply the watermark.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s3.o1.4': 'Download the pictures one by one, or all of them in a ZIP.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s3.p2': 'Your originals are never changed. The marked copies are saved as new files, so the clean versions stay where they were.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s4.h': 'Position and layout',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s4.p1': 'You can place a single mark or repeat it in a tiled pattern.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s4.p2': 'A single mark can go in a corner or in the centre. Corners are discreet and the usual choice for credit. Keep the mark away from the main subject, but not so close to the edge that it could be cropped off in one move. A mark across the centre is more difficult to remove, and it is more noticeable.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s4.p3': 'A tiled layout repeats the mark across the whole picture. It is the best choice for previews and proofs, because there is no clean area to crop. For finished pictures it is usually too heavy, since viewers can no longer enjoy the photo.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s5.h': 'Size and opacity',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s5.p1': 'Two settings decide how the watermark looks.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s5.l1.1': 'Size. A mark that is too small can be missed, and one that is too large takes over. A good starting point for a corner mark is around a tenth to a fifth of the picture width, and you can adjust from there.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s5.l1.2': 'Opacity. Reducing opacity lets the picture show through. Around 40 to 60 percent is a common choice for corner marks, and tiled marks are often fainter.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s5.p2': 'Always preview on a bright photo and a dark photo. A white mark that is clear on a dark picture can disappear on a bright sky, and a dark mark can do the opposite. If your set has both, choose a mid-tone colour or a mark with a thin outline.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s6.h': 'Prepare the pictures first',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s6.p1': 'Watermark at the end of your workflow, after you have edited the pictures. Do the other steps first:',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s6.o1.1': 'Edit and crop. <tool_photo_editor>Photo Editor</tool_photo_editor> adjusts colours and crops, and our guide to <post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser>editing a photo in your browser</post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser> explains it.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s6.o1.2': 'Resize to the size you will publish, with <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer>. A mark added before resizing changes size with the picture, which can make it too small or blurry.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s6.o1.3': 'Add the watermark.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s6.o1.4': 'Compress the result with <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> if the files are for the web.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s6.p2': 'Keep a clean master copy of every picture in a private place. Never delete the original after watermarking.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s7.h': 'Format and file size',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s7.p1': 'Watermarked pictures can be saved in common picture formats. If your site needs a different format, <tool_image_format_converter>Convert Image Format</tool_image_format_converter> changes it. Our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> explains which format suits which use.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s7.p2': 'Remember that re-saving a lossy format such as JPG applies compression once more. Do your edits in a sequence that keeps the number of saves low, and keep the clean master.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s8.h': 'What a watermark does not do',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s8.p1': 'Be realistic about what a watermark can achieve.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s8.l1.1': 'It does not stop copying. Anyone can screenshot or download the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s8.l1.2': 'A corner mark can be cropped away, and a mark on a plain area can be painted over with editing software.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s8.l1.3': 'It does not prove ownership by itself. Keep your original files, with their original dates, as evidence.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s8.l1.4': 'Pictures lose their metadata when they are saved again here, so the details that identify the camera and the place are not carried over. Our guide to <post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs>removing metadata from photos and PDFs</post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs> explains what that data contains.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s8.p2': 'If you also share documents, <tool_watermark_pdf>Watermark PDF</tool_watermark_pdf> applies the same idea to PDF pages, and our guide to <post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf>adding a watermark to a PDF</post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf> covers it.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s9.h': 'Hiding what should not be shared at all',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s9.p1': 'A watermark labels a picture; it does not hide anything in it. If a photo contains a licence plate, a face or an address that should not be shown, hide that part first. Our guide to <post_how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image>blurring or pixelating part of an image</post_how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image> shows how, and then you can watermark the result.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s10.l1.1': 'Making the mark so strong that nobody wants to look at the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s10.l1.2': 'Putting the mark in a corner that can be cropped off in seconds.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s10.l1.3': 'Watermarking before resizing, so the mark ends up too small.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s10.l1.4': 'Choosing a colour that disappears on some of your pictures.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s10.l1.5': 'Deleting the clean originals.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s11.o1.1': 'Edit, crop and resize the pictures first.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s11.o1.2': 'Choose text for speed or a transparent PNG logo for a clean look.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s11.o1.3': 'Pick a single mark for credit, or a tiled pattern for previews.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s11.o1.4': 'Set the opacity and size, and test on a bright and a dark picture.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.s11.o1.5': 'Download the marked copies and keep the clean masters.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.1.q': 'Can I use Urdu or other alphabets?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.1.a': 'Yes. Image watermarks use your device\'s fonts, so any script your system can show will work.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.2.q': 'Does it change my originals?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.2.a': 'No. Marked copies are saved as new files.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.3.q': 'Which logo files can I use?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.3.a': 'PNG, JPG or WebP files up to 25 MB. A PNG with a transparent background looks best.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.4.q': 'How many photos can I mark at once?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.4.a': 'Up to 20 files per batch, each up to 25 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.5.q': 'Will a watermark stop people from copying my pictures?',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.faq.5.a': 'No. It credits the author and discourages casual copying, but it can be cropped or edited out.',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos.cta.p': 'Open <tool_image_watermark>Watermark Image</tool_image_watermark>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.title': 'How to Add Page Numbers to a PDF: Position, Format and Start',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.description': 'Number the pages of any PDF with the position, format and starting page you choose, skip the cover page and avoid common numbering mistakes.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.excerpt': 'A practical guide to numbering PDF pages: where to place the numbers, how to skip the cover page, which formats exist and how to check the result.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.imageAlt': 'A PDF page with a page number stamped in the bottom corner on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.intro.1': 'A document without page numbers is hard to discuss. “Look at the table on page 14” only works when page 14 says so. Reports, contracts, theses, manuals and printed handouts all need numbers, yet many PDFs are created without them, or are merged from several files that each start at one.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.intro.2': 'You can add the numbers afterwards. This guide explains where to put them, how to start counting from the right page, which formats are available and how to check that nothing was covered up.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.sum.1': 'Page numbers are added to a copy of your PDF; the original is not changed.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.sum.2': 'Choose the position, the number format and which pages to number.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.sum.3': 'Set “First page to number” to 2 to skip a cover page.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.sum.4': 'The numbers are drawn on top of each page, so keep margins free of content.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.sum.5': 'Merge your files first and number them once, so the sequence is continuous.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s1.h': 'When to add page numbers',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.1': 'A report was exported without footers.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.2': 'You merged several PDFs and each part starts at page 1.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.3': 'A scan of a printed document has no numbers at all.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.4': 'A contract needs numbering so that nobody can claim a page was removed.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s1.l1.5': 'You are printing handouts and want readers to find pages quickly.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s1.p1': 'If your source is a word processor, adding numbers there is usually better, because the layout adapts. For a finished PDF, the approach in this guide is the practical option.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s2.h': 'How to number a PDF in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s2.p1': '<tool_add_page_numbers>Add Page Numbers</tool_add_page_numbers> works in your browser tab, so the file is read from your device. The steps:',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it is password-protected.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Choose where the numbers go, for example bottom centre or top right.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Choose the format, such as a plain number or a longer form with the total.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Choose which pages to number, and which number the first numbered page should show.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s2.o1.5': 'Add the numbers and download the PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s3.h': 'Choosing the position',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s3.p1': 'The usual places are the bottom centre, the bottom outer corner and the top right. Think about how the document will be used:',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s3.l1.1': 'Documents that are printed double-sided read best with numbers on the outer corners, so they are easy to find when flicking through.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s3.l1.2': 'Single-sided documents and screen reading work well with the bottom centre.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s3.l1.3': 'A header position is useful when the bottom of the page is crowded with footers or a signature block.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s3.p2': 'Look at a sample page before you decide. If a page already has a footer, a number placed over it will collide with the text. The numbers are always drawn on top of the page and never behind existing content, so they cannot hide themselves, but they can cover something.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s4.h': 'Number formats',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s4.p1': 'Formats are about how much information the number carries:',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s4.l1.1': 'A plain number: 7.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s4.l1.2': 'A number with the total: 7 of 42. This is useful for contracts, because a missing page is obvious.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s4.l1.3': 'A number with a label, such as Page 7.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s4.p2': 'Pick the one that matches the document’s purpose. A formal agreement benefits from “7 of 42”, a handout rarely needs more than the number.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s4.p3': 'The tool writes the numbers in Helvetica, which covers digits and Latin letters. That is enough for any numbering format, even in documents written in other alphabets.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s5.h': 'Skipping the cover page',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s5.p1': 'Many documents have a cover or title page that should not carry a number. Set “First page to number” to 2. You can also choose which number that page should show: if the cover is page 1 and the first numbered page should read 1 as well, start counting from 1 there.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s5.p2': 'The same idea works for a table of contents, a preface or appendices: number only the range you need, and leave the rest empty. If you need different numbering styles in different parts, number the parts separately and then combine them with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s6.h': 'Merged documents: number last',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s6.p1': 'When you combine files, the order of operations decides whether the numbering is continuous. Merge first and number second. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains how to set the order, and then <tool_add_page_numbers>Add Page Numbers</tool_add_page_numbers> counts through the whole result.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s6.p2': 'If you number first and merge afterwards, every part restarts at one. That can be what you want, for example for separate chapters, but it is rarely right for a single report.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s7.h': 'Rotated pages',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s7.p1': 'Numbers are placed relative to what you see on screen, including pages that are rotated, so a landscape page gets its number in the visible bottom corner. If a page is lying on its side because of a wrong rotation, fix it first with <tool_rotate_pdf>Rotate PDF</tool_rotate_pdf> and then add the numbers. Our guide to <post_how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently>rotating PDF pages permanently</post_how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently> explains how.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s8.h': 'What to check afterwards',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'Open the new file and scroll through every page, not only the first.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'Look for a number that sits on top of a footer, a table or a logo.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'Check that the first numbered page shows the number you expect.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'Check the last page, especially if you use the “of total” format.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s8.l1.5': 'Compare the file size with the original. Adding numbers should barely change it; if the file is large, <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> can help.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s9.h': 'Page numbers and other stamps',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s9.p1': 'Page numbers and watermarks are both drawn on top of the page. If you also need a CONFIDENTIAL stamp or a logo across the pages, use <tool_watermark_pdf>Watermark PDF</tool_watermark_pdf>, and number the pages in a separate step. Our guide to <post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf>adding a watermark to a PDF</post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf> covers opacity, angle and what a watermark cannot protect. Doing them separately lets you correct one without redoing the other.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s10.l1.1': 'Numbering before merging, so each part starts at one.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s10.l1.2': 'Placing the number where a footer already is.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s10.l1.3': 'Forgetting to skip the cover page.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s10.l1.4': 'Not looking at the last pages of the result.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s10.l1.5': 'Saving over the original instead of keeping an unnumbered copy.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s11.o1.1': 'Merge, rotate and extract first, so the page order is final.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s11.o1.2': 'Choose a position that does not collide with existing content.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s11.o1.3': 'Choose the format and the first page to number.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s11.o1.4': 'Download the result and scroll through every page.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.s11.o1.5': 'Keep the unnumbered original.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Can I start numbering from page 2?',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.1.a': 'Yes. Set the first page to number to 2, and choose which number it should show.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Can I add page numbers to a scanned PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.2.a': 'Yes. The numbers are drawn on top of each page, whether the page is text or a picture.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Will the numbers appear in the right place on rotated pages?',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.3.a': 'Yes. They are placed relative to what you see on screen, including rotated pages.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.4.q': 'Which font is used?',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.4.a': 'Helvetica, which covers digits and Latin letters.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.5.q': 'Can I number a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Not directly. Unlock it first, then add the numbers to the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.6.q': 'Can I remove the numbers later?',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.faq.6.a': 'Not with this tool. The numbers are drawn into the page, so keep the original file if you may need an unnumbered version.',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-add-page-numbers-to-a-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_add_page_numbers>Add Page Numbers</tool_add_page_numbers>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.title': 'How to Blur or Pixelate Part of an Image Safely',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.description': 'Hide faces, licence plates, ID numbers and private details in a photo or screenshot with blur, pixelation or a black box, and learn which one is safe.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.excerpt': 'Learn how to cover private details in pictures: when blur is enough, why a black box is safer, how to draw boxes accurately and what else to check before you share the image.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.imageAlt': 'A photo with a blurred rectangle hiding a licence plate on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.intro.1': 'Pictures and screenshots often show more than you mean to share. A street photo includes a licence plate. A screenshot of a chat shows a phone number. A picture of a parcel shows an address, and a photo of a document shows an ID number. Before you post, sell, report or send such a picture, the sensitive part has to be hidden.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.intro.2': 'This guide shows how to hide parts of a picture in your browser, explains the difference between blur, pixelation and a black box, and lists the other places where private information can hide.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.sum.1': 'Blur and pixelation can sometimes be partly reversed. For ID numbers, licence plates and other secrets, use the black box.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.sum.2': 'You draw the boxes yourself, because the tool does not detect faces automatically.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.sum.3': 'Cover a little more than the sensitive area, and zoom in to check the result.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.sum.4': 'Your original file is never changed. The hidden version is saved as a new picture.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.sum.5': 'Check the file name, the caption and the rest of the picture too, not only the part you covered.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.h': 'When you need to hide part of a picture',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.p1': 'The common cases are easy to recognise:',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.l1.1': 'Faces of people who did not agree to be shown, especially children.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.l1.2': 'Licence plates in photos of cars for sale or of street scenes.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.l1.3': 'Names, addresses and phone numbers in screenshots.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.l1.4': 'Account numbers, order numbers and barcodes on receipts and parcels.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.l1.5': 'Documents such as ID cards, tickets and letters.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.l1.6': 'Private messages and notifications that appear at the edge of a screenshot.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s1.p2': 'In all of these cases the best habit is to hide the detail before the picture leaves your device, and to keep the original in a private place.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s2.h': 'Blur, pixelate or black box',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s2.p1': '<tool_blur_image_area>Blur or Pixelate Image</tool_blur_image_area> offers three ways to hide an area.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s2.o1.1': 'Blur smooths the area so details cannot be read at a glance. It looks natural and is good for faces in the background.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s2.o1.2': 'Pixelate turns the area into large coloured squares. It is a familiar look, and the squares are bigger the stronger you set the effect.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s2.o1.3': 'Black box paints the area solid black. Nothing remains of the original pixels in that area.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s2.p2': 'The difference matters for safety. Blur and pixelation keep a rough trace of the original, and with effort and software they can sometimes be partly reversed, especially when the text was large and clear. For anything that must stay private, such as an ID number, a licence plate, an address or a password, choose the black box.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s3.h': 'How to hide an area',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s3.p1': 'The tool works in your browser tab, so the picture stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your picture. It accepts one image at a time, up to 25 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s3.o1.2': 'Drag on the picture to draw a box over what you want to hide, or press the button to add a box and then move and resize it with the X, Y, width and height fields.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s3.o1.3': 'Choose blur, pixelate or black box. For blur and pixelate you can also set the strength.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s3.o1.4': 'Apply the effect and download the result.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s3.p2': 'You can draw as many boxes as you need. Before you apply, you can remove a box or redraw it, and your original file is not touched at any point.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s4.h': 'Drawing accurate boxes',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s4.p1': 'Accuracy is what makes the difference between a safe picture and a leak.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s4.l1.1': 'Make the box a little larger than the detail. A box that only just fits can leave the edge of a letter or the tip of a nose visible.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s4.l1.2': 'For text, cover every character, including the first and the last.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s4.l1.3': 'For a face, cover the whole head including the hair and ears if the person could be recognised from them.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s4.l1.4': 'Zoom in on the finished picture and look at every edge.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s4.p2': 'If one box leaves a gap, add a second box that overlaps it. Overlapping boxes are fine.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s5.h': 'Check the whole picture, not only the part you covered',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s5.p1': 'Private information hides in unexpected places.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s5.l1.1': 'The picture can show a reflection in a window, a mirror or a pair of sunglasses.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s5.l1.2': 'A screenshot can show a name in a tab, a notification or a status bar.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s5.l1.3': 'A document photo can show text on the next page.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s5.l1.4': 'A parcel can have a barcode that holds the address even if the printed address is covered.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s5.p2': 'Then think about everything that travels with the picture: the file name, the caption you write and the place where you post it. A picture of a covered address with a file name that contains the street is still a leak. Our guide to <post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs>removing metadata from photos and PDFs</post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs> covers the hidden data inside the file. The tool saves a new picture and does not copy the original details such as the camera model or location into it.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s6.h': 'Cropping instead of hiding',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s6.p1': 'Sometimes the sensitive part is at the edge of the picture. In that case cropping is simpler and safer than hiding. <tool_image_cropper>Crop Image</tool_image_cropper> removes the unwanted part completely, so there is nothing left to recover. Our guide to <post_how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size>cropping an image to a specific size</post_how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size> explains how to crop precisely.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s6.p2': 'Hiding is the right choice when the sensitive part sits in the middle of an otherwise useful picture, and cropping is the right choice when it sits at the edge.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s7.h': 'Documents and PDFs',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s7.p1': 'If the sensitive content is in a PDF rather than a picture, a black rectangle drawn on top of the text is not enough, because the text stays underneath and can be selected. Use a tool that removes the text itself. Our guide to <post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly>redacting a PDF properly</post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly> explains how this works.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s8.h': 'Size, format and sharing',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s8.p1': 'After hiding the area, you may want to make the picture smaller before you send it. <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> reduces the file size, and <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer> makes the dimensions smaller. If the place you are posting accepts only one format, <tool_image_format_converter>Convert Image Format</tool_image_format_converter> changes it.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s8.p2': 'If the picture also needs a name or logo, our guide to <post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos>adding a watermark to photos</post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos> explains how, and <tool_photo_editor>Photo Editor</tool_photo_editor> handles colour fixes and text, as described in our guide to <post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser>editing a photo in your browser</post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser>.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s8.p3': 'Do these steps after hiding, not before, so that the final picture is the one you checked. Save the original, with everything visible, in a private place in case you need it later.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s9.h': 'Limits of the tool',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s9.p1': 'The tool is made for careful, manual work. It does not find faces automatically, because that needs a large AI model that is not included. It works on one picture at a time, and animated pictures use their first frame. If you have many pictures, take a moment to plan: a repeated detail such as a logo or a number can also be hidden one picture at a time.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s10.l1.1': 'Using blur or pixelation for a number or a name that must stay private.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s10.l1.2': 'Drawing a box that only just fits the detail.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s10.l1.3': 'Forgetting a second copy of the detail somewhere else in the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s10.l1.4': 'Leaving a revealing file name or caption.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s10.l1.5': 'Sharing the original instead of the hidden version.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s10.l1.6': 'Not zooming in to check the edges.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s11.o1.1': 'Find every sensitive detail in the picture, including reflections and edges.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s11.o1.2': 'Choose the black box for secrets, and blur for casual cases.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s11.o1.3': 'Draw boxes a little larger than the detail.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s11.o1.4': 'Apply, zoom in and check every edge.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.s11.o1.5': 'Check the file name and caption, then share the new picture and keep the original private.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.1.q': 'Is blurring safe for sensitive details?',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.1.a': 'For anything that must stay private, such as ID numbers or licence plates, use the black box. Blur and pixelation can sometimes be partly reversed.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.2.q': 'Does it find faces automatically?',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.2.a': 'No. You draw the boxes yourself. Automatic detection needs a large AI model that is not included.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.3.q': 'Can I change my mind?',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.3.a': 'Yes. Remove or redraw boxes before you apply. Your original file is never changed.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.4.q': 'Does the hidden picture keep my location data?',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.4.a': 'No. The tool saves a new picture, and details such as the camera model or location are not copied into it.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.5.q': 'What are the size limits?',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.faq.5.a': 'One image at a time, up to 25 MB. Animated images use the first frame.',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image.cta.p': 'Open <tool_blur_image_area>Blur or Pixelate Image</tool_blur_image_area>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.title': 'How to Compare Two PDF Files and See Exactly What Changed',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.description': 'Find the differences between two versions of a PDF with a text comparison and a visual view, and learn the limits for scans and inserted pages.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.excerpt': 'Compare an original and a revised PDF page by page, read the added and removed words, switch to a visual view of changed areas and avoid misleading results.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.imageAlt': 'Two PDF pages side by side with changed words highlighted in green and red on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.intro.1': 'A colleague sends back “the final version” of a contract, and you have a bad feeling that more than the agreed paragraph changed. A supplier updates a price list, and you need to know which lines are different. A thesis goes through a third round of edits and the supervisor wants only the changes. In each case you hold two PDFs and want a list of differences.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.intro.2': 'Reading both files side by side is slow and unreliable; the eye skips changes it does not expect. This guide shows how to compare two PDFs in your browser, how to read the result and where comparisons can mislead you.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.sum.1': 'Add the original and the revised PDF; the tool lists each page with the number of words added and removed.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.sum.2': 'Open a page to read the text changes, or use the visual view to see changed areas marked in red.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.sum.3': 'Pages are matched by number, so an inserted page makes all later pages look changed.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.sum.4': 'Scans have no selectable text; use the visual view or run OCR on both files first.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.sum.5': 'A comparison is a guide for review, not a legal proof. Read the important parts yourself.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s1.h': 'Two kinds of comparison',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s1.p1': 'There are two ways to compare documents, and they answer different questions.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s1.p2': 'A text comparison looks at the words. For each page it shows which words were added and which were removed, and collapses the unchanged text so you can concentrate on the differences. It is ideal for contracts, reports and anything where the wording matters. It ignores layout, so moving a paragraph to another place on the page is not flagged unless the words changed.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s1.p3': 'A visual comparison looks at the picture of each page and marks the areas that are different. It catches things that text comparison cannot: a changed image, a different logo, a moved table, a shifted signature line. It also works on scanned documents, because it does not need selectable text.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s1.p4': 'Use both when the stakes are high.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.h': 'How to compare two PDFs in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.p1': '<tool_compare_pdf>Compare PDF</tool_compare_pdf> works inside your browser tab, so both files are read from your device. The steps:',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add the original PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.o1.2': 'Add the revised PDF. Unlock either file first if it has a password.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.o1.3': 'Run the comparison. Each page appears in a list with the number of words added and removed.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.o1.4': 'Open a page to read the text changes, with additions in green and deletions in red.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.o1.5': 'Switch to the visual view to see changed areas marked in red.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.o1.6': 'Go through every page that shows a change.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s2.p2': 'Up to 100 pages per file are compared.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s3.h': 'Reading the text view',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s3.p1': 'Added words appear in green and removed words in red. The unchanged text is collapsed, with only a little context around each change, so a page with one edited sentence is easy to scan.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s3.p2': 'Things to keep in mind while reading:',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s3.l1.1': 'A reworded sentence shows as removed words followed by added words. Read both parts together.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s3.l1.2': 'Changed numbers are the most important and the easiest to miss in a long text. Look at them first.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s3.l1.3': 'A page with a very large number of changes usually means the layout shifted. Open it and compare with your eyes.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s4.h': 'Reading the visual view',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s4.p1': 'The visual view renders both pages at screen resolution and highlights the areas that differ. Use it for:',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s4.l1.1': 'Changed pictures, charts and logos.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s4.l1.2': 'Moved or resized elements.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s4.l1.3': 'Scanned documents.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s4.l1.4': 'Layout changes that do not alter the words, such as a different font size or spacing.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s4.p2': 'Tiny differences below screen resolution may not show up, so a change of a single dot or accent can slip through. For text-heavy documents, rely on the text view for wording and on the visual view for everything else.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s5.h': 'The inserted page problem',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s5.p1': 'Pages are compared by number. If someone inserts a page after page 3, page 4 of the revised file is page 3 of the original, and so on, so every following page appears changed. This is the most common reason for a comparison to look much worse than reality.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s5.p2': 'To avoid it, remove the inserted page first, or compare only the matching ranges. <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> lets you cut each document to the pages that correspond, and you can then compare the two shorter files. Our guide to <post_how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf>extracting pages from a PDF</post_how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf> explains how.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s6.h': 'Comparing scanned documents',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s6.p1': 'A scan is a picture. It has no text to compare, so the text view finds nothing. You have two options:',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s6.l1.1': 'Use the visual view. It works on pictures, but even a tiny shift in the scan makes many areas look different.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s6.l1.2': 'Run <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf> on both files first, so they have a text layer, and then compare the results. Recognition is English only and never perfect, so expect some false differences caused by misread characters. Our guide to <post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>making a scanned PDF searchable</post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable> explains what to expect.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s7.h': 'Other ways to look at differences',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s7.l1.1': 'If you only need the words of one file, <tool_extract_pdf_text>Extract Text from PDF</tool_extract_pdf_text> gives you plain text you can compare in another tool.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s7.l1.2': 'If you are checking a redacted copy against the original, our guide to <post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly>redacting a PDF properly</post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly> lists the checks to run.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s7.l1.3': 'If you want to read a long document comfortably before comparing, <tool_pdf_viewer>PDF Viewer</tool_pdf_viewer> lets you browse it in the browser.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s7.l1.4': 'If several documents will be joined, compare the final merged file with the previous one, not the separate parts. <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>Merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains the order of work.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s8.h': 'How to use the result responsibly',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s8.p1': 'A comparison tells you where to look. It does not tell you whether a change is acceptable, and it cannot replace reading. For contracts and anything legally binding:',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s8.l1.1': 'Read each flagged page in full.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s8.l1.2': 'Check numbers, dates, names and negations such as “not” and “except”.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s8.l1.3': 'Look at pages with no flagged change if a page was inserted or removed.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s8.l1.4': 'Ask the other party to confirm the list of changes in writing.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s9.l1.1': 'Comparing files in the wrong order, so additions look like deletions.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s9.l1.2': 'Ignoring the inserted page problem and concluding that everything changed.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s9.l1.3': 'Trusting a text comparison on a scanned document.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s9.l1.4': 'Skipping the visual view for pages with images or tables.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s9.l1.5': 'Treating the result as proof instead of a review aid.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s10.o1.1': 'Add the original first and the revised file second.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s10.o1.2': 'Look at the page list for pages with many changes.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s10.o1.3': 'Read the text changes, then the visual view.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s10.o1.4': 'Handle inserted or removed pages before drawing conclusions.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.s10.o1.5': 'For scans, use the visual view or run OCR on both files.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.1.q': 'What does the text comparison show?',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.1.a': 'For each page, the words that were added (green) and removed (red) between the original and the revised document, with the unchanged text collapsed. Pages are matched by number.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.2.q': 'What about scanned PDFs?',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.2.a': 'Scans have no selectable text, so the text comparison finds nothing. Use the visual comparison, or run OCR PDF on both files first.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.3.q': 'Why do all pages after page 3 show changes?',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.3.a': 'Most likely a page was inserted or removed. Pages are compared by number, so everything after the change appears shifted.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.4.q': 'Can it find tiny differences in a picture?',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.4.a': 'The visual comparison renders each page at screen resolution, so very small differences may not show.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.5.q': 'How many pages can be compared?',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.5.a': 'Up to 100 pages per file. Password-protected PDFs must be unlocked first.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.6.q': 'Are my files uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.faq.6.a': 'No. The comparison runs inside your browser, so both files stay on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-compare-two-pdf-files.cta.p': 'Open <tool_compare_pdf>Compare PDF</tool_compare_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.title': 'How to Convert Excel to PDF: Tables, Formulas and Limits',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.description': 'Convert an XLSX workbook to a PDF with every visible sheet as a table. Learn what is kept, why formulas need saved values and how to get a clean result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.excerpt': 'Turn an Excel workbook into a PDF you can send or print, understand what the conversion keeps and drops, and prepare the file so the numbers come out right.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.imageAlt': 'A spreadsheet grid turning into a PDF document on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.intro.1': 'A spreadsheet is a good place to work and a poor thing to send. Recipients may not have Excel, formulas can break on another computer, and anyone can change a number by accident. A PDF freezes the table as it is: the same on every device, easy to print and hard to alter by mistake.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to convert an Excel workbook to PDF in your browser, what the result looks like, why formulas sometimes come out empty and how to prepare a workbook so the PDF is clean.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.sum.1': 'Choose an XLSX file; every visible sheet becomes a table in the PDF, fitted to the page width.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.sum.2': 'The PDF shows the values saved in the file. Formulas are not recalculated.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.sum.3': 'Colours, merged cells, charts, pictures, print areas and page breaks are not kept.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.sum.4': 'Hidden sheets are skipped, and very large sheets are limited to 5,000 rows and 60 columns.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.sum.5': 'The text is written in Noto Sans, which covers Latin, Cyrillic, Greek, Vietnamese and Devanagari.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s1.h': 'What the conversion produces',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s1.p1': 'The tool reads your workbook and writes each visible sheet as a simple table that fits the page width. The result is real text in a PDF, so you can select, search and copy numbers from it. It is not a picture of the Excel window and it is not Excel’s print layout.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s1.p2': 'That distinction matters. If your workbook was carefully formatted for printing, with coloured headers, merged cells, a chart beside the table and manual page breaks, the PDF will look plainer. For many uses, such as sending a price list, an attendance table or a set of figures for review, a clean table is exactly what you want.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s2.h': 'How to convert a workbook',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s2.p1': '<tool_excel_to_pdf>Excel to PDF</tool_excel_to_pdf> works in your browser tab, so the workbook is read from your device. The steps:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and choose an XLSX file (up to 50 MB).',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Press Convert.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Read the notes under the result. They tell you what could not be converted, for example formulas without values or hidden sheets.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Check the tables and download the PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s2.p2': 'Old .xls files and password-protected workbooks are not supported. If you have an old file, open it in Excel and save it as XLSX first.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s3.h': 'Why formulas can come out empty',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s3.p1': 'A formula cell in an XLSX file stores two things: the formula and the last result Excel calculated. The converter does not run a spreadsheet engine; it shows the saved result. If a file was created by a program that wrote formulas without calculating them, the saved value is missing, and the cell is left empty. The tool counts those cells and tells you.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s3.p2': 'The fix is simple: open the workbook in Excel, let it calculate, save it and convert it again. Do the same when you changed numbers in a program that does not recalculate.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.h': 'What is not kept',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.p1': 'To set expectations, here is what you will not find in the PDF:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.l1.1': 'Cell colours and fonts. The table uses a uniform style.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.l1.2': 'Merged cells, which appear as ordinary cells.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.l1.3': 'Column widths set manually. Columns are fitted to the page.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.l1.4': 'Charts, pictures and shapes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.l1.5': 'Print areas and page breaks defined in Excel.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.l1.6': 'Hidden sheets, which are skipped.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s4.p2': 'If a chart is the point of your workbook, copy the chart as a picture in Excel and put it into a document instead, or export the sheet from Excel directly.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s5.h': 'Size limits',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s5.p1': 'Each sheet can have up to 5,000 rows and 60 columns. Anything beyond that is left out, and the tool says so. This keeps the conversion fast and the file a sensible size. For very large data sets, a PDF is not the right format anyway; consider splitting the data into several workbooks.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s6.h': 'Languages and fonts',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s6.p1': 'The PDF is written with Noto Sans. It covers Latin letters, including the special letters of Polish, Czech, Romanian and Turkish, as well as Cyrillic, Greek, Vietnamese and Devanagari. Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean text is not supported yet. Those characters appear as question marks, and the tool tells you how many were affected. The original fonts of the workbook are not kept.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s7.h': 'Preparing the workbook for a clean PDF',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s7.p1': 'A few minutes of preparation give a much better result:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s7.o1.1': 'Remove columns and rows that nobody needs, and unhide only the sheets you want to include. Hidden sheets are skipped.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s7.o1.2': 'Put the important table at the top left, with a header row.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s7.o1.3': 'Make sure formulas have been calculated and saved.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s7.o1.4': 'Keep column counts moderate so the table remains readable when fitted to the page.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s7.o1.5': 'Check for text in Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese or Korean, which the PDF cannot show yet.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s8.h': 'After the conversion',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'Open the PDF and check numbers against the workbook, especially totals and percentages.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'If you need page numbers on a long table, use <tool_add_page_numbers>Add Page Numbers</tool_add_page_numbers>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'If several workbooks should become one document, convert each one and then join them with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains the order of work.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'If the file is too large to email, <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> can reduce it, although tables are mostly text and shrink little.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s8.p1': 'Other Office files follow the same pattern: <tool_word_to_pdf>Word to PDF</tool_word_to_pdf> and <tool_powerpoint_to_pdf>PowerPoint to PDF</tool_powerpoint_to_pdf> convert documents and presentations, and our guides to <post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting>converting Word to PDF</post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting> and <post_how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf>converting PowerPoint to PDF</post_how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf> compare what each approach keeps. For long-term storage, see <post_what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it>what PDF/A is</post_what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s9.l1.1': 'Converting without checking for empty formula cells.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s9.l1.2': 'Expecting the Excel print layout, colours and charts to appear.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s9.l1.3': 'Leaving hidden sheets that you wanted included.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s9.l1.4': 'Sending a PDF without checking that the numbers match the workbook.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s9.l1.5': 'Throwing away the workbook. The PDF is a snapshot, not a replacement.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s10.o1.1': 'Calculate and save the workbook in Excel.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s10.o1.2': 'Unhide the sheets you need and tidy the columns.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s10.o1.3': 'Convert and read the notes shown under the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s10.o1.4': 'Check totals and numbers in the PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the workbook as the source of truth.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Are formulas calculated?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.1.a': 'No. The PDF shows the values Excel saved with the file. If a formula cell has no saved value, it is left empty and the tool tells you. Open the file in Excel, let it calculate, save it and convert it again.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Will it look like the print layout in Excel?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.2.a': 'No. Each visible sheet becomes a simple table fitted to the page width. Cell colours, merged cells, column widths, charts, pictures, print areas and page breaks are not kept.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Can I convert an old .xls file?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.3.a': 'No. Open it in Excel and save it as an XLSX file first.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.4.q': 'How large can the workbook be?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.4.a': 'XLSX files up to 50 MB, with up to 5,000 rows and 60 columns per sheet. Anything beyond that is left out and the tool says so.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.5.q': 'Which languages are supported?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Latin (including Polish, Czech, Romanian and Turkish letters), Cyrillic, Greek, Vietnamese and Devanagari. Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean are not supported yet.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.6.q': 'Is my workbook uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.faq.6.a': 'No. The conversion runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_excel_to_pdf>Excel to PDF</tool_excel_to_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.title': 'How to Convert HTML to PDF: Files, Pasted Code and Limits',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.description': 'Turn an HTML file or pasted HTML into a PDF with selectable text. Learn what is converted, why web page addresses are not accepted and how to save pages first.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.excerpt': 'Convert HTML documents, invoices and saved web pages into PDF in your browser, understand which tags and styles are supported and prepare your HTML for a clean result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.imageAlt': 'HTML code brackets turning into a PDF page on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.intro.1': 'HTML is the language of web pages, but it is also a convenient way to write documents: an invoice template, a report generated by a script, a newsletter saved from an email, a page of notes exported from a wiki. When such a document has to be sent, printed or archived, a PDF is the format everybody can open.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to convert HTML to PDF in your browser, which parts of HTML are supported, why the tool does not take web page addresses and how to prepare your HTML for a clean, readable result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.sum.1': 'Choose an HTML file or paste your HTML, pick a page size and convert. The PDF has real, selectable text.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.sum.2': 'Headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, links, bold and italic text and embedded pictures are converted.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.sum.3': 'CSS styles, scripts, web fonts, columns and positioned elements are not applied.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.sum.4': 'You cannot enter a web address. Save the page as an HTML file first and use that file, or paste its HTML.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.sum.5': 'The HTML is read as inert text: scripts do not run and nothing is loaded from the internet.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.h': 'What the converter reads',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.p1': 'The tool reads the structure of your document and lays it out as a simple, clean PDF. It understands:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.l1.1': 'Headings and paragraphs.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.l1.2': 'Ordered and unordered lists.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.l1.3': 'Tables.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.l1.4': 'Links, bold and italic text.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.l1.5': 'Pictures that are embedded in the HTML itself, using data addresses.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s1.p2': 'It does not try to reproduce a web design. A page that depends on CSS for its look, with columns, floating boxes and custom fonts, will come out as a plain document: the content in reading order, without the styling. This is a deliberate trade-off. It keeps the conversion predictable and safe.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s2.h': 'How to convert HTML in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s2.p1': '<tool_html_to_pdf>HTML to PDF</tool_html_to_pdf> works in your browser tab, so your file or pasted code is processed on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and choose an HTML file (up to 10 MB) or paste your HTML into the box.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Pick the page size.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Press Convert.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Read the notes under the result, which list anything that was skipped.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s2.o1.5': 'Download the PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s3.h': 'Why you cannot enter a web address',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s3.p1': 'It is natural to expect a box for a web address. The tool does not offer one, for two reasons. First, fetching other websites would mean sending the address to a server, which would break the promise that your work stays on your device. Second, a page rarely converts well without its styles and scripts, and the fetched result would disappoint.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s3.p2': 'The workaround is easy: in your browser, save the page as an HTML file, or view its source and copy it, and then use that file or paste the code. For pages with a lot of styling, expect a plainer result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s4.h': 'What is not converted',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s4.l1.1': 'Scripts, which do not run.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s4.l1.2': 'Forms, frames, canvas elements, video and SVG graphics.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s4.l1.3': 'Pictures and style sheets loaded from other websites. They are skipped, not fetched.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s4.l1.4': 'CSS layout: columns, positioning, backgrounds and web fonts.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s4.p1': 'The tool lists what it left out, so you know whether the PDF is complete enough.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s5.h': 'Is it safe to convert HTML you do not trust?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s5.p1': 'The HTML is read as inert text. Scripts are not executed and nothing is loaded from the internet, so a hostile page cannot run code or ping a tracking address through the conversion. Only pictures embedded in the HTML are used. That makes the tool a reasonable way to turn a saved email or a downloaded page into a document without opening it in a browser tab.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.h': 'Preparing HTML for a good PDF',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.p1': 'A few habits make the output better:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.o1.1': 'Use real headings, not bold text, for titles. Headings give the document a structure.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.o1.2': 'Use proper lists and tables instead of spaces and dashes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.o1.3': 'Embed important pictures as data addresses, or they will be skipped.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.o1.4': 'Keep the document in a single column.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.o1.5': 'Add a clear title and short paragraphs. Long unbroken blocks are hard to read on paper.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s6.o1.6': 'Save the file with UTF-8 encoding so that special characters come through.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s7.h': 'Languages and fonts',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s7.p1': 'The PDF is written with Noto Sans, which covers Latin letters, including Polish, Czech, Romanian and Turkish characters, as well as Cyrillic, Greek, Vietnamese and Devanagari. Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean text is not supported yet. Those characters appear as question marks, and the tool tells you how many were affected. The original fonts of the document are not kept.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s8.h': 'After the conversion',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'Open the PDF and read it from start to finish. Check tables and lists.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'If the document is long, add numbers with <tool_add_page_numbers>Add Page Numbers</tool_add_page_numbers>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'If several HTML documents belong together, convert each one and join them with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains the order of work.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'If you need the text again as lightweight markup, <tool_pdf_to_markdown>PDF to Markdown</tool_pdf_to_markdown> converts it back to Markdown; our guide to <post_how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown>converting PDF to Markdown</post_how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown> explains the limits.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s8.l1.5': 'For documents that start in a word processor, <tool_word_to_pdf>Word to PDF</tool_word_to_pdf> is the better starting point; see <post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting>converting Word to PDF</post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s8.l1.6': 'If the PDF is too large for email, <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> can reduce it.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s9.l1.1': 'Expecting the PDF to look like the web page in a browser.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s9.l1.2': 'Referencing pictures by web address, which are skipped.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s9.l1.3': 'Pasting only a fragment without the surrounding structure and wondering about the layout.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s9.l1.4': 'Not checking special characters in languages the font does not cover.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s9.l1.5': 'Not keeping the HTML source. The PDF cannot be turned back into the original page.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s10.o1.1': 'Save the page as an HTML file, or copy its HTML.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s10.o1.2': 'Make sure pictures are embedded and the text is real text.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s10.o1.3': 'Choose a page size and convert.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s10.o1.4': 'Read the notes about skipped items and check the PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the HTML source.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Can I enter a web page address?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.1.a': 'No. Fetching other websites would require sending the address to a server, and a page rarely converts well without its styles. Save the page as an HTML file in your browser and use that file, or paste its HTML.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Will the PDF look like the web page?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.2.a': 'No, not exactly. The tool reads headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, links, bold and italic text and embedded pictures and lays them out as a simple document. CSS styles, scripts, web fonts, columns and positioned elements are not applied.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Is it safe to convert HTML that I do not trust?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.3.a': 'Yes. The HTML is read as inert text: scripts do not run and nothing is loaded from the internet.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.4.q': 'What is the size limit?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.4.a': 'Up to 10 MB of HTML.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.5.q': 'Which languages are supported in the PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Latin, Cyrillic, Greek, Vietnamese and Devanagari. Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean are not supported yet.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.6.q': 'Is my HTML uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.faq.6.a': 'No. The conversion runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-convert-html-to-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_html_to_pdf>HTML to PDF</tool_html_to_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.title': 'How to Convert PDF to JPG: Resolution, Pages and Quality',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.description': 'Turn PDF pages into JPG images in your browser. Learn which DPI to pick, how to convert only some pages and when PNG is the better choice.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.excerpt': 'A practical guide to turning PDF pages into JPG pictures: choosing the resolution, converting selected pages, keeping file sizes sensible and avoiding common mistakes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.imageAlt': 'A PDF page turning into a JPG picture on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.intro.1': 'Sometimes a PDF is the wrong container for what you need. A slide for a social post, a page of a brochure for a website, a scanned receipt for an expense form that only accepts pictures: in all of these, the page has to become an image. JPG is the most widely accepted picture format, which makes it the usual target.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.intro.2': 'This guide explains how a PDF page becomes a JPG, which resolution to choose, how to convert only the pages you need, and what to do when the result is too large or too soft. Everything here can be done in your browser, without installing software.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.sum.1': 'Each PDF page becomes one JPG. A 10-page document gives ten pictures.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.sum.2': '150 DPI suits screens and sharing; 300 DPI suits printing. Higher values mean larger files.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.sum.3': 'You can convert a few pages instead of the whole document, which saves time and space.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.sum.4': 'JPG is lossy and has no transparency. Use PNG for sharp diagrams or pages with fine line art.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.sum.5': 'Password-protected PDFs must be unlocked before they can be converted.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s1.h': 'What happens when a PDF becomes a JPG',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s1.p1': 'A PDF page is a set of instructions: draw this text in this font, place this picture here, fill this rectangle with that colour. Converting it to JPG means a program follows those instructions and paints the result onto a grid of pixels, then saves the grid as a picture. The text, which was real selectable text in the PDF, becomes part of the picture and can no longer be selected or searched.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s1.p2': 'That one-way step is the most important thing to know. Keep your original PDF. The JPG is a snapshot, and you cannot turn it back into editable text without running a recognition tool such as <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf>, which is never perfect.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s2.h': 'Choosing the resolution',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s2.p1': 'Resolution is measured in DPI, dots per inch. A standard page is about 8.3 by 11.7 inches (A4), so the DPI decides how many pixels the picture has: at 150 DPI an A4 page is roughly 1,240 by 1,750 pixels, and at 300 DPI roughly 2,480 by 3,500.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s2.l1.1': '150 DPI is a good default for screens, email, chat and web pages. Text is readable and the files stay modest.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s2.l1.2': '300 DPI is the usual choice for printing, or for pages with small print that people will zoom into.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s2.l1.3': 'Lower settings make small files but soften text. They are fine for thumbnails and previews.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s2.p2': 'A higher setting is not automatically better. It takes longer, needs more memory in your browser and produces files several times larger without looking different on a phone screen. The tool caps each page at about 50 megapixels, so extremely large pages such as posters or maps may be refused at the highest resolution.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.h': 'How to convert a PDF to JPG step by step',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.p1': '<tool_pdf_to_jpg>PDF to JPG</tool_pdf_to_jpg> runs inside your browser tab, so the PDF is read from your device and the pictures are created there. The steps are short:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB).',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.o1.2': 'Choose the resolution.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.o1.3': 'Optionally enter the pages you want, for example 1-3, 5. Leave the field empty to convert every page.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.o1.4': 'Press Convert.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.o1.5': 'Download the images one by one, or all together as a ZIP file.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s3.p2': 'The pictures are named after the document and the page number, with leading zeros, so they sort correctly in a folder: report-page-01.jpg, report-page-02.jpg and so on.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s4.h': 'Converting only some pages',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s4.p1': 'Converting a 200-page report when you need three pages wastes time and creates files you have to delete. Use the page field instead. Ranges and single pages can be combined: 1-3, 8, 12-14 converts pages 1, 2, 3, 8, 12, 13 and 14.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s4.p2': 'If you only need a few pages as a new PDF rather than as pictures, <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> is the better tool, and the result stays selectable text. Our guide to <post_how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf>extracting pages from a PDF</post_how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf> explains the details.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s5.h': 'JPG or PNG?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s5.p1': '<tool_pdf_to_png>PDF to PNG</tool_pdf_to_png> works the same way but writes lossless PNG files. The choice depends on what is on the page:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s5.l1.1': 'Photographs, scans and pages with large images: JPG. The files are much smaller and the loss is invisible at sensible settings.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s5.l1.2': 'Pages with sharp text on flat colours, diagrams, charts and screenshots: PNG. JPG can leave faint blotches around hard edges.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s5.l1.3': 'Anything that must not lose detail on the next edit: PNG.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s5.p2': 'Our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> explains the differences in more depth.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s6.h': 'Keeping the files small',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s6.p1': 'If the pictures are too large for an upload limit, try these in order:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s6.o1.1': 'Lower the resolution, for example from 300 to 150 DPI.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s6.o1.2': 'Convert only the pages you need.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s6.o1.3': 'Run the images through <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor>, which also lets you limit the width or height.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s6.p2': 'For email, a width of about 1,200 to 1,600 pixels is plenty. Our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> covers the routine in detail.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s7.h': 'Password-protected and damaged PDFs',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s7.p1': 'Encrypted PDFs are detected and refused with a clear message, because the pages cannot be read without the password. If the file belongs to you, remove the password first with <tool_unlock_pdf>Unlock PDF</tool_unlock_pdf> and then convert the unlocked copy. Our guide to <post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf>protecting and unlocking a PDF</post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf> explains what unlocking does and does not do.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s7.p2': 'A PDF that will not open at all may be damaged. <post_repair-a-corrupted-pdf>Repairing a corrupted PDF</post_repair-a-corrupted-pdf> describes how to recover what can be recovered.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s8.h': 'How accurate is the rendering?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s8.p1': 'Pages are drawn with Mozilla’s PDF.js, the same engine used by Firefox, which handles the large majority of PDFs well. Unusual fonts, exotic blend modes or very advanced graphics can look slightly different from Adobe’s viewer. Always look at the first picture before you convert a long document, and zoom in on small text.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s9.l1.1': 'Choosing a very high resolution for something that will only be seen on a phone.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s9.l1.2': 'Forgetting that the text is no longer selectable and deleting the original PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s9.l1.3': 'Using JPG for a page of thin lines and then wondering about the fuzzy edges. Use PNG there.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s9.l1.4': 'Converting a whole document when only a few pages are needed.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s9.l1.5': 'Sharing pictures of a confidential document without checking every page first.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s10.o1.1': 'Decide where the pictures will be used: screen, print or upload form.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s10.o1.2': 'Pick 150 DPI for screens and 300 DPI for print.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s10.o1.3': 'Enter the pages you need, or leave the field empty for all of them.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s10.o1.4': 'Look at the first picture at full size before downloading the rest.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the original PDF safe.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.1.q': 'Does converting a PDF to JPG reduce its quality?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.1.a': 'The picture is a copy of the page at the resolution you chose, saved with JPG compression. At 150 or 300 DPI the difference from the PDF is rarely visible, but the text can no longer be selected.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.2.q': 'How many pages can I convert at once?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.2.a': 'You can convert every page of a PDF up to 100 MB in one go, and download the pictures as a ZIP. Very long or complex documents depend on your device’s memory, so convert in smaller page ranges if the browser slows down.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.3.q': 'Can I convert a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.3.a': 'Not directly. Remove the password first with Unlock PDF, then convert the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.4.q': 'Which resolution should I choose for printing?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.4.a': '300 DPI is the usual choice for printing. Use 150 DPI when the pictures will only be viewed on a screen.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.5.q': 'Is my PDF uploaded to a server?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.5.a': 'No. The conversion runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.6.q': 'Can I turn the JPG images back into a PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.faq.6.a': 'Yes. JPG to PDF puts the pictures onto pages again, but the result is a set of pictures, not editable text.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg.cta.p': 'Open <tool_pdf_to_jpg>PDF to JPG</tool_pdf_to_jpg>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.title': 'How to Convert PDF to Markdown: Headings, Lists and Limits',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.description': 'Turn the text of a PDF into a Markdown file with headings, paragraphs and lists. Learn how structure is guessed, what is lost and how to clean the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.excerpt': 'Extract a PDF’s text as Markdown for notes, wikis and documentation, understand how headings and lists are detected and fix the usual problems with tables and scans.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.imageAlt': 'A PDF page turning into lines of Markdown text on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.intro.1': 'Markdown is the plain-text format of notes, documentation, wikis and static websites. It is easy to read, easy to edit and easy to move between tools. PDFs, on the other hand, are made for appearance. When you have a PDF article or manual and want to reuse its text in Markdown, copy and paste produces a mess of broken lines and lost structure.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.intro.2': 'A converter can do better by looking at font sizes and spacing to guess the headings, lists and paragraphs. This guide explains how to turn a PDF into Markdown in your browser, how the structure is estimated, what is not carried over and how to clean up the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.sum.1': 'The tool converts the real text of a PDF into Markdown with headings, paragraphs and lists.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.sum.2': 'Headings are lines in a larger font, lists are lines starting with a bullet or number, and paragraphs follow the spacing.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.sum.3': 'The structure is a good guess, not a guarantee. Always check the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.sum.4': 'Tables, columns, images, links, bold and italic text, footnotes and equations are not carried over.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.sum.5': 'Scanned PDFs have no text; run OCR first.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s1.h': 'What PDF to Markdown does',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s1.p1': 'A PDF does not store structure. It stores positioned pieces of text: this word at this place in this size. A converter therefore reconstructs structure from clues:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s1.l1.1': 'Lines set in a larger font than the body text are probably headings.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s1.l1.2': 'Lines that start with a bullet or a number are probably list items.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s1.l1.3': 'Gaps between lines separate paragraphs.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s1.l1.4': 'Text that repeats at the top or bottom of every page is probably a header or footer.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s1.p2': 'The result is a Markdown file in which headings start with hash signs, lists have dashes or numbers and paragraphs are separated by blank lines. This is a very good starting point for editing, but it is an estimate.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.h': 'How to convert a PDF to Markdown',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.p1': '<tool_pdf_to_markdown>PDF to Markdown</tool_pdf_to_markdown> works in your browser tab, so the PDF is read from your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add a PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it is password-protected.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.o1.2': 'Decide whether to remove repeating page headers, footers and page numbers. This is usually a good idea for books and reports.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.o1.3': 'Optionally mark page breaks with a line, which helps when you want to see where pages ended.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.o1.4': 'Press Convert.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.o1.5': 'Check the Markdown shown in the box, then copy it or download the .md file.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s2.p2': 'The summary under the box tells you how many pages had text, how many headings and how many list items were found. If a long document reports zero headings, the PDF probably uses one font size throughout.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s3.h': 'Removing headers, footers and page numbers',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s3.p1': 'Repeated text such as a running title, a company name or a page number is a nuisance in Markdown, because it ends up in the middle of sentences when a paragraph crosses a page. The option to remove repeating elements detects lines that appear on many pages in the same place and drops them. Use it for books, reports and articles. Turn it off if the repeating text is meaningful, for example if every page begins with a different value in the same position that you want to keep.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.h': 'What is not carried over',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.p1': 'Be realistic about the limits. These elements do not come through:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.l1.1': 'Tables and multi-column layouts are not rebuilt. Their text appears as ordinary lines, in reading order where possible.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.l1.2': 'Images and figures.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.l1.3': 'Links.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.l1.4': 'Bold and italic text.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.l1.5': 'Footnotes and equations.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s4.p2': 'For documents where tables matter, retype the tables by hand or copy them from the PDF with another approach, and check every number against the original.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s5.h': 'Scanned PDFs give empty results',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s5.p1': 'A scanned page is a picture. There is no text to extract, so the result is empty. First run <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf> to add a text layer, then convert the result. Recognition is English only and not perfect, so proofread the output. Our guide to <post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>making a scanned PDF searchable</post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable> explains the process and what to expect.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s6.h': 'When plain text is enough',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s6.p1': 'If you do not need headings or lists, <tool_extract_pdf_text>Extract Text from PDF</tool_extract_pdf_text> gives you the text without any Markdown syntax. It can be the better choice for searching, counting words or feeding text to another program.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.h': 'Cleaning up the Markdown',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.p1': 'After conversion, spend a few minutes on these fixes:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.o1.1': 'Check the headings. A large line that is not really a heading, for example a pull quote, may be marked as one. Remove or demote it.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.o1.2': 'Join broken lines. Where the PDF wrapped a sentence mid-paragraph, make sure it reads as one paragraph.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.o1.3': 'Repair lists. Numbered lists may restart at one, or a long bullet may be split.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.o1.4': 'Retype tables. Turn the loose lines into a Markdown table by hand if you need one.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.o1.5': 'Add links and emphasis again where they matter.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s7.o1.6': 'Preview the result. <tool_markdown_previewer>Markdown Previewer</tool_markdown_previewer> shows how it will look, which makes mistakes easy to spot.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s8.h': 'Going the other way',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s8.p1': 'If you need a PDF from HTML, <tool_html_to_pdf>HTML to PDF</tool_html_to_pdf> turns an HTML file or pasted HTML into a PDF, as our guide to <post_how-to-convert-html-to-pdf>converting HTML to PDF</post_how-to-convert-html-to-pdf> explains. For documents that start in a word processor, see <post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting>converting Word to PDF</post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s9.l1.1': 'Trusting the headings without looking at them.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s9.l1.2': 'Expecting tables to survive as tables.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s9.l1.3': 'Converting a scan without running OCR first.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s9.l1.4': 'Leaving page numbers and running headers in the text.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s9.l1.5': 'Forgetting that the structure is estimated from font sizes and spacing, so complex layouts can go wrong.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s10.o1.1': 'Convert with the repeating-element option on for books and reports.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s10.o1.2': 'Check the summary of headings and list items.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s10.o1.3': 'Read the Markdown from top to bottom and fix headings, lists and broken lines.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s10.o1.4': 'Retype tables.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the PDF as the reference.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.1.q': 'How are headings and lists found?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.1.a': 'Headings are lines set in a larger font than the body text, lists are lines that start with a bullet or a number, and paragraphs are separated by the spacing between lines. This is a good guess, not a guarantee, so check the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.2.q': 'Why is the result empty for my scanned PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.2.a': 'A scan is a picture of a page and has no text to extract. Run OCR PDF first to add a text layer, then convert the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.3.q': 'What about tables, columns and images?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.3.a': 'Tables and columns are not rebuilt: their text appears as ordinary lines, in reading order where possible. Images, links, bold and italic text, footnotes and equations are not carried over.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.4.q': 'Can I convert a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.4.a': 'Not directly. Unlock it first, then convert the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.5.q': 'How large can the PDF be?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.5.a': 'Up to 100 MB. Only text that is real text in the PDF is converted.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.6.q': 'Is my PDF uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.faq.6.a': 'No. The conversion runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-convert-pdf-to-markdown.cta.p': 'Open <tool_pdf_to_markdown>PDF to Markdown</tool_pdf_to_markdown>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.title': 'Convert PowerPoint to PDF: What Is Kept and What Is Not',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.description': 'Turn a PPTX presentation into a PDF with one page per slide. Learn how text, pictures and shapes convert, what is left out and how to check the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.excerpt': 'Convert a PowerPoint deck to PDF in your browser, understand which elements are drawn and which are skipped, and prepare slides so the PDF looks right.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.imageAlt': 'A presentation slide turning into a PDF page on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.intro.1': 'A presentation is made to be shown, but it is often shared as a file: a handout for a meeting, slides for people who missed a talk, a deck attached to an application. Sending a PPTX has risks. Fonts may be missing on the recipient’s computer, slides may rearrange themselves and anyone can edit them. A PDF looks the same everywhere and opens on any device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to convert a PowerPoint presentation to PDF in your browser, what the converter draws and what it leaves out, and how to prepare a deck so that the PDF comes out well.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.sum.1': 'Choose a PPTX file; every slide becomes one page with the slide’s own size and orientation.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.sum.2': 'Text boxes, pictures, simple shapes and tables are drawn. Fonts and effects differ from PowerPoint.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.sum.3': 'Charts, SmartArt, video, audio, animations, transitions and speaker notes are not converted, and the tool tells you how many items were left out.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.sum.4': 'Text that does not fit its box is shrunk, down to half of its size.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.sum.5': 'Always look through the PDF before you share it.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s1.h': 'What the converter does',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s1.p1': 'The tool reads the structure of the PPTX file, which is a package of XML and picture files, and draws each slide as a PDF page from the elements it understands: text boxes, pictures, simple shapes and tables. It does not run PowerPoint’s rendering engine, so the result is a faithful but simplified version of each slide.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s1.p2': 'The PDF contains real text, so you can select and search it, and the pages have the same size and orientation as your slides. A widescreen deck gives widescreen pages.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s2.h': 'How to convert a presentation',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s2.p1': '<tool_powerpoint_to_pdf>PowerPoint to PDF</tool_powerpoint_to_pdf> works inside your browser tab, so the file is read from your device. The steps:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and choose a PPTX file (up to 100 MB and 300 slides).',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Press Convert.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Read the notes under the result. They list what could not be converted.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Check the pages and download the PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s2.p2': 'Old .ppt files and password-protected presentations are not supported. Open the old file in PowerPoint and save it as PPTX, and remove the password before converting.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.h': 'What is drawn and what is not',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.p1': 'Drawn: text, pictures in JPEG and PNG format, simple shapes and tables.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.p2': 'Not converted:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.l1.1': 'Charts and SmartArt graphics.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.l1.2': 'Video and audio.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.l1.3': 'Animations and transitions.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.l1.4': 'Speaker notes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.l1.5': 'Gradients, lines and connectors.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.l1.6': 'Pictures in formats other than JPEG and PNG.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s3.p3': 'The tool counts the items it skipped and shows the number. If your deck relies on charts or diagrams, check those slides particularly carefully. A workaround for important charts is to copy them in PowerPoint as pictures, paste them back as images and convert again.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s4.h': 'Fonts and text fitting',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s4.p1': 'The PDF is written with Noto Sans, so the original fonts of the deck are not kept. Noto Sans covers Latin letters, including the special letters of Polish, Czech, Romanian and Turkish, as well as Cyrillic, Greek, Vietnamese and Devanagari. Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean text is not supported yet: those characters appear as question marks, and the tool tells you how many were affected.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s4.p2': 'Because the font changes, text may take a different amount of space. When text does not fit its box, it is shrunk to fit, down to half of its original size. If a slide looks crowded in the PDF, shorten the text in PowerPoint and convert again.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.h': 'Preparing a deck for a good result',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.p1': 'A little preparation makes the conversion more predictable:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.o1.1': 'Save the file as PPTX and remove any password.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.o1.2': 'Replace charts and SmartArt with pictures if they are important.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.o1.3': 'Use JPEG or PNG pictures. Other formats are skipped.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.o1.4': 'Avoid very small text, which gets smaller when it has to be fitted.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.o1.5': 'Do not rely on animations to reveal content step by step. In a PDF every element is visible at once.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s5.o1.6': 'Put essential information into slide text instead of speaker notes, which are not carried over.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s6.h': 'Reviewing the PDF',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s6.p1': 'Always flip through the whole PDF. Look at:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s6.l1.1': 'Title slides and closing slides, where large text and logos are common.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s6.l1.2': 'Slides with tables, which can wrap differently.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s6.l1.3': 'Slides with pictures, to check that none are missing because of an unsupported format.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s6.l1.4': 'Slides that relied on an effect, such as a shadow or gradient for contrast. Without it, white text on a pale background may be hard to read.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s6.p2': 'If you only need some slides, <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> lets you keep the pages you want.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s7.h': 'Handouts and sharing',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s7.p1': 'A PDF is ideal as a handout. A few ways to make it better:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s7.l1.1': 'Combine several decks into one file with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains the details.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s7.l1.2': 'If the file is too large to email, <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> recompresses the pictures. Our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains what to expect.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s7.l1.3': 'For written reports, <tool_word_to_pdf>Word to PDF</tool_word_to_pdf> and <tool_excel_to_pdf>Excel to PDF</tool_excel_to_pdf> follow the same approach; see <post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting>converting Word to PDF</post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting> and <post_how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf>converting Excel to PDF</post_how-to-convert-excel-to-pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s7.l1.4': 'If the deck must be archived for years, <post_what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it>PDF/A</post_what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it> explains the archival format.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s8.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'Sharing the PDF without looking at every slide.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'Expecting charts, SmartArt, videos and animations to come through.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'Using picture formats other than JPEG and PNG.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'Letting text run so long that it has to be shrunk to half size.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s8.l1.5': 'Deleting the PPTX after converting. Keep it as the editable source.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s9.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s9.o1.1': 'Save as PPTX, remove the password and convert important charts into pictures.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s9.o1.2': 'Convert and read the notes about skipped items.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s9.o1.3': 'Flip through every page of the PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s9.o1.4': 'Fix problem slides in PowerPoint and convert again.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.s9.o1.5': 'Keep the PPTX file.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Will the slides look exactly like in PowerPoint?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.1.a': 'Not exactly. Kitwell draws each slide from its text boxes, pictures, simple shapes and tables. Fonts, gradients, shadows, effects and exact text fitting differ. Always look through the PDF before you use it.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.2.q': 'What is not converted?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.2.a': 'Charts, SmartArt, video and audio, animations and transitions, speaker notes, gradients, lines and connectors, and pictures in formats other than JPEG and PNG. The tool tells you how many items were left out.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Can I convert an old .ppt file?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.3.a': 'No. Open it in PowerPoint and save it as a PPTX file first.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.4.q': 'How large can the presentation be?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.4.a': 'PPTX files up to 100 MB and 300 slides.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.5.q': 'Which languages are supported?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Latin, Cyrillic, Greek, Vietnamese and Devanagari text. Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean are not supported yet.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.6.q': 'Is my presentation uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.faq.6.a': 'No. The conversion runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-convert-powerpoint-to-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_powerpoint_to_pdf>PowerPoint to PDF</tool_powerpoint_to_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.title': 'How to Convert SVG to PNG, JPG or WebP at Any Size',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.description': 'Turn SVG logos and icons into sharp PNG, JPG or WebP images at the exact size you need, and learn why an SVG can look different once it is exported.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.excerpt': 'A practical guide to exporting SVG files as PNG, JPG or WebP: how scale and width work, how to keep transparency, why fonts go missing and which size to choose for each use.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.imageAlt': 'A vector shape being turned into a picture file on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.intro.1': 'SVG is the format of logos, icons, charts and illustrations. It describes shapes with numbers instead of storing pixels, so it stays sharp at any size and the files are tiny. The trouble starts when you have to hand an SVG to a place that only accepts pictures: a social network, an email program, a slide deck, a print shop form or a marketplace listing.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to turn an SVG into a PNG, JPG or WebP in your browser, how to choose the size, how to keep a transparent background and what to do when the exported picture does not match the original.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.sum.1': 'An SVG is drawn as a picture at the size you choose, so a large export is as crisp as a small one.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.sum.2': 'Choose PNG when you need transparency, JPG for the smallest file with a solid background and WebP for web pages.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.sum.3': 'Fonts, images and styles that are linked from outside the SVG are not loaded, so embed them or turn text into shapes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.sum.4': 'Check the final size in pixels and compress the result if it will go on a web page.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.sum.5': 'Scripts inside an SVG do not run during conversion.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s1.h': 'What an SVG is, and why you sometimes need a picture',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s1.p1': 'An SVG file is text. It lists shapes, curves, colours and text, and the program that opens it draws them. Because nothing is stored as pixels, you can enlarge the drawing to the size of a billboard and the edges stay sharp.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s1.p2': 'Pictures such as PNG, JPG and WebP store a fixed grid of pixels. They are accepted almost everywhere, which is why a conversion is often needed. Many upload forms do not accept SVG at all, email programs may block it and some design programs import it unevenly. Turning the SVG into a picture at the right size solves all of that, and our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> explains how the picture formats differ.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s2.h': 'Which output format to choose',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s2.p1': '<tool_svg_converter>SVG to PNG / JPG</tool_svg_converter> can export three formats.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s2.l1.1': 'PNG keeps transparency and has no loss of detail. It is the right choice for logos and icons that will sit on different backgrounds.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s2.l1.2': 'JPG makes small files but cannot store transparency, so transparent areas become a solid background. Use it for illustrations with a background and for places that only accept JPG.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s2.l1.3': 'WebP gives small files for web pages and also supports transparency, if the browser can create it.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s2.p2': 'If you are not sure, export a PNG first. You can convert it later with <tool_image_format_converter>Convert Image Format</tool_image_format_converter> or <tool_png_to_webp>PNG to WebP</tool_png_to_webp>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s3.h': 'How to convert an SVG',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s3.p1': 'The conversion runs inside your browser tab, so the files stay on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add one or more SVG files. You can add up to 20 files at once, each up to 25 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s3.o1.2': 'Choose PNG, JPG or WebP.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s3.o1.3': 'Choose the output size, either as a scale or as an exact width.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s3.o1.4': 'Convert and download the results one by one or together.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s3.p2': 'Before you start, think about where the picture will be used, because that decides the size. The next section explains how to pick it.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.h': 'Choosing the size',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.p1': 'Because the SVG is redrawn at the size you choose, you can ask for any size without losing sharpness. Choose by use:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.l1.1': 'For a web page, export at the size it is shown, and at twice that size if the page serves sharp pictures to high-density screens.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.l1.2': 'For a social profile picture or a marketplace listing, check the size the platform asks for and export exactly that width.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.l1.3': 'For a presentation, 1920 pixels wide is enough for a full slide.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.l1.4': 'For print, work out the size in pixels from the printed size and the print resolution, and export at that width.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.p2': 'The converter limits the output to 16,000 pixels per side, which is plenty for almost all uses. A scale such as 2x or 4x is the quickest way to get a larger version of the same drawing, and it is as crisp as the 1x version.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s4.p3': 'If an SVG has no width and height written in it, the tool uses its viewBox, and if neither exists it falls back to 300 by 150 pixels. When a result comes out in an odd size, set the width yourself.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s5.h': 'Keeping a transparent background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s5.p1': 'PNG and WebP can hold transparent areas, so a logo exported from SVG keeps its clear background and sits cleanly on a coloured page. JPG cannot, so a transparent area becomes a solid colour.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s5.p2': 'Always check a transparent export on the background where it will be used. White edges around a logo on a dark page are a sign that the picture was saved with a solid background somewhere along the way.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s6.h': 'Why the picture can look different from the SVG',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s6.p1': 'Browsers do not support every SVG feature, and the converter draws the SVG the same way a browser does. The most common surprises are:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s6.l1.1': 'Missing fonts. If the SVG uses a font that is installed on the designer\'s computer but is not embedded in the file, the browser replaces it with a default font.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s6.l1.2': 'Linked pictures. An SVG can point to a picture on the web or on a disk. Linked pictures and styles are not loaded during conversion, so they are missing in the result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s6.l1.3': 'Filters and effects that a particular program adds, which not every browser draws in the same way.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s6.p2': 'The fix is to make the SVG self-contained before you convert it. In your design program, convert text to outlines or paths, embed any pictures inside the file and save again. After that, the picture looks the same everywhere.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s7.h': 'Safety of SVG files',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s7.p1': 'An SVG can contain scripts, which is why websites are careful about uploads. During conversion the SVG is drawn as a picture, so scripts inside it do not run. You can still open files you did not make, but keep the usual caution about where files come from.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s8.h': 'After the conversion: size and quality',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s8.p1': 'A large PNG can be heavy. If the picture will go on a web page, resize it to the displayed size and compress it. <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> reduces the file size, and our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> explains how to hit a target size.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s8.p2': 'If you need a different pixel size later, <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer> makes the picture smaller. Making a picture bigger from pixels is different from exporting the SVG at a bigger size: <tool_enlarge_image>Enlarge Image</tool_enlarge_image> can enlarge pictures smoothly but cannot add missing detail, so always go back to the SVG for a larger version when you can.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s8.p3': 'Watermarks and logos are a common next step. Our guide to <post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos>adding a watermark to photos</post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos> shows how to stamp an exported logo onto many pictures at once.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s9.l1.1': 'Exporting at a small size and then enlarging the picture. Export at the size you need instead.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s9.l1.2': 'Choosing JPG for a logo that needs a transparent background.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s9.l1.3': 'Forgetting that fonts must be embedded or converted to shapes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s9.l1.4': 'Using a very large export on a web page, which slows the page down.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s9.l1.5': 'Not checking the result on the background where it will be used.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s10.o1.1': 'Make the SVG self-contained: embed pictures and convert text to shapes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s10.o1.2': 'Choose PNG for transparency, JPG for a solid background, WebP for the web.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s10.o1.3': 'Set the width you need, or a scale such as 2x.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s10.o1.4': 'Convert, then check the result on its final background.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.s10.o1.5': 'Compress the picture if it will be used on a web page.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.1.q': 'Will the image stay sharp at large sizes?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.1.a': 'Yes. The SVG is drawn at the size you choose, so a 4x export is as crisp as a 1x one.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.2.q': 'Why does my SVG look different after conversion?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.2.a': 'Browsers do not support every SVG feature, and SVGs that rely on external fonts or images fall back to defaults. Embed fonts and images inside the SVG for the best result.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.3.q': 'Is it safe to open SVG files here?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.3.a': 'Yes. The SVG is drawn as a picture, so scripts inside it do not run.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.4.q': 'Which size limits apply?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.4.a': 'Each file can be up to 25 MB, you can convert 20 files at once and the output is limited to 16,000 pixels per side.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.5.q': 'Can I keep a transparent background?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.faq.5.a': 'Yes, choose PNG or WebP. JPG cannot store transparency, so transparent areas become a solid colour.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-convert-svg-to-png.cta.p': 'Open <tool_svg_converter>SVG to PNG / JPG</tool_svg_converter>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.title': 'How to Convert WebP to JPG When a Site or App Rejects It',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.description': 'Convert WebP images to JPG for forms, email and older software that do not accept WebP. Learn what happens to transparency, quality and animation.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.excerpt': 'Why websites save images as WebP, why some places still reject them, and how to convert them to widely accepted JPG files in your browser without losing the look.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.imageAlt': 'A WebP file badge turning into a JPG file badge on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.intro.1': 'You save a picture from a website, try to attach it to a job application, and the form says the file type is not supported. You look at the file and find that it ends in .webp. Many sites now serve pictures in WebP because it makes pages load faster, so more and more downloads arrive in that format, yet plenty of programs and forms were built before WebP existed and still expect JPG.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.intro.2': 'This guide explains what WebP is, why conversion to JPG is sometimes needed, what happens to transparency and animation, and how to convert files in your browser in a few clicks.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.sum.1': 'WebP is a modern image format that makes smaller files; JPG is the format that is accepted almost everywhere.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.sum.2': 'Converting WebP to JPG is a one-way trip for transparency: transparent areas are filled with white.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.sum.3': 'Animated WebP files are converted using their first frame only.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.sum.4': 'The tool re-encodes the picture at a high, fixed quality, so the result looks the same at normal sizes.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.sum.5': 'Camera and location metadata are not kept in the converted file.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s1.h': 'What is WebP, and why do you meet it so often?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s1.p1': 'WebP was created for the web. It can compress like JPG, with some loss of detail, or like PNG, without loss, and it supports transparency and animation. At similar visual quality, WebP files are typically 20 to 35 percent smaller than JPG, which is why browsers, content management systems and image services convert uploads to it automatically.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s1.p2': 'When you right-click and save a picture from a modern site, you may therefore get a WebP even though the original upload was a JPG. Nothing is wrong with the file; it simply is not the format your destination expects.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s2.h': 'Where WebP still causes problems',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s2.l1.1': 'Some upload forms, such as job portals and government sites, accept only JPG, PNG or PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s2.l1.2': 'Older email clients and office programs may not display WebP pictures.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s2.l1.3': 'Image editors that are not updated can fail to open them.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s2.l1.4': 'Some printing services only take JPG.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s2.l1.5': 'Marketplaces and CMS plugins occasionally reject files by extension.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s2.p1': 'In all these cases a JPG copy solves the problem, because JPG is accepted nearly everywhere, from phones to photo labs.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s3.h': 'How to convert in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s3.p1': '<tool_webp_to_jpg>WebP to JPG</tool_webp_to_jpg> works inside your browser tab, so your pictures are read and converted on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your WebP files (up to 20 files, each up to 25 MB).',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s3.o1.2': 'Press Convert.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s3.o1.3': 'Download the JPG files individually, or all together as a ZIP.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s3.p2': 'There is nothing to set. The tool applies a high, fixed quality, which is why you do not see a quality slider. The file names stay the same apart from the extension, so a picture called photo.webp becomes photo.jpg.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s4.h': 'Transparency becomes white',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s4.p1': 'JPG cannot store transparency. A WebP with a transparent background, such as a logo or a product cut-out, has to be flattened onto something. The tool fills the transparent areas with white. That looks right on a white page, but if you need the transparency to remain, convert to PNG instead with <tool_webp_to_png>WebP to PNG</tool_webp_to_png>, and use PNG wherever transparency matters.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s4.p2': 'If your logo ends up with a white box on a coloured page, that is the reason. Use the PNG version there.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s5.h': 'Animated WebP files',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s5.p1': 'WebP can contain an animation, like a GIF. JPG cannot, so only the first frame is converted. If you need all frames, keep the WebP or use a tool designed for animation. For a still preview, the first-frame JPG is usually fine.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s6.h': 'Does conversion lose quality?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s6.p1': 'Converting between two lossy formats always involves another round of compression, so in theory some detail is lost. In practice, at a high quality setting, the difference is not visible at normal viewing sizes. To keep it minimal:',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s6.l1.1': 'Convert once, from the original WebP, not from an already converted JPG.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s6.l1.2': 'Do not convert a picture back and forth between formats.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s6.l1.3': 'Keep the WebP as your archive copy if the picture matters.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s6.p2': 'Our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> explains how each format compresses and when to use which, and <post_how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality>resizing an image</post_how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality> helps when the picture is also too large.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s7.h': 'Making the JPG smaller or larger',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s7.p1': 'A JPG made from a WebP is often larger than the WebP, because JPG is less efficient. If a size limit is a problem, run the result through <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor>, which can also limit the width or height. Our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> shows the routine. If you need the opposite direction, to make web pictures smaller, <tool_jpg_to_webp>JPG to WebP</tool_jpg_to_webp> and <tool_png_to_webp>PNG to WebP</tool_png_to_webp> convert to WebP.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s7.p2': 'To convert to another format, or to pick the output format yourself, <tool_image_format_converter>Convert Image Format</tool_image_format_converter> covers JPG, PNG and WebP in one tool.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s8.h': 'Metadata and privacy',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s8.p1': 'The converted file does not carry the original’s metadata. Camera model, date and GPS location are not included, and embedded colour profiles are not preserved. For sharing photos, this is often an advantage. The trade-off is that colours may shift slightly on some screens, particularly for pictures in wide-gamut colour spaces.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s9.l1.1': 'Converting a logo with a transparent background to JPG and then wondering about the white box.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s9.l1.2': 'Converting a picture again and again, which compounds the loss.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s9.l1.3': 'Renaming the extension from .webp to .jpg by hand. This does not convert anything and the file may still be rejected.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s9.l1.4': 'Throwing away the original WebP.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s9.l1.5': 'Expecting an animated picture to stay animated.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s10.o1.1': 'Check whether the picture has transparency or animation.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s10.o1.2': 'Use JPG for photographs and PNG when transparency matters.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s10.o1.3': 'Convert from the original WebP, once.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s10.o1.4': 'Compress or resize the JPG if there is a size limit.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the WebP original.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.1.q': 'Why convert WebP to JPG?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.1.a': 'Some older software, email clients and upload forms still reject WebP. JPG is accepted almost everywhere.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.2.q': 'What happens to transparent areas?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.2.a': 'JPG cannot store transparency, so transparent areas are filled with white. Convert to PNG if you need to keep the transparency.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.3.q': 'Does converting reduce quality?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.3.a': 'The tool saves the JPG at a high fixed quality, so the difference is rarely visible at normal sizes. Convert once from the original to keep the loss to a minimum.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.4.q': 'What happens to animated WebP files?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.4.a': 'Only the first frame is converted.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.5.q': 'Can I convert many files at once?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.5.a': 'Yes, up to 20 files per batch, each up to 25 MB, and you can download them as a ZIP.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.6.q': 'Are my pictures uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.faq.6.a': 'No. The conversion runs inside your browser, so the files stay on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg.cta.p': 'Open <tool_webp_to_jpg>WebP to JPG</tool_webp_to_jpg>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.title': 'How to Count Words and Characters and Check Length Limits',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.description': 'Count words, characters, bytes and reading time, and check your text against limits for page titles, meta descriptions, social posts and SMS messages.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.excerpt': 'Learn how word and character counts really work, why emoji and bytes change the totals, and how to keep titles, meta descriptions, posts and text messages inside their limits.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.imageAlt': 'A text document with a counter showing words and characters on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.intro.1': 'Almost every piece of writing has a number attached to it. A school essay must be 800 words. A page title should fit in the search results. A post on a social network stops at a fixed number of characters, and a text message is split into several parts when it grows too long. Counting by hand is slow and unreliable, and the counts that different programs show do not always agree.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.intro.2': 'This guide explains how words and characters are counted, why the totals sometimes differ, which limits are worth knowing and how to check your text in your browser before you publish or send it.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.sum.1': 'A word is a run of characters separated by spaces or line breaks. Hyphenated words count as one.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.sum.2': 'Characters, characters without spaces and bytes are three different numbers. Emoji and non-Latin letters use more bytes than plain letters.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.sum.3': 'Reading time uses about 238 words per minute and speaking time about 150 words per minute.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.sum.4': 'Length limits for titles, descriptions and posts are guidelines that platforms change, so check the current rule.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.sum.5': 'Clean pasted text first, because invisible characters and extra spaces can change a count.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s1.h': 'Why counts matter',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s1.p1': 'Word counts decide whether an assignment is accepted, whether an article fits its slot and whether a translation is priced fairly. Character counts decide whether a headline is cut off with three dots, whether a meta description shows in full and whether a message fits in one SMS.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s1.p2': 'Counting also helps you write better. A paragraph of 200 words is easy to read; one of 600 words probably needs to be split. A page title that is far too long loses its ending in search results, and a title that is too short wastes space that could carry a useful keyword.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s2.h': 'How words are counted',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s2.p1': '<tool_word_counter>Word Counter</tool_word_counter> follows the rule that most programs use: a word is any run of characters separated by whitespace, meaning spaces, tabs and line breaks. This has a few consequences.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s2.l1.1': 'A hyphenated word such as well-known counts as one word.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s2.l1.2': 'A number such as 2026 counts as one word.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s2.l1.3': 'Two words joined by a slash, such as and/or, count as one word because there is no space between them.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s2.l1.4': 'A dash with spaces around it counts as a word of its own, because it is separated by whitespace.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s2.p2': 'Other tools may use slightly different rules. A word processor might treat a dash with spaces differently, and a database might split on punctuation. If an editor, a client or a school gives you a limit, count with the same program they use, and keep a small margin.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s3.h': 'Characters, characters without spaces and bytes',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s3.p1': 'A character count looks simple, but there are three useful versions of it.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s3.o1.1': 'Characters, which includes spaces and punctuation. This is the number most platforms use for their limits.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s3.o1.2': 'Characters without spaces, which some translation and writing jobs use.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s3.o1.3': 'Bytes, which is the amount of storage the text needs when saved as UTF-8.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s3.p2': 'For plain English letters, one character is one byte. Letters with accents, Arabic, Chinese and Urdu letters and emoji need two, three or four bytes each. That is why a short message full of emoji can be small in characters and still large in bytes.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s3.p3': '<tool_character_counter>Character Counter</tool_character_counter> shows all of these, plus the number of lines. It counts what you see, so an emoji counts as one character even though it is made of several pieces behind the scenes.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s4.h': 'Reading time and speaking time',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s4.p1': 'A word count is more useful when it is turned into time. The word counter estimates reading time at about 238 words per minute and speaking time at about 150 words per minute. These are typical averages for adults, not rules.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s4.p2': 'Use reading time to decide whether an article will feel short or long. Use speaking time to plan a talk or a video script: a five-minute video needs roughly 750 words, and a ten-minute talk about 1,500. Read the script aloud once and time yourself, because pauses, slides and questions change the real figure.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.h': 'Length limits worth knowing',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.p1': 'The character counter has bars for common limits, so you can see at a glance whether your text fits. The usual guideline values are:',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.l1.1': 'A page title in search results: about 60 characters.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.l1.2': 'A meta description: about 160 characters.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.l1.3': 'A post on X: 280 characters.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.l1.4': 'A single SMS in the basic GSM-7 alphabet: 160 characters.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.l1.5': 'An Instagram caption: 2,200 characters.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.p2': 'Treat these as guidelines. Search engines cut titles and descriptions by pixel width, not by character count, so a title with many wide letters is cut sooner than one with narrow letters. Platforms also change their limits from time to time. When a limit really matters, look up the current rule and leave a few characters of margin.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s5.p3': 'SMS has an extra detail. The 160-character figure applies only to messages that use the basic alphabet. If a message contains emoji or letters outside that alphabet, the limit per message drops, and a long message is split into several parts that are billed separately.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s6.h': 'Clean the text before you count it',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s6.p1': 'Text copied from web pages, PDFs and chat apps often carries extras: double spaces, empty lines, curly quotes and invisible characters such as zero-width spaces. Some of these change the count, and some break code or comparisons later.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s6.p2': '<tool_text_cleaner>Text Cleaner</tool_text_cleaner> removes them. You can trim spaces at the start and end of lines, collapse repeated spaces, remove invisible characters, delete empty lines, join lines into one paragraph, replace curly quotes with straight ones and strip HTML tags. Tick the options you need and copy the result. If the text came from a scanned document, run it through <post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>OCR</post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable> first, because a scan has no text to count until it is recognised.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s6.p3': 'When the same cleanup has to be repeated on many lines, a pattern does the job faster than clicking options. Our guide to <post_how-to-test-regular-expressions>testing regular expressions</post_how-to-test-regular-expressions> shows how to find and replace text by pattern.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s7.h': 'Fix the case and remove repeats',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s7.p1': 'Two more small tools save time when you prepare text for publishing. <tool_case_converter>Case Converter</tool_case_converter> changes a headline to title case, sentence case or capitals in one click, and it also produces camel case, snake case and kebab case for developers. Title case keeps short words such as of and the in lowercase unless they start or end the text, which follows common English style.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s7.p2': '<tool_remove_duplicate_lines>Remove Duplicate Lines</tool_remove_duplicate_lines> is useful for keyword lists, email lists and any list where the same item appears twice. It keeps the first copy of every line and preserves your order. You can choose to ignore case and whitespace, and to drop empty lines.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s8.h': 'Compare two versions of a text',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s8.p1': 'When an editor sends back a changed draft, the quickest way to see what changed is a comparison. <tool_text_diff_checker>Text Diff Checker</tool_text_diff_checker> shows added and removed lines, or added and removed words in word mode, which suits prose. Paste the original on one side and the new version on the other. Our guide to <post_how-to-compare-two-pdf-files>comparing two PDF files</post_how-to-compare-two-pdf-files> covers the same idea for documents that are not plain text.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s8.p2': 'After a comparison you can count both versions and see how much longer or shorter the new one is.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s9.h': 'Languages written without spaces',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s9.p1': 'The word rule depends on spaces, so it does not suit every language. Chinese and Japanese are written without spaces between words, so a whole sentence counts as one long run. For these languages, the number of characters is the more useful figure, and it is also the figure that translators and platforms usually use.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s9.p2': 'Urdu, Arabic and Persian use spaces between words, so word counts work, but the shape of letters changes with their position in a word. The tools count the letters you see, not the different forms of each letter.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s10.l1.1': 'Counting with a different tool than the one your client uses.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s10.l1.2': 'Forgetting that spaces count as characters in most limits.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s10.l1.3': 'Pasting text with hidden characters and trusting the number without cleaning it.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s10.l1.4': 'Writing a meta description right up to the limit, then finding that it is cut off on some screens.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s10.l1.5': 'Using the SMS limit for messages that contain emoji.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s10.l1.6': 'Counting a text that still contains the headings, captions and notes that the limit does not cover.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s11.o1.1': 'Paste your text into the counter and read words, characters and bytes.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s11.o1.2': 'Clean the text if it came from a web page or a PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s11.o1.3': 'Check the limit bar for the place where the text will be used.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s11.o1.4': 'Leave a small margin below the limit.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.s11.o1.5': 'Read the text aloud once to check the time and the flow.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.1.q': 'How are words counted?',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.1.a': 'A word is any run of characters separated by whitespace. Hyphenated words count as one, and numbers count as words.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.2.q': 'Are emoji counted as one character?',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.2.a': 'Yes. The character counter counts visible characters, so an emoji counts as one even though it uses several bytes.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.3.q': 'How is reading time calculated?',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.3.a': 'Reading time assumes 238 words per minute and speaking time 150 words per minute, which are typical averages for adults.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.4.q': 'Why do my SMS limits differ?',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.4.a': 'SMS length depends on the encoding. Messages with non-Latin characters or emoji use a shorter limit than the 160-character reference shown in the tool.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.5.q': 'Can I count Chinese or Japanese text?',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.faq.5.a': 'Yes, but the word count will show one word per run of text, because these languages are written without spaces. Use the character count instead.',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-count-words-and-characters-online.cta.p': 'Open <tool_word_counter>Word Counter</tool_word_counter>, or browse all of the <all_text>text tools</all_text>.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.title': 'How to Create a QR Code for Links, Wi-Fi, Email and Text',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.description': 'Make static QR codes for links, Wi-Fi, email and phone numbers, choose size, colours and error correction, and test them before printing.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.excerpt': 'A practical guide to QR codes: what static codes are, how to pick error correction, which colours scan best, how large to print them and how to avoid unreadable codes.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.imageAlt': 'A QR code on a card being scanned by a phone on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.intro.1': 'A QR code is the quickest way to move something from paper or a screen into a phone: a menu link on a restaurant table, Wi-Fi details on a guest card, a website address on a poster, a phone number on a business card. People point the camera, tap the notification and they are there, without typing.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.intro.2': 'Making one is easy; making one that scans reliably is the part that needs a little care. This guide explains what a QR code contains, how to choose size, colours and error correction, how to create codes for different purposes and how to test them before they are printed.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.sum.1': 'A static QR code stores its data in the pattern itself. It never expires and nothing is tracked.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.sum.2': 'Keep the content short. Long text makes a dense code that is hard to scan.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.sum.3': 'Dark-on-light colours with strong contrast scan best.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.sum.4': 'Medium error correction suits most uses; choose higher levels for codes that may get dirty or damaged.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.sum.5': 'Always test the printed code with at least two phones before you distribute it.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s1.h': 'What is inside a QR code',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s1.p1': 'A QR code is a square pattern of dark and light modules. The pattern encodes a string of characters, plus extra data used to repair errors. When your phone scans it, it decodes the string and decides what to do: open a link, join a Wi-Fi network, start an email or show text.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s1.p2': 'A static code carries its data directly. There is no company in between, no redirect, no counter and no expiry date. If you print it today, it works as long as the data is valid. A dynamic code, which many paid services sell, points to a short address on their server so the destination can change and scans can be counted; that is a different product, and it stops working when the subscription ends. Static codes made here are the opposite: permanent and untracked.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.h': 'What you can encode',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.p1': '<tool_qr_code_generator>QR Code Generator</tool_qr_code_generator> offers common types:',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.l1.1': 'A link, so scanning opens a web page.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.l1.2': 'Plain text.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.l1.3': 'Wi-Fi details, so guests can join a network without typing the password.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.l1.4': 'An email address with an optional subject and message.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.l1.5': 'A phone number, so scanning starts a call.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.p2': 'For links, use the full address including https://. If the address contains unusual characters, <tool_url_encoder_decoder>URL Encoder / Decoder</tool_url_encoder_decoder> helps you check the encoding. Keep the address short: a short link makes a simpler code that scans from further away.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s2.p3': 'If the code contains a Wi-Fi password, remember that anybody who sees the printed code can read it. For a guest network that is fine, but never print the password of your main network in a public place. Use <tool_password_generator>Password Generator</tool_password_generator> to make a strong password for a separate guest network; our guide to <post_how-to-generate-a-strong-password>generating a strong password</post_how-to-generate-a-strong-password> explains how long it should be.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.h': 'How to create a code in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.p1': 'The tool runs inside your browser tab, so what you type stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and choose what the code should contain.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.o1.2': 'Fill in the details.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.o1.3': 'Adjust the size, colours and error correction if you wish.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.o1.4': 'Download the PNG or SVG.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.o1.5': 'Test it with your phone before printing.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s3.p2': 'PNG is a picture with a fixed size, fine for screens and small prints (<post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> compares picture formats). SVG is a vector file that scales without any blur, so choose SVG for posters, signs and anything printed large.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s4.h': 'Size and quiet zone',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s4.p1': 'A code that is too small cannot be read. As a rule of thumb, a code should be at least about 2 by 2 centimetres on paper, and larger when people scan from a distance: the scanning distance should be about ten times the width of the code. A poster read from two metres needs a code about 20 centimetres wide.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s4.p2': 'Leave an empty border around the code, called the quiet zone, of at least four modules. Do not place text or pictures against the code, and do not crop it. Scanners use the border to find the edges.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s5.h': 'Colours and contrast',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s5.p1': 'Scanners look for the difference between dark and light modules. The best choice is black on white, and any dark colour on a light background works. Problems appear when:',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s5.l1.1': 'The light colour is not light enough, or the dark colour is not dark enough.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s5.l1.2': 'The colours are inverted, so the modules are light on a dark background. Many phones read this, but not all scanners do.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s5.l1.3': 'A busy photograph sits behind the code.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s5.l1.4': 'A gradient crosses the code and changes contrast from one corner to another.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s5.p2': 'If you use brand colours, check the contrast and test with several phones.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s6.h': 'Error correction',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s6.p1': 'QR codes include redundancy so they can be read even when part of them is damaged or hidden. You can choose how much:',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s6.l1.1': 'Low and Medium keep the code simple and are fine for clean screens and paper.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s6.l1.2': 'Quartile and High allow for dirt, wear or a small logo in the centre, but they make the code denser, which means it needs more space to remain readable.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s6.p2': 'Medium suits most uses. Choose Quartile or High if the code will be on a sticker outdoors, on a product or on a surface that gets scratched. Higher levels make the code denser and harder to scan at small sizes, so enlarge the print if you raise the level.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.h': 'Testing before printing',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.p1': 'Testing takes two minutes and saves reprints:',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.o1.1': 'Scan the code from your screen with the phone’s camera, not only with the app that made it.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.o1.2': 'Scan the printed proof from the real distance it will be used at.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.o1.3': 'Try an iPhone and an Android phone, and a cheaper older model if you can.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.o1.4': 'Check that the result is what you intended, for example that the link opens and goes to the right page.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.o1.5': 'Try in poor light.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s7.p2': 'You can also check a code with <tool_qr_code_scanner>QR Code Scanner</tool_qr_code_scanner>, which reads a picture of a code and shows its content.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s8.h': 'Safety for people who scan',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s8.p1': 'Treat codes you find in public with the same caution as links in email. Criminals paste fake codes over genuine ones on parking meters and posters. If you put codes in public places, make them hard to cover, and tell people what they link to. If you scan one, look at the address your phone shows before you tap.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s9.l1.1': 'Encoding long text and getting a dense code that nobody can scan.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s9.l1.2': 'Printing too small, or cropping the quiet zone.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s9.l1.3': 'Low contrast or inverted colours.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s9.l1.4': 'Never testing the printed code.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s9.l1.5': 'Putting a private Wi-Fi password in a public place.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s10.o1.1': 'Keep the content short and complete, with https:// for links.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s10.o1.2': 'Use dark on light colours with strong contrast.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s10.o1.3': 'Choose Medium error correction unless the code will be damaged.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s10.o1.4': 'Use SVG for large prints and leave a border around the code.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.s10.o1.5': 'Test the printed code with two phones.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.1.q': 'Do the codes expire?',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.1.a': 'No. These are static codes: the data is stored in the code itself, so they work forever and nothing is tracked.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.2.q': 'Which error-correction level should I choose?',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.2.a': 'Medium suits most uses. Choose Quartile or High if the code may get dirty or damaged, but higher levels make the code denser and harder to scan at small sizes.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.3.q': 'Can I use the codes commercially?',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.3.a': 'Yes. The QR code standard is open, and codes made here carry no fees, watermarks or tracking from us.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.4.q': 'Should I download PNG or SVG?',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.4.a': 'PNG is fine for screens and small prints. Choose SVG for large prints, because it scales without blur.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.5.q': 'Can I track how many people scan my code?',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.5.a': 'No. These are static codes without scan tracking.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.6.q': 'Is my data uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.faq.6.a': 'No. The code is created inside your browser, so what you type stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-create-a-qr-code.cta.p': 'Open <tool_qr_code_generator>QR Code Generator</tool_qr_code_generator>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.title': 'How to Crop an Image to an Exact Size or Aspect Ratio',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.description': 'Crop a photo to an exact region or a fixed aspect ratio with a live preview, fine-tune the position with pixel values and choose the output format.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.excerpt': 'Learn to crop pictures for profile photos, thumbnails and print: pick an aspect ratio, set exact X, Y, width and height in pixels, and keep the quality.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.imageAlt': 'A photo with a crop frame and corner handles on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.intro.1': 'A photo almost never has the right shape for where it is going. A profile picture needs a square, a video thumbnail needs a wide frame, a passport photo needs a particular proportion, and a banner needs a long strip. Cropping cuts away what you do not need and leaves the part that matters, in the shape the destination wants.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to crop an image in your browser, how aspect ratios work, how to use exact pixel values for precision and how to avoid the mistakes that make a cropped picture look cramped or blurry.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.sum.1': 'Cropping keeps the original pixels of the area you choose; it does not shrink or blur them.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.sum.2': 'Choose a fixed aspect ratio, or leave it free, and drag the crop box over the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.sum.3': 'Type X, Y, width and height in pixels for exact control. Y and X can be set anywhere inside the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.sum.4': 'Crop first, then resize, because it keeps the most detail.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.sum.5': 'JPG and WebP results are saved at a high, fixed quality; PNG is lossless.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s1.h': 'What cropping does',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s1.p1': 'An image is a grid of pixels. Cropping selects a rectangle of that grid and discards everything outside it. Nothing inside the rectangle is changed, so there is no loss of quality from the crop itself. The picture simply becomes smaller in pixel dimensions.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s1.p2': 'This is different from resizing, which changes how many pixels the picture has and therefore needs to invent or merge pixels. If you need both, crop first and resize afterwards with <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer>; <post_how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality>our resizing guide</post_how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality> explains pixels, percentages and quality. Our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> explains why that order keeps more detail.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.h': 'How to crop in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.p1': '<tool_image_cropper>Crop Image</tool_image_cropper> works inside your browser tab, so your picture is read from your device.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add an image (up to 25 MB).',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.o1.2': 'Pick an aspect ratio, or leave it free.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.o1.3': 'Drag the crop box, or drag its handles, until it frames what you want.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.o1.4': 'Fine-tune the position and size with the numeric fields.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.o1.5': 'Choose the output format, or keep the same as the original.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.o1.6': 'Press Crop and download the result.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s2.p2': 'The tool shows the original size, so you always know how much room you have.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.h': 'Aspect ratios',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.p1': 'An aspect ratio describes the shape of a rectangle as width to height. Common ones:',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.l1.1': '1:1, a square, for profile pictures and many social posts.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.l1.2': '4:3 and 3:2, the classic photo shapes.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.l1.3': '16:9, wide frames for video thumbnails and slides.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.l1.4': '9:16, tall frames for phone stories.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.l1.5': 'A free ratio, when you just want to cut away part of a picture.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s3.p2': 'With a ratio selected, the crop box keeps that shape as you resize it, and typing a new width or height adjusts the other one to match. Choose free when the destination does not care about the shape.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s4.h': 'Exact values in pixels',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s4.p1': 'The numeric fields show the position and size of the box in pixels of the original picture:',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s4.l1.1': 'X is the distance from the left edge.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s4.l1.2': 'Y is the distance from the top edge.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s4.l1.3': 'Width and Height are the size of the box.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s4.p2': 'Type exact values when a form tells you precisely what it wants, such as 600 by 800 pixels, or when you need to crop several pictures in the same way. You can place the box anywhere inside the picture: if you type a position that would push the box outside, the box shrinks so it stays inside, and with a locked ratio it keeps its shape.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s4.p3': 'A tip: set the aspect ratio first, then the width, then adjust X and Y to position the box. This way the numbers cooperate instead of fighting each other.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s5.h': 'Composition: what to keep',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s5.p1': 'A crop is a creative decision. A few guidelines help:',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s5.l1.1': 'Leave breathing room. A face cropped tightly at the chin and forehead looks uncomfortable.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s5.l1.2': 'Place the subject off centre for landscapes and wide frames. The rule of thirds is a good starting point.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s5.l1.3': 'For profile pictures, keep the eyes in the upper third and make sure the face will still be recognisable at a small size.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s5.l1.4': 'Remove distractions at the edges, such as part of a stranger or a bright object.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s5.l1.5': 'If the subject must be centred, as in an ID photo, check the requirements of the form first.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s6.h': 'Quality and format',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s6.p1': 'Cropping never reduces the quality of the pixels you keep, but saving the result in a lossy format applies compression once more. For JPG and WebP, the tool uses a high, fixed quality, so normal use shows no visible loss. PNG is lossless. You can choose a different output format, for example PNG to keep sharp edges, or keep the original format.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s6.p2': 'If you need a smaller file after cropping, run <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor>, and to change the format, <tool_image_format_converter>Convert Image Format</tool_image_format_converter> does it. Our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> helps you choose.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s7.h': 'Before and after cropping',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s7.p1': 'Two tools often help around a crop:',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s7.l1.1': 'If the photo is sideways or tilted by a quarter turn, use <tool_image_rotator>Rotate Image</tool_image_rotator> first, then crop. Rotating after cropping can leave you with the wrong part of the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s7.l1.2': 'If the picture is mirrored, such as a selfie, <tool_image_flipper>Flip Image</tool_image_flipper> corrects it.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s7.p2': 'The crop tool handles one image at a time, which is right for careful framing. For many pictures that only need resizing to the same width, use the resizer instead.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s8.h': 'Limits to know',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s8.l1.1': 'One image at a time, up to 25 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s8.l1.2': 'Animated images use the first frame only.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s8.l1.3': 'The crop works on the visible picture. Camera metadata such as location is not kept in the result.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s9.l1.1': 'Cropping very tightly and then enlarging the result, which makes it soft.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s9.l1.2': 'Choosing a ratio that does not match the destination, so the platform crops it again automatically and cuts off the wrong part.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s9.l1.3': 'Forgetting to rotate before cropping.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s9.l1.4': 'Overwriting the original. Keep the uncropped file in case you need a different framing.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s9.l1.5': 'Ignoring the output format. PNG for sharp graphics and JPG for photographs are the usual choices.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s10.o1.1': 'Check what shape and pixel size the destination wants.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s10.o1.2': 'Rotate or flip the picture first if needed.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s10.o1.3': 'Select an aspect ratio, then frame the subject.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s10.o1.4': 'Fine-tune with pixel values and look at the preview.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.s10.o1.5': 'Choose the output format and download. Keep the original.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.1.q': 'Does cropping reduce quality?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.1.a': 'Cropping keeps the original pixels. JPG and WebP results are saved at a high fixed quality, so normal use shows no visible loss.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.2.q': 'Can I crop to an exact pixel size?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.2.a': 'Yes. Type the width and height in pixels, and set X and Y to position the box. With a locked aspect ratio, the box keeps its shape.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.3.q': 'Why does the box shrink when I enter a large position?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.3.a': 'The crop box must stay inside the picture. If the position you type would push it outside, the box becomes smaller so that it fits.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.4.q': 'Can I crop several images at once?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.4.a': 'No. Cropping needs individual framing, so the tool works on one image at a time.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.5.q': 'Is my picture uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.5.a': 'No. The tool runs inside your browser, so the image stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.6.q': 'What happens to animated pictures?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.faq.6.a': 'Animated images use the first frame.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size.cta.p': 'Open <tool_image_cropper>Crop Image</tool_image_cropper>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.title': 'How to Crop PDF Pages and Trim Margins Without Losing Text',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.description': 'Trim white margins or keep only a chosen area of every PDF page with a live preview, and learn why cropping hides content instead of deleting it.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.excerpt': 'Crop PDF pages by dragging a box or typing margins, apply it to all or some pages, and understand why cropping is not a way to hide sensitive content.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.imageAlt': 'A PDF page with a crop frame trimming the white margins on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.intro.1': 'Wide white margins waste space on a phone screen, shrink the text when you print a handout, and make a scanned page look like a photograph of a table. Cropping removes what you do not need from view and lets the content fill the page.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to crop PDF pages with a live preview, how to apply the same crop to many pages, and the most important thing to understand about cropping: it hides what is outside the box, it does not delete it.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.sum.1': 'Cropping changes the visible area of each page. The content outside it is hidden, not removed.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.sum.2': 'Select the area to keep by dragging a box or typing margins, and watch the live preview.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.sum.3': 'The crop is applied as a share of each page, so pages of different sizes are trimmed proportionally.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.sum.4': 'Do not use cropping to hide sensitive information. Use Redact PDF for that.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.sum.5': 'Check several pages after cropping, because layouts can differ from page to page.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s1.h': 'When cropping helps',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s1.l1.1': 'A scanned document has thick black or grey borders from the scanner.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s1.l1.2': 'A report exported from a spreadsheet has huge empty margins around a small table.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s1.l1.3': 'You want to read a two-page spread, a slide or an article on a small screen.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s1.l1.4': 'A form portal wants a page with the content as large as possible.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s1.l1.5': 'You are preparing a handout and want the text to print larger.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s1.p1': 'Cropping is also the right tool when the page is the wrong shape for its content, for example a wide landscape table placed on a portrait page.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s2.h': 'How to crop a PDF in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s2.p1': '<tool_crop_pdf>Crop PDF</tool_crop_pdf> works inside your browser tab, so the file is read from your device. The steps:',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it is password-protected.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s2.o1.2': 'Pick a page to preview.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s2.o1.3': 'Drag the crop box over the area you want to keep, or type the margins to trim.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s2.o1.4': 'Choose which pages to crop: all of them or a selection.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s2.o1.5': 'Crop and download the new PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s3.h': 'Dragging versus typing margins',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s3.p1': 'Dragging is intuitive: you see the page and draw a frame around what matters. Typing margins is better when you want precision or the same result on several documents, for example 10 percent off each side.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s3.p2': 'You can combine both: drag roughly, then correct the numbers. Leave a little breathing room around the content. A crop that touches the edge of the text looks cramped, and printing can cut off the outermost millimetres.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s4.h': 'Pages of different sizes',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s4.p1': 'The crop is stored as a share of each page, not as a fixed size in millimetres. That means the same proportions are applied to every page you choose, so pages of different sizes are trimmed by the same percentage. It works well for documents whose pages are all alike, such as scans from one machine.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s4.p2': 'It works less well when pages differ in layout. A document with one portrait page and one landscape page can come out oddly. In that case, crop the groups separately by choosing the pages for each run, or <tool_rotate_pdf>rotate</tool_rotate_pdf> the odd pages first so that all pages have the same orientation, as described in <post_how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently>our rotation guide</post_how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently>.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s5.h': 'Cropping hides, it does not delete',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s5.p1': 'This is the point people most often get wrong. A PDF page has a visible area, called the crop box, and the content may extend beyond it. Cropping only changes that visible area. The text and pictures outside the box are still inside the file, and a PDF editor can bring them back.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s5.p2': 'Consequences:',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s5.l1.1': 'Cropping does not make the file smaller in any meaningful way.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s5.l1.2': 'You must not rely on cropping to hide a name, an account number or a signature. Anybody can restore it.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s5.l1.3': 'If you need the content outside the box to be gone for good, use <tool_redact_pdf>Redact PDF</tool_redact_pdf>, which rebuilds redacted pages as pictures so the hidden content does not exist in the result. Our guide to <post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly>redacting a PDF properly</post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly> explains the workflow.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s5.p3': 'For the same reason, cropping is safe for cosmetic work and unsafe for confidentiality.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s6.h': 'Cropping scans',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s6.p1': 'Scans benefit most from cropping, because they often have shadows and borders at the edges. A few tips:',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s6.l1.1': 'Crop slightly inside the paper edge so no dark line remains.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s6.l1.2': 'Keep a small margin of white around the text.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s6.l1.3': 'If the scan itself is crooked, cropping cannot straighten it. Rescan it, or fix it before turning pictures into a PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s6.p2': 'If the scan is made from photographs, <tool_scan_to_pdf>Scan to PDF</tool_scan_to_pdf> can turn them into clean pages with consistent size, and our guide to <post_convert-images-to-pdf>converting images to PDF</post_convert-images-to-pdf> explains the options.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s7.h': 'Keeping a few pages only',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s7.p1': 'Sometimes the real aim is not a different margin but fewer pages. Use <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> to keep only the pages you need, and crop afterwards. Fewer pages and tighter margins together also reduce the work for the reader.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s8.h': 'What to check after cropping',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s8.l1.1': 'Is any text cut off at an edge? Look at the first, the last and a dense middle page.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s8.l1.2': 'Do page numbers and footers still show?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s8.l1.3': 'Are pages with different layouts all acceptable?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s8.l1.4': 'Is the file still the size you expect? If it is large because of images, run <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s9.l1.1': 'Cropping away a page number or footer without noticing.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s9.l1.2': 'Using cropping to hide confidential content.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s9.l1.3': 'Applying one crop to pages that have very different layouts.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s9.l1.4': 'Cropping too tightly, so printed pages lose their outer edge.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s9.l1.5': 'Overwriting the original file instead of saving a new one.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s10.o1.1': 'Preview a representative page and draw the crop box.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s10.o1.2': 'Leave a small margin around the content.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s10.o1.3': 'Apply to all pages only if they share a layout.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s10.o1.4': 'Check the first, a middle and the last page.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the original, and use Redact PDF when content must really disappear.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.1.q': 'Is the cropped-away content deleted?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.1.a': 'No. Cropping changes the visible area of each page, but the content outside it is still inside the file. Do not rely on cropping to hide sensitive information.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.2.q': 'What happens if my pages have different sizes?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.2.a': 'The same proportions are applied to every page you choose, so pages of different sizes are trimmed by the same percentage.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.3.q': 'Can I crop only some pages?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.3.a': 'Yes. Choose which pages to crop before you download the result.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.4.q': 'Will cropping make my PDF smaller?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.4.a': 'Not meaningfully, because the hidden content stays in the file. Use Compress PDF if you need a smaller file.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.5.q': 'Can I crop a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.5.a': 'Not directly. Unlock it first, then crop the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.6.q': 'Is my file uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.faq.6.a': 'No. The tool runs inside your browser, so the PDF stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-crop-pdf-pages.cta.p': 'Open <tool_crop_pdf>Crop PDF</tool_crop_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.title': 'How to Edit a Photo in Your Browser: Adjust, Crop, Text',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.description': 'Edit a photo online without installing software: fix brightness and colour, crop and rotate, add text and export at full quality in the format you need.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.excerpt': 'A step-by-step guide to editing photos in your browser: colour adjustments, filters, rotate and crop, sharpening and text, the order in which edits are applied and how to export without losing quality.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.imageAlt': 'A photo with sliders for brightness and colour beside it on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.intro.1': 'Most photos need only a little help. A picture taken indoors is too dark, a snapshot from a phone is slightly crooked, a product photo has too much empty space around it, or a poster needs a line of text. For these jobs you do not need a large program with layers and brushes. A simple editor in your browser is quicker, and it keeps the picture on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.intro.2': 'This guide shows how to make everyday edits with a live preview, in which order the edits are applied, how to export without losing quality and which other tools complete the job.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.sum.1': 'You can adjust colours, apply a filter, rotate, crop, sharpen, add a vignette and add text, with a preview that updates as you go.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.sum.2': 'Your original file is never changed. The edited picture is created as a new download.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.sum.3': 'Edits are applied at the full size of the picture, not at the preview size.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.sum.4': 'PNG keeps every pixel. For JPG and WebP, use a quality of 90 or higher to keep photos looking the same.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.sum.5': 'Location and camera details are not copied into the edited picture.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.h': 'What the editor can and cannot do',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.p1': '<tool_photo_editor>Photo Editor</tool_photo_editor> is built for quick, careful edits of one photo at a time.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.p2': 'It can:',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.l1.1': 'Adjust colours, such as brightness, contrast and saturation.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.l1.2': 'Apply a filter for a quick change of mood.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.l1.3': 'Rotate and crop.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.l1.4': 'Blur and sharpen.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.l1.5': 'Add a vignette, which darkens the edges slightly.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.l1.6': 'Add text on the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s1.p3': 'It does not have layers, brushes or AI features, so it is not a replacement for a full design program. That is on purpose: for most photos, a few adjustments are all that is needed, and a small tool is faster to learn.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s2.h': 'How to edit a photo',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s2.p1': 'The editor works inside your browser tab, so your photo is not uploaded.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add a photo. It accepts one photo at a time, up to 25 MB and about 50 megapixels.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s2.o1.2': 'Use the tabs to adjust colours, apply a filter, rotate or crop, and add text. The preview updates as you go.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s2.o1.3': 'Choose the format and download your edited photo.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s2.p2': 'Because the preview is a smaller version for speed, the edits are applied again at the full size when you export. The result is as sharp as your original.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s3.h': 'The order of edits',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s3.p1': 'The editor applies edits in a fixed order: rotate and crop first, then colour adjustments, then blur and sharpen, then the vignette and finally the text. Knowing the order helps you predict the result.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s3.l1.1': 'Cropping comes first, so every later effect applies only to the part you keep. A vignette, for instance, darkens the edges of the cropped picture, not of the original.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s3.l1.2': 'Text comes last, so it is never blurred or recoloured by the effects that came before.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s3.l1.3': 'If you want a different order, for example to sharpen after adding text, export once, then open the result and make the second edit.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s4.h': 'Fixing exposure and colour',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s4.p1': 'Most photos improve with small steps.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s4.o1.1': 'Start with brightness if the picture is too dark or too bright.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s4.o1.2': 'Add a little contrast if it looks flat, and lower it if it looks harsh.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s4.o1.3': 'Adjust saturation last. A small increase makes colours livelier, and too much makes skin look orange.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s4.o1.4': 'Look at the picture at full size and then at a small size, the way people will see it.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s4.p2': 'Make changes in small amounts. A photo that looks edited is usually over-edited. If you are unsure, take the sliders back to roughly half of where you first put them.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s5.h': 'Rotating and cropping',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s5.p1': 'A crooked horizon or a wrong orientation is easy to fix. Rotate by a quarter turn for portrait or landscape corrections, and by a small angle to level a crooked picture. Then crop to remove empty space and to give the subject room.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s5.p2': 'Crop for a purpose. A square crop suits a profile picture, a wide crop suits a banner and a tall crop suits a story. If you need an exact pixel size or a fixed ratio, <tool_image_cropper>Crop Image</tool_image_cropper> and our guide to <post_how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size>cropping an image to a specific size</post_how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size> go into more detail. For rotating or mirroring a whole batch, <tool_image_rotator>Rotate Image</tool_image_rotator> does it in one go.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s6.h': 'Sharpening and blurring',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s6.p1': 'Sharpening increases the contrast at edges, which makes a slightly soft picture look crisper. Use a small amount. Too much creates bright outlines around objects, which look artificial.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s6.p2': 'Blur softens a picture and can be used to calm a busy background. It affects the whole picture, so it is not the way to hide a face or a number. If you need to hide part of a picture, see our guide to <post_how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image>blurring or pixelating part of an image</post_how-to-blur-or-pixelate-part-of-an-image>, which also explains why a black box is safer for secrets.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s7.h': 'Adding text',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s7.p1': 'Text is useful for labels, captions and posters. Choose a size that is readable at the size where the picture will be seen, and a colour that contrasts with the background behind it. White text on a bright sky is hard to read, and dark text on a dark corner disappears.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s7.p2': 'Because text is applied last, it always sits on top of every other edit and is not affected by the effects. For a name or logo on many pictures, <tool_image_watermark>Watermark Image</tool_image_watermark> is faster, and our guide to <post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos>adding a watermark to photos</post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-photos> explains how.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s8.h': 'Exporting without losing quality',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s8.p1': 'The last step is the format, and it matters.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s8.l1.1': 'PNG keeps every pixel. Choose it when you plan to edit the picture again or need transparency, and expect a bigger file.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s8.l1.2': 'JPG and WebP are lossy, which means that they make the file smaller by throwing away detail that is hard to see. A quality of 90 or higher keeps photos looking the same as the original to most eyes.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s8.p2': 'Do not edit and save the same JPG again and again. Each save applies compression once more, and detail slowly disappears. Keep your original, make all edits in one session and export once. Our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> explains which format is right for which job.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s9.h': 'Make the file the right size',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s9.p1': 'After editing, the picture may be larger than you need. Phone photos often have 12 megapixels or more, which is far more than a web page shows. <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer> reduces the dimensions, and <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> reduces the file size. Our guide to <post_how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality>resizing an image without losing quality</post_how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality> explains the steps.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s9.p2': 'If you want to turn a series of edited pictures into a short loop, our guide to <post_how-to-make-a-gif-from-images>making an animated GIF from images</post_how-to-make-a-gif-from-images> shows how.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s10.h': 'Privacy',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s10.p1': 'Photos carry hidden data, such as the camera model and the place where they were taken. The edited picture is created as a new file, and details such as the location are not copied into it. If you also want to check other files before you share them, see our guide to <post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs>removing metadata from photos and PDFs</post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs>.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s11.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s11.l1.1': 'Over-editing, so that the picture looks unnatural.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s11.l1.2': 'Saving a JPG again and again, which lowers quality each time.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s11.l1.3': 'Cropping too tightly and leaving no room for later changes.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s11.l1.4': 'Choosing a text colour that disappears on part of the picture.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s11.l1.5': 'Forgetting to keep the original file.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s11.l1.6': 'Judging the picture only at full size and not at the size where it will be seen.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s12.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s12.o1.1': 'Rotate and crop first.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s12.o1.2': 'Fix brightness and contrast, then saturation, in small steps.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s12.o1.3': 'Sharpen a little, if needed.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s12.o1.4': 'Add text last, with a colour that stands out.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.s12.o1.5': 'Export once, at quality 90 or higher, and keep the original.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.1.q': 'Is the original file changed?',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.1.a': 'No. Your file is never modified. The edited picture is created as a new download.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.2.q': 'Does exporting lose quality?',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.2.a': 'PNG keeps every pixel. JPG and WebP are lossy. Use a quality of 90 or higher to keep photos looking the same. Edits are applied at the full size of your picture, not at the preview size.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.3.q': 'What are the size limits?',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.3.a': 'One photo at a time, up to 25 MB and about 50 megapixels.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.4.q': 'In what order are edits applied?',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.4.a': 'Rotate and crop, colour adjustments, blur and sharpen, vignette, then text.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.5.q': 'Does the edited picture keep my location?',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.faq.5.a': 'No. EXIF details such as location are not copied to the edited picture.',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser.cta.p': 'Open <tool_photo_editor>Photo Editor</tool_photo_editor>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.title': 'How to Extract Pages From a PDF and Save Them as a New File',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.description': 'Pick the pages you need from a PDF and save them as a new document. Learn page ranges, how extracting differs from splitting and how to keep the order.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.excerpt': 'Select pages by thumbnail or range, save just those pages as a new PDF, and learn when to extract, split or remove pages instead.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.imageAlt': 'Selected pages being lifted out of a stack of PDF pages into a new document on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.intro.1': 'Most PDFs arrive with more pages than you need. A 60-page contract where only the signature page matters, a bank statement where you need one month, a report where a manager asked for pages 4 to 9: sending the whole file wastes the reader’s time and sometimes shares information they were never meant to see.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.intro.2': 'Extracting pages solves this. You choose the pages you want, and the tool writes them into a new PDF. This guide shows how to do it, how page ranges work, and how extracting relates to splitting and removing pages.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.sum.1': 'Extracting creates a new PDF that contains only the pages you chose. Your original is not changed.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.sum.2': 'Choose pages by clicking thumbnails or by typing a range such as 1-3, 8.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.sum.3': 'Extracted pages keep the order they had in the original document.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.sum.4': 'Extract the pages to keep, or use Remove PDF Pages when it is easier to name the pages to drop.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.sum.5': 'Check the result before you send it, because the pages you left out are not in the new file at all.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s1.h': 'What “extract” means',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s1.p1': 'A PDF is a list of pages. Extracting builds a new list that contains a copy of some of them. The text stays real, selectable text, the pictures keep their quality and the page layout is untouched, because the pages are copied as they are rather than redrawn.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s1.p2': 'This is different from converting pages to pictures. If you want images, use <tool_pdf_to_jpg>PDF to JPG</tool_pdf_to_jpg>; our guide to <post_how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg>converting PDF to JPG</post_how-to-convert-pdf-to-jpg> explains resolution and page ranges. If you want a PDF with fewer pages, extraction is the right operation.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s2.h': 'How to extract pages in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s2.p1': '<tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> works inside your browser tab, so the PDF is read from your device. The steps:',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). If it is password-protected, unlock it first.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Wait for the page thumbnails to appear.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Select the pages you want by clicking their thumbnails, or type a range in the range field and press Apply range.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Check the count shown under the field. It tells you how many pages are selected.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s2.o1.5': 'Press Extract and download the new PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s3.h': 'How page ranges work',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s3.p1': 'Ranges are the fastest way to select many pages. Separate items with commas and use a dash for a stretch of pages:',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s3.l1.1': '5 selects only page 5.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s3.l1.2': '2-4 selects pages 2, 3 and 4.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s3.l1.3': '1-3, 8, 12-14 selects pages 1, 2, 3, 8, 12, 13 and 14.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s3.l1.4': '10- selects page 10 and every page after it.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s3.p2': 'Selecting by thumbnail is better when you do not remember page numbers but recognise the page when you see it, for example a signature page or a certain chart. You can mix both methods: type a range, then click thumbnails to add or remove single pages.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s4.h': 'Order matters',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s4.p1': 'Pages in the new file appear in the same order as in the original, no matter in which order you clicked them. If you need a different order, for example the summary first, extract first and then use <tool_reorder_pdf_pages>Reorder PDF Pages</tool_reorder_pdf_pages> on the new file.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s5.h': 'Extract, split or remove?',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s5.p1': 'These three operations sound similar but give different results:',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s5.l1.1': 'Extract: you name the pages to keep, and get one new file.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s5.l1.2': 'Remove: you name the pages to drop, and get the rest as one new file. <tool_remove_pdf_pages>Remove PDF Pages</tool_remove_pdf_pages> is quicker when only a few pages must go.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s5.l1.3': 'Split: you cut the document into several files at once, for example one file per chapter. <tool_split_pdf>Split PDF</tool_split_pdf> is the tool for that, and our guide to <post_how-to-split-a-pdf>splitting a PDF</post_how-to-split-a-pdf> explains the options.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s5.p2': 'A simple rule: if you can describe the pages you want in a few words, extract. If you can describe the pages you do not want, remove them. If you need several smaller documents, split.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s6.h': 'Typical uses',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s6.l1.1': 'Sending one statement from a long bank or utility document.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s6.l1.2': 'Pulling out the pages a form portal asks for, such as only the identity page of a scan.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s6.l1.3': 'Sharing a chapter of an e-book or a manual with a colleague.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s6.l1.4': 'Removing an internal cover sheet or appendix before a document goes to a customer.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s6.l1.5': 'Combining chosen pages from several documents: extract from each one and then join the results with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> covers the details.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s7.h': 'Privacy: what is really left out',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s7.p1': 'Because extraction copies pages, the pages you did not select are not present in the new file. That makes it a sensible way to share part of a document. Two cautions apply:',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s7.l1.1': 'Document properties such as the title and author may or may not be present in the new file. If those details matter, open <tool_edit_pdf_metadata>Edit PDF Metadata</tool_edit_pdf_metadata> on the result and check them.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s7.l1.2': 'Pages that you do include still contain everything on them. If a page has a name or a number that must be hidden, use <tool_redact_pdf>Redact PDF</tool_redact_pdf> rather than hoping nobody looks.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s8.h': 'Keeping the result small',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s8.p1': 'An extracted file is usually much smaller than the original, but it can still be large if the pages contain photos or scans. If an upload limit is a problem, run the new file through <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>. Our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains what to expect.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s9.l1.1': 'Selecting pages by printed page number when the PDF’s own numbering is different. The tool counts physical pages from the first one, so a cover page makes printed page 1 into PDF page 2.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s9.l1.2': 'Forgetting to check the result. Open the new file and make sure the right pages are in it.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s9.l1.3': 'Sending a file that still has sensitive document properties.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s9.l1.4': 'Extracting from an already-compressed copy when the original would give better quality.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s9.l1.5': 'Overwriting the original. Save the new file under a clear name such as invoice-march.pdf.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s10.o1.1': 'Unlock the PDF if it has a password.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s10.o1.2': 'Select pages by range or by thumbnail.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s10.o1.3': 'Look at the count shown under the field.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s10.o1.4': 'Extract, download and open the result.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the original file untouched.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Does extracting pages change my original PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.1.a': 'No. The tool reads your file and writes a new one. The original stays exactly as it was.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Can I extract pages from a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.2.a': 'Not directly. Unlock the file first with Unlock PDF, then extract from the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Can I use extraction to delete pages?',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.3.a': 'Yes. Select the pages you want to keep and extract them; the others are left out of the new file. For a few pages, Remove PDF Pages is quicker.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.4.q': 'Will the text stay selectable?',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.4.a': 'Yes. Pages are copied, not redrawn, so text, links and images behave as in the original.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.5.q': 'Is there a limit on file size?',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.5.a': 'PDFs up to 100 MB are supported. Very large documents depend on the memory of your device.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.6.q': 'Can I choose pages in a different order?',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.faq.6.a': 'The new file keeps the original order. Use Reorder PDF Pages afterwards if you need another order.',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-extract-pages-from-a-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.title': 'How to Fill Out a PDF Form Online, Even Without Form Fields',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.description': 'Fill in text boxes, checkboxes and menus of a PDF form in your browser, flatten the answers, and type onto flat PDFs and scans that have no fields.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.excerpt': 'Complete fillable PDF forms, decide whether to keep them editable or flatten them, and learn what to do with scanned forms and XFA forms that have no usable fields.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.imageAlt': 'A PDF form with filled text boxes and a ticked checkbox on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.intro.1': 'Applications, tax declarations, insurance claims, school registrations: a lot of paperwork still arrives as a PDF, and the first instinct is to print it, fill it in by hand and scan it again. That is slow, the handwriting is rarely tidy and the scan is large. Filling the form on screen is faster and produces a cleaner document.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to fill in a PDF form in your browser, what the difference is between editable and flattened results, and what to do when the PDF turns out not to be a real form at all.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.sum.1': 'Fillable PDFs contain real form fields: text boxes, checkboxes, radio buttons and drop-down menus.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.sum.2': 'You can keep the filled form editable, or flatten it so the answers become part of the page.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.sum.3': 'A PDF without fields, such as a scan, can still be typed onto by clicking the page where the text should go.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.sum.4': 'XFA forms, used by some government and bank forms, cannot have their fields filled here.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.sum.5': 'Check every page of the filled document before you send it.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s1.h': 'Fillable forms versus flat documents',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s1.p1': 'Not every PDF that looks like a form is one. There are three situations:',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s1.l1.1': 'A fillable form has real fields. When you open it, you can click a box and type. This is the best case.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s1.l1.2': 'A flat form is only a picture or plain page. It looks like a form, but there is nothing to click, because the boxes are just drawn lines. Scans of paper forms are flat.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s1.l1.3': 'An XFA form is a special dynamic form, built with an older Adobe technology. Some government and bank forms use it. Many tools cannot fill its fields.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s1.p2': '<tool_fill_pdf_forms>Fill PDF Forms</tool_fill_pdf_forms> handles the first case with proper fields, offers a typing mode for the second, and tells you honestly about the third.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s2.h': 'How to fill a form in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s2.p1': 'The tool runs inside your browser tab, so the PDF is read from your device.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add the PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it has a password.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s2.o1.2': 'If the form has fields, they are listed under the file name, grouped by page. Fill them in: type into text boxes, tick checkboxes, choose from menus.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s2.o1.3': 'Decide whether to flatten the form.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s2.o1.4': 'Save and download the filled PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s2.p2': 'Fields marked as required show an asterisk, read-only fields are shown but cannot be changed, and the count under the list tells you how many fillable fields were found.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s3.h': 'Editable or flattened?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s3.p1': 'After filling, you choose how the answers are stored:',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s3.l1.1': 'Keep editable: the fields stay live. You or the recipient can change an answer later. Use this for drafts and for files you still need to correct.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s3.l1.2': 'Flatten: the answers are burned into the page and the fields are removed, so nobody can edit them. Use this for the copy you send, upload or archive.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s3.p2': 'A sensible habit is to keep two copies: an editable one for yourself and a flattened one to send. If a form portal rejects the file, try the flattened version, because some systems struggle with live fields.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s4.h': 'What about characters and languages?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s4.p1': 'Text in fields is written with the form’s font, which covers Latin letters, digits and common symbols. Other alphabets are not available in fields. If a name contains characters the font does not have, the tool tells you instead of writing garbage. For non-Latin text, a flat PDF or a different approach, such as typing the name in Latin letters as the form instructions allow, is usually needed.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s5.h': 'No fields? Type directly onto the page',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s5.p1': 'If the PDF has no fillable fields, for example a scan or a form that is only a picture, the tool switches to a typing mode:',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s5.o1.1': 'Click on the page where a line of text should go.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s5.o1.2': 'Type the text in the box that appears under the preview.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s5.o1.3': 'Repeat for every item, on any page.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s5.o1.4': 'Save the PDF with the text.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s5.p2': 'The text is written into the page and cannot be edited afterwards, so keep the original. Choose a text size that fits the line, and check the placement on the preview before you save. This mode is also a way to fill XFA forms that cannot be completed with fields, by typing onto their pages.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s6.h': 'Signatures',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s6.p1': 'Signature fields and buttons are shown but cannot be filled by this tool. To add your signature as a picture, use <tool_sign_pdf>Sign PDF</tool_sign_pdf>, and read our guide to <post_sign-a-pdf-without-printing>signing a PDF without printing</post_sign-a-pdf-without-printing> to understand what such a signature is and is not. If the filled form also needs a status stamp, <post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf>adding a watermark to a PDF</post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf> explains how. Sign after you have filled and flattened the form, so the order is clear.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s7.h': 'After filling',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s7.l1.1': 'Look at every page. Fields can run past the edge of a box, and checkboxes on later pages are easy to forget.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s7.l1.2': 'Compare the answers with your source documents, especially numbers and dates.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s7.l1.3': 'If the form must be sent with attachments, join them with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains the order of pages.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s7.l1.4': 'If the portal limits file size, use <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s7.l1.5': 'If the document contains personal data, consider protecting it with a password using <tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf> before you email it.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s7.p1': 'Forms that you receive as scans may also be worth running through <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf> to make them searchable, but you do not need to do that to type onto them.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s8.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s8.l1.1': 'Sending the editable version when the recipient needs a final one, or the other way round.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s8.l1.2': 'Typing into a flat scan and assuming the text can be edited later. It cannot.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s8.l1.3': 'Forgetting required fields, marked with an asterisk.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s8.l1.4': 'Missing a checkbox on a later page.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s8.l1.5': 'Not keeping an editable copy for corrections.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s9.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s9.o1.1': 'Add the form and look at the field count.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s9.o1.2': 'Fill every required field and check the optional ones.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s9.o1.3': 'For a flat form, click and type where each answer goes.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s9.o1.4': 'Flatten the copy you send, keep the editable copy.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.s9.o1.5': 'Open the result and read it from the first page to the last.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.1.q': 'My PDF shows no fields. Why?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.1.a': 'The PDF has no real form fields, for example a scan or a form that is only a picture. You can still click the page where text should go, type it and save a copy with the text written on. Forms made with XFA cannot have their fields filled.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.2.q': 'What does flattening do?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.2.a': 'Flattening burns your answers into the page and removes the form fields, so the answers can no longer be edited. Use it for the copy you send and keep an editable copy for yourself.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.3.q': 'Can I fill in a signature field?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.3.a': 'Not with this tool. Use Sign PDF to place a picture of your signature on the page.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.4.q': 'Can I use non-Latin characters in the fields?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.4.a': 'The form font covers Latin letters, digits and common symbols only. If a character cannot be written, the tool tells you.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.5.q': 'Can I edit the text I typed onto a flat PDF later?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.5.a': 'No. It is written into the page. Keep the original file so you can start again.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.6.q': 'Is my form uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.faq.6.a': 'No. The tool runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-fill-out-a-pdf-form.cta.p': 'Open <tool_fill_pdf_forms>Fill PDF Forms</tool_fill_pdf_forms>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.title': 'How to Format and Validate JSON: A Practical Guide',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.description': 'Learn the rules of valid JSON and the mistakes that break it, then format, validate and minify JSON in your browser with exact line and column errors.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.excerpt': 'Fix broken JSON quickly. See the syntax rules, the most common errors and how to pretty-print, validate and minify JSON.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.imageAlt': 'A block of curly-brace JSON code being aligned into neat indented lines on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.intro.1': 'An API returns one long line of brackets and quotes. A config file will not load and the error says only “unexpected token”. A colleague pastes a JSON snippet into a chat and it has lost half its structure. JSON is simple, but it is strict, and a single stray character breaks the whole document.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.intro.2': 'This guide covers the rules that matter, the mistakes behind most errors, and a quick workflow for formatting, checking and shrinking JSON in your browser, with nothing uploaded to a server.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.sum.1': 'JSON allows only double-quoted strings and keys, no trailing commas and no comments.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.sum.2': 'A validator tells you the exact line and column of the first error, which is usually enough to fix it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.sum.3': 'Format (pretty-print) JSON to read it and minify it to send it; neither changes the data.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.sum.4': 'Numbers larger than 2 to the power of 53 lose precision when a browser parses them.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s1.h': 'What JSON is',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s1.p1': 'JSON, short for JavaScript Object Notation, is a plain-text format for structured data. It has six kinds of value: objects in curly braces, arrays in square brackets, strings, numbers, true or false, and null. Objects hold key and value pairs; arrays hold ordered lists.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s1.p2': 'Because it is plain text, it works everywhere: web APIs, configuration files, app settings, exports from databases and tools. That portability is why everyone uses it, and why a small mistake in a hand-edited file causes so much trouble.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.h': 'The rules that cause most errors',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.p1': 'JSON looks like JavaScript, but its rules are stricter. Almost every “invalid JSON” message traces back to one of these:',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.l1.1': 'Keys and strings must use double quotes. Single quotes and unquoted keys are not valid.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.l1.2': 'No trailing commas. A comma after the last item of an object or array breaks the document.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.l1.3': 'No comments. JSON has no comment syntax, so lines starting with // or blocks in slashes and asterisks are errors.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.l1.4': 'Values must be one of the six types. undefined, NaN and Infinity are not valid JSON values.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.l1.5': 'Strings must escape special characters. A quote inside a string needs a backslash, and a raw line break inside a string is not allowed; write it as \\n.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s2.l1.6': 'Brackets and braces must match. Every opening one needs its closing partner, in the right order.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s3.h': 'Validate: find the exact problem',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s3.p1': '<tool_json_validator>JSON Validator</tool_json_validator> checks whether your text is valid JSON and, if it is not, points to the exact line and column of the error. Paste the document, read the message and fix it, then check again.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s3.p2': 'Two tips make this faster:',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s3.l1.1': 'The reported position is where the parser noticed the problem, which can be just after the real mistake. If the line looks fine, check the line above, especially for a missing comma or a missing closing quote.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s3.l1.2': 'Fix one error at a time. A single missing bracket can produce confusing messages further down, and most of them disappear once the first is fixed.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s3.p3': 'The validator checks syntax only. It tells you whether the JSON is well-formed, not whether it has the fields your application expects. Checking against a JSON Schema is not included.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s4.h': 'Format: make it readable',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s4.p1': '<tool_json_formatter>JSON Formatter</tool_json_formatter> adds line breaks and indentation so that nested structures become easy to scan. You can choose the indentation and sort the keys alphabetically.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s4.o1.1': 'Paste your JSON.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s4.o1.2': 'Choose the indentation, for example two spaces, and whether to sort keys.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s4.o1.3': 'Copy the formatted result or download it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s4.p2': 'Sorting keys is useful when you want to compare two documents, because the same data then appears in the same order. It changes only the order of keys in objects, not the order of array items, and it does not change any values. If you want to see exactly what differs between two versions, paste both into <tool_text_diff_checker>Text Diff Checker</tool_text_diff_checker> after formatting them the same way.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s5.h': 'Minify: make it small',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s5.p1': '<tool_json_minifier>JSON Minifier</tool_json_minifier> does the opposite of formatting. It removes all whitespace that is not needed, which makes the text smaller for sending over a network or storing in a field. The data stays exactly the same, so you can format it again at any time.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s5.p2': 'Use formatted JSON for reading and editing, and minified JSON for sending and storing. Do not try to read minified output by eye; format it first.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s6.h': 'Large numbers and other surprises',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s6.p1': 'JavaScript, and therefore the browser, stores numbers as 64-bit floating-point values. Integers larger than 2 to the power of 53, about 9 quadrillion, cannot all be represented exactly. If your JSON contains such an id or a very long number, parsing and re-printing it can change the last digits. When ids are that long, treat them as strings in the JSON.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s6.p2': 'Other things to watch for:',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s6.l1.1': 'Duplicate keys in the same object are allowed by the syntax but unreliable, because programs differ in which value they keep.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s6.l1.2': 'Dates have no special type in JSON. They are usually strings in a standard format, so their meaning depends on the agreement between sender and receiver.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s6.l1.3': 'Special characters and non-Latin text are fine. JSON is Unicode text, and the tools handle it correctly.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s7.h': 'A quick workflow for broken JSON',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s7.o1.1': 'Paste it into the JSON Validator and read the message.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s7.o1.2': 'Fix the first error, usually a quote, a comma or a bracket.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s7.o1.3': 'Repeat until the validator reports valid JSON.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s7.o1.4': 'Format it so you can read the structure.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s7.o1.5': 'Minify it again if you need the compact version.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s8.h': 'Related tasks',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s8.p1': 'JSON often travels inside other text. Query-string values and URLs need percent-encoding, which <tool_url_encoder_decoder>URL Encoder / Decoder</tool_url_encoder_decoder> handles, and binary or awkward text is often wrapped in Base64. Our guide to <post_what-is-base64-encoding>what Base64 encoding is</post_what-is-base64-encoding> explains when and why, and <tool_base64_encoder_decoder>Base64 Encoder / Decoder</tool_base64_encoder_decoder> converts it in your browser.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s9.h': 'Embedded pictures make JSON heavy',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s9.p1': 'Some JSON files carry images as Base64 text. Because Base64 is about a third larger than the original data, a few large pictures can make a file huge. Shrink the pictures before encoding them; our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> shows how.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s10.h': 'Privacy',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.s10.p1': 'All three JSON tools run in your browser. What you paste is parsed on your device and is not sent to a server. Even so, avoid pasting live credentials, tokens or personal data into any tool you do not control, and remove them from samples you share.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.1.q': 'Why is my JSON invalid when it looks fine?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.1.a': 'The usual causes are single quotes instead of double quotes, a trailing comma, a comment, or an unquoted key. The validator shows the exact line and column of the first error.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.2.q': 'Does formatting JSON change the data?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.2.a': 'No. Formatting only changes whitespace, and sorting changes the order of keys in objects. Values and array order stay the same.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.3.q': 'Can JSON have comments?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.3.a': 'No. Standard JSON has no comment syntax. If a file with comments is accepted by some program, that program supports an extended format, not JSON itself.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.4.q': 'What is the difference between formatting and minifying?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.4.a': 'Formatting adds indentation and line breaks for readability. Minifying removes unneeded whitespace to make the text smaller. The content is identical.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.5.q': 'Is my JSON uploaded when I use these tools?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.faq.5.a': 'No. The tools run in your browser, so the text stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.cta.h': 'Format your JSON now',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-json.cta.p': 'Open <tool_json_formatter>JSON Formatter</tool_json_formatter>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.title': 'How to Format and Validate XML and Find Errors Quickly',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.description': 'Pretty print or minify XML, find unclosed tags and mismatched elements with line and column numbers, and learn what well-formed XML means and what it does not.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.excerpt': 'A practical guide to XML: what well-formed means, how to format and minify it, how to find nesting errors, how escaping works and why schema validation is a separate step.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.imageAlt': 'Nested XML tags being indented and checked on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.intro.1': 'XML is a text format for structured data. It is the format behind website sitemaps, RSS feeds, many configuration files, vector graphics and the inside of Word, Excel and PowerPoint files. When it is generated by a program it is usually squeezed onto one long line, and when it is typed by a person a single missing tag can break everything.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.intro.2': 'A formatter fixes both problems. It indents the data so you can read it, and it checks that the structure is sound. This guide explains what well-formed XML is, how to format and minify it, how to find the exact place of an error and what a formatter cannot check.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.sum.1': 'XML is well-formed when every tag is closed, tags are nested correctly and special characters are escaped.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.sum.2': 'Formatting adds indentation so you can read the structure, and minifying removes the extra whitespace to save space.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.sum.3': 'Error messages give the line and column where the problem was found, which is the quickest way to fix it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.sum.4': 'A formatter checks structure only. It does not validate against a DTD or an XSD schema.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.sum.5': 'Escape the ampersand and the angle brackets inside text, or the document breaks.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s1.h': 'What XML looks like',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s1.p1': 'XML marks up data with tags, written in pairs. An opening tag starts an element, a closing tag ends it and the text or other elements between them are its content. Elements can contain other elements, which creates a tree: a document has one root element, which holds children, which hold their own children.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s1.p2': 'Elements can carry attributes, which are small named values inside the opening tag. An element with no content can be written as a single self-closing tag. The very first line of many files is a declaration that states the version and the character encoding, usually UTF-8.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s1.p3': 'The structure is strict. Unlike HTML, where browsers forgive many mistakes, an XML program stops at the first error, which is why checking matters.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.h': 'Where you meet XML',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.p1': 'XML is more common than it seems.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.l1.1': 'Sitemaps that tell search engines which pages exist.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.l1.2': 'RSS and Atom feeds.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.l1.3': 'Configuration files for build tools and applications.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.l1.4': 'SVG pictures, which are XML. Our guide to <post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png>converting SVG to PNG</post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png> shows what to do with them.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.l1.5': 'Word, Excel and PowerPoint documents, which are zip files full of XML parts.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.l1.6': 'Data exchange between older systems and services.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s2.p2': 'You often receive XML you did not write. A formatter helps you read it before you decide what to do with it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.h': 'What well-formed means',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.p1': 'A document is well-formed when it follows the basic rules of the language.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.o1.1': 'There is exactly one root element.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.o1.2': 'Every opening tag has a matching closing tag, or is self-closing.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.o1.3': 'Tags are nested properly, so the element that opened last closes first.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.o1.4': 'Attribute values are in quotes.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.o1.5': 'Special characters inside text are escaped.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.o1.6': 'Names are spelled the same way in the opening and the closing tag, including capital letters, because XML is case sensitive.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s3.p2': 'If any rule is broken, the document is not XML, and programs will refuse to read it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s4.h': 'How to format XML',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s4.p1': '<tool_xml_formatter>XML Formatter</tool_xml_formatter> runs inside your browser tab, so your data stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s4.o1.1': 'Paste your XML into the box.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s4.o1.2': 'Choose Format or Minify, and the indentation you prefer.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s4.o1.3': 'Read the result and copy it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s4.p2': 'Formatting puts each element on its own line and indents children under their parents, so the shape of the document becomes obvious. Minifying does the opposite: it removes the whitespace between tags to make the text as short as possible, which is useful when you must send or store a large amount of XML.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s4.p3': 'Neither changes the data. Only the whitespace between elements changes.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.h': 'Finding errors',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.p1': 'When the structure is broken, the tool tells you where. It checks the nesting of tags, unclosed tags and comments or CDATA sections that are never closed, and it reports the line and column of the problem together with what it expected.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.p2': 'The most common errors are easy to recognise.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.l1.1': 'A closing tag that does not match the opening tag, which is often a typo or a wrong capital letter.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.l1.2': 'A tag that was opened and never closed, usually found at the end of the document.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.l1.3': 'A tag that closes too early because two elements overlap.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.l1.4': 'An ampersand that was not escaped, for example in a company name or a link.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.l1.5': 'A comment that was started and never finished.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s5.p3': 'Fix the first error and check again. One mistake often causes a chain of messages, and fixing the first one removes the rest.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s6.h': 'Escaping special characters',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s6.p1': 'Some characters have a meaning in XML and cannot appear as plain text inside content. The five to remember are the ampersand, the two angle brackets and the two kinds of quotation mark.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s6.p2': 'They are written as entities: the ampersand as &amp;, the less-than sign as &lt;, the greater-than sign as &gt;, the double quote as &quot; and the apostrophe as &apos;. If a company is called Smith and Sons and you write an ampersand in the name, the document breaks until you replace it with the entity.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s6.p3': '<tool_html_encoder_decoder>HTML Encoder / Decoder</tool_html_encoder_decoder> converts text to and from these entities, and it works for XML as well because the entities are the same for the basic characters. If your text contains an address with special characters, <tool_url_encoder_decoder>URL Encoder / Decoder</tool_url_encoder_decoder> shows how to write it safely inside a link. Our guide to <post_what-is-base64-encoding>Base64 encoding</post_what-is-base64-encoding> explains another way to carry data that does not fit into plain text.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s7.h': 'What a formatter cannot check',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s7.p1': 'A formatter checks that XML is well-formed. It does not check that the content makes sense.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s7.l1.1': 'It does not validate against a DTD or an XSD schema, which are the documents that define which elements and attributes are allowed and in which order.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s7.l1.2': 'It cannot tell you that a date is wrong, that a number is out of range or that a required element is missing.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s7.p2': 'A document can be well-formed and still be rejected by the system that receives it, because it does not follow that system\'s schema. For that, you need a validator that knows the schema. Use the formatter first to make sure the structure is sound, and then run schema validation where the schema lives.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s8.h': 'XML and JSON',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s8.p1': 'JSON has taken over many jobs that XML once did, especially in web services, because it is shorter and easier to handle in code. XML is still the better choice for documents with mixed text and markup, and for the many standards that are built on it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s8.p2': 'If you work with both, <tool_json_formatter>JSON Formatter</tool_json_formatter> and <tool_json_validator>JSON Validator</tool_json_validator> do for JSON what the XML formatter does here. Our guide to <post_how-to-format-and-validate-json>formatting and validating JSON</post_how-to-format-and-validate-json> explains the JSON side. For colours and other values inside either format, our guide to <post_hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained>colour codes</post_hex-rgb-and-hsl-colors-explained> may help.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s9.h': 'Comparing two versions',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s9.p1': 'When a configuration file or feed changes, the quickest way to see what is different is to format both versions the same way and compare them. <tool_text_diff_checker>Text Diff Checker</tool_text_diff_checker> shows added and removed lines. Formatting first matters, because a minified file is one line, and a comparison of two long lines tells you almost nothing.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s10.l1.1': 'Forgetting to escape an ampersand in text or in a link.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s10.l1.2': 'Mixing capital and small letters in opening and closing tags.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s10.l1.3': 'Leaving a tag open when copying only part of a document.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s10.l1.4': 'Assuming that a well-formed file is also valid for the receiving system.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s10.l1.5': 'Comparing minified files instead of formatting them first.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s11.o1.1': 'Paste the XML and format it.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s11.o1.2': 'Read the first error, fix it and check again.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s11.o1.3': 'Escape special characters in text.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s11.o1.4': 'Remember that schema validation is a separate step.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.s11.o1.5': 'Format both versions before you compare them.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.1.q': 'How thoroughly is the XML validated?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.1.a': 'The tool checks tag nesting, unclosed tags and unterminated comments or CDATA. It does not validate against a DTD or XSD schema.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.2.q': 'What is the difference between format and minify?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.2.a': 'Format adds indentation and line breaks so the structure is easy to read. Minify removes the extra whitespace to make the text as short as possible.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.3.q': 'Why is my XML rejected even though the formatter accepts it?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.3.a': 'The formatter checks that the XML is well-formed. The receiving system may also require that it follows a schema.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.4.q': 'How do I use an ampersand in XML text?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.4.a': 'Write it as the entity &amp; so the document stays well-formed.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.5.q': 'Does formatting change my data?',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.faq.5.a': 'No. Only the whitespace between elements changes.',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-format-and-validate-xml.cta.p': 'Open <tool_xml_formatter>XML Formatter</tool_xml_formatter>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.title': 'How to Generate a Strong Password and Actually Remember It',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.description': 'What makes a password strong, why length beats complexity, when to use random or name-based passwords and how to store them safely with a manager.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.excerpt': 'Learn how password strength is measured, how long a password should be, the difference between fully random and memorable passwords and how to keep them safe.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.imageAlt': 'A key and a long password field with dots on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.intro.1': 'Most account breaches do not involve clever hacking. They involve a password that was easy to guess, reused on several sites, or leaked from one site and tried on the others. Choosing a good password is the cheapest security measure there is, and yet it is where most of us take shortcuts, because strong passwords are hard to remember.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.intro.2': 'This guide explains how password strength works, how long a password should be, when a memorable password is acceptable and when only a fully random one will do, and how to store passwords so you never have to remember them all.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.sum.1': 'Length matters more than complexity. Aim for at least 16 characters for important accounts.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.sum.2': 'A password is only as strong as the number of possibilities an attacker would have to try.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.sum.3': 'Use fully random passwords for email, banking and your password manager; name-based passwords are for low-risk accounts.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.sum.4': 'Never reuse a password. One leak should not open every door.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.sum.5': 'Store passwords in a password manager, protected by one very strong password.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s1.h': 'How strength is measured',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s1.p1': 'An attacker who does not know your password has to guess it. Strength is the number of guesses required, and it is usually expressed in bits. Each bit doubles the number of possibilities. A password with 40 bits of strength needs about a trillion guesses on average, while one with 80 bits needs a number so large that guessing is unrealistic with current technology.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s1.p2': 'Strength depends on two things: how many characters the password has, and how many different symbols each character could be. A random password of 16 characters drawn from letters, digits and symbols has far more possibilities than a short one with the same alphabet, which is why length wins.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s1.p3': 'Computers make guesses at enormous speed, especially when a leaked list of hashed passwords is attacked offline. A password that survives a casual human guess may still fall in minutes to an automated attack.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s2.h': 'Complexity rules are not the point',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s2.p1': 'Websites love rules: one capital, one digit, one symbol. People satisfy them with predictable patterns, such as a capital letter at the start, a number at the end and an exclamation mark. Attackers know these patterns, so Password1! is weak despite meeting every rule.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s2.p2': 'What helps is unpredictability and length. A string of four random words is stronger than a short jumble of symbols, and a long random string is stronger still. This is why the guidance from security agencies has shifted from complexity towards length and unique passwords.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s3.h': 'Two styles of generated password',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s3.p1': '<tool_password_generator>Password Generator</tool_password_generator> offers two styles, because security and memorability pull in different directions.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s3.l1.1': 'Fully random: the maximum strength for its length. You cannot remember it, and that is fine, because a password manager will.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s3.l1.2': 'Name plus word: a more memorable pattern built on names and words, with digits and a symbol. It is easier to type and recall, but weaker, because an attacker who knows how such passwords are built can start from lists of names.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s3.p2': 'The tool shows the estimated strength in bits, so you can compare. The estimate is based on the number of possible combinations, assuming an attacker who knows how the password was built. It does not use breach databases.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s3.p3': 'Use name-based passwords for low-risk accounts, such as a forum you rarely visit. For email, banking and the master password of your password manager, use fully random ones. Email is especially important: whoever controls your email can reset almost every other password.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s4.h': 'How to generate a password in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s4.p1': 'The tool runs inside your browser tab, so the password is created on your device and not sent anywhere.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s4.o1.1': 'Open the tool and choose a style.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s4.o1.2': 'Set the length, how many passwords you need and which character types to include.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s4.o1.3': 'Generate and look at the strength shown.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s4.o1.4': 'Copy a password and save it in a password manager.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s4.p2': 'Generated passwords use only four symbols, the at sign, the hash, the dollar sign and the asterisk. They are accepted by almost every website and easy to type on any keyboard. The limited alphabet slightly reduces the strength per character, so a few extra characters of length more than make up for it.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s5.h': 'How long should a password be?',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s5.l1.1': 'At least 16 characters for important accounts: email, banking, cloud storage, your password manager.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s5.l1.2': '12 to 14 characters for ordinary accounts, if the site limits length.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s5.l1.3': 'As long as the site allows for anything that protects money or identity.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s5.p1': 'If a website refuses long passwords, it is a sign of poor security practice on their side; use the longest it allows and turn on two-factor authentication.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.h': 'Keeping passwords safe',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.p1': 'A strong password is useless if it is stored badly. Good habits:',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.l1.1': 'Use a password manager. It generates, stores and fills passwords, so you only remember one master password. The master password should be long and unique; a passphrase of several random words is a good approach.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.l1.2': 'Never reuse a password. If a site is breached, attackers try the same email and password on other sites.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.l1.3': 'Turn on two-factor authentication wherever it is offered, especially for email.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.l1.4': 'Do not email passwords to yourself or keep them in a notes file on your desktop.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.l1.5': 'Change a password when a service reports a breach.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s6.l1.6': 'Do not share a password by chat. Use a sharing feature of the password manager, or a temporary password that the person changes at once.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s7.h': 'Passwords for files',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s7.p1': 'When you protect a file, the same rules apply, and the consequences are worse because there is often no reset. If you encrypt a PDF with <tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf>, the file’s strength depends on your password, and if you lose it the file cannot be opened. Our guide to <post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf>protecting and unlocking a PDF</post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf> explains what encryption does and does not enforce. Store the password in your manager at the moment you create it.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s8.h': 'Other things that look like passwords',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s8.p1': 'Not every secret is a password. For identifiers that must be unique but not secret, <tool_uuid_generator>UUID Generator</tool_uuid_generator> makes random identifiers. For a Wi-Fi guest network, generate a strong password and put it into a code with <tool_qr_code_generator>QR Code Generator</tool_qr_code_generator>; our guide to <post_how-to-create-a-qr-code>creating a QR code</post_how-to-create-a-qr-code> explains how and warns about printing secrets in public. Encoding a password with Base64 does not protect it; see <post_what-is-base64-encoding>what is Base64 encoding</post_what-is-base64-encoding> for why encoding is not encryption.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s9.l1.1': 'Reusing one password across sites.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s9.l1.2': 'Choosing a pattern of capital, word, digit and symbol and believing it is strong.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s9.l1.3': 'Using personal details such as birthdays, pet names or phone numbers.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s9.l1.4': 'Storing passwords in a plain text file or in a browser without a master password.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s9.l1.5': 'Forgetting two-factor authentication on the email account.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s10.o1.1': 'Choose fully random passwords for important accounts.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s10.o1.2': 'Make them at least 16 characters long.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s10.o1.3': 'Save each one in a password manager the moment you create it.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s10.o1.4': 'Use a unique password for every account.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.s10.o1.5': 'Turn on two-factor authentication for email and money.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.1.q': 'Is a password like Tesla2026#Tech safe?',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.1.a': 'It is better than a plain word, but weaker than random text. Anyone guessing can start from lists of well-known names, so the real strength comes from the number of possibilities, shown as bits. Use name-based passwords for low-risk accounts and fully random ones for email, banking and password managers.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.2.q': 'Why does the generator use only a few symbols?',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.2.a': 'Generated passwords use only the four symbols @ # $ * because they are accepted by almost every website and are easy to type on any keyboard.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.3.q': 'How many characters should a password have?',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.3.a': 'At least 16 characters for important accounts. Length matters more than complexity.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.4.q': 'Are the passwords stored or sent anywhere?',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.4.a': 'No. They are generated inside your browser and are not stored or sent by the tool. Save the one you use in a password manager.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.5.q': 'Does the strength estimate use breach databases?',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.5.a': 'No. It is based on the number of possible combinations, not on lists of leaked passwords.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.6.q': 'Should I change my passwords regularly?',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.faq.6.a': 'Change a password when you have reason to think it was exposed, for example after a breach notice. Regular changes of strong, unique passwords add little.',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-generate-a-strong-password.cta.p': 'Open <tool_password_generator>Password Generator</tool_password_generator>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.title': 'How to Make an Animated GIF From Images and Keep It Small',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.description': 'Turn a set of pictures into an animated GIF in your browser, set the timing, size and loop, keep transparency and shrink a GIF that came out too large.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.excerpt': 'A hands-on guide to making animated GIFs from photos and screenshots: frame order and timing, size and colours, transparency, looping and the best ways to reduce the file size.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.imageAlt': 'A stack of pictures turning into an animated GIF on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.intro.1': 'An animated GIF is a small silent movie that plays anywhere: in a chat, an email, a help article or a web page. It needs no player and no sound, which makes it perfect for showing a short process, a before and after, a product from several angles or a simple joke.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.intro.2': 'You do not need video software to make one. This guide shows how to build a GIF from a set of pictures in your browser, how to choose timing, size and looping, and how to keep the file small enough to send.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.sum.1': 'A GIF is a sequence of pictures, called frames, shown one after another with a delay between them.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.sum.2': 'Fewer frames, a smaller width and fewer colours all make the file smaller.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.sum.3': 'GIFs are limited to 256 colours per frame, so photos can look grainy.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.sum.4': 'Transparent areas can be kept, but GIF transparency is on or off for each pixel, so soft edges become hard.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.sum.5': 'Animated pictures that you add contribute only their first frame.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s1.h': 'What a GIF is, and when it is the right choice',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s1.p1': 'A GIF stores every frame as a picture and plays them in order. Because every frame is a full picture limited to 256 colours, GIFs are best for simple content: screenshots, diagrams, icons, logos and short sequences with flat colours.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s1.p2': 'They are less good for long clips and for photographs. A ten-second video clip turned into a GIF is usually much larger than the video and looks worse. For long or detailed motion, a video file is the better choice. For a short, simple loop of three to twenty frames, a GIF is hard to beat because it works everywhere without any player.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s2.h': 'Plan your frames',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s2.p1': 'Good GIFs begin with good preparation.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s2.o1.1': 'Decide what the loop shows. A before and after needs two frames. A process needs one frame per step. A product turn needs six to twelve frames.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s2.o1.2': 'Make every frame the same size, or at least the same proportions. If frames differ, the animation jumps.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s2.o1.3': 'Keep the subject in the same place from frame to frame, so the eye follows the change and not the camera.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s2.o1.4': 'Name the files in order, such as step-01, step-02 and so on, which makes them easy to add in the right sequence.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s2.p2': 'If the pictures need small edits first, such as brightness, a crop or a text label, do them before you build the GIF. <tool_photo_editor>Photo Editor</tool_photo_editor> adjusts colours, rotates, crops and adds text, and our guide to <post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser>editing a photo in your browser</post_how-to-edit-a-photo-in-your-browser> walks through it. <tool_image_cropper>Crop Image</tool_image_cropper> gives every frame the same shape.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s3.h': 'How to make a GIF',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s3.p1': '<tool_gif_maker>GIF Maker</tool_gif_maker> runs inside your browser tab, so the pictures stay on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add two or more pictures. You can add a single picture if you only want a still GIF.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s3.o1.2': 'Drag the pictures into the order you want.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s3.o1.3': 'Set how long each frame is shown, and choose the size, the loop and the colours.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s3.o1.4': 'Create the GIF, watch the preview and download it.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s3.p2': 'The tool shows the size of the GIF as soon as it is made, so you can adjust and try again if it is too large.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s4.h': 'Timing and looping',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s4.p1': 'The delay decides how the animation feels. A short delay, such as a tenth of a second per frame, gives smooth motion if you have many frames. A long delay, such as one or two seconds, suits slides and step-by-step guides where people need time to read.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s4.p2': 'You can also set a different delay for a frame that deserves attention. Give the last frame of a before and after a longer pause, so the viewer sees the result before the loop restarts.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s4.p3': 'Looping decides whether the GIF repeats forever or plays a set number of times. Endless loops are the usual choice for decoration, and a limited loop suits instructions that should not distract once they are understood.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s5.h': 'Choosing the size',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s5.p1': 'Width is the biggest lever on file size, because the number of pixels in every frame multiplies by the number of frames.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s5.l1.1': 'For a chat or an email, a width of 480 to 600 pixels is usually plenty.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s5.l1.2': 'For a web page, use the width at which it is displayed.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s5.l1.3': 'Avoid very large GIFs. They are slow to load and heavy on phones.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s5.p2': 'If a picture is much larger than you need, make it smaller before you build the GIF. <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer> resizes many pictures at once, which also keeps the frames consistent. The tool allows up to 100 frames, and larger frames need more memory in your browser, so use fewer frames when the pictures are large.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s6.h': 'Colours and transparency',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s6.p1': 'A GIF frame has at most 256 colours. Flat graphics fit easily, and photographs show banding, which looks like grainy steps in gradients. Reducing the number of colours makes the file smaller, and with graphics you often see no difference.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s6.p2': 'If your pictures have transparent areas, such as a PNG logo, turn on the option to keep them. Be aware that GIF transparency is on or off for each pixel. A soft shadow or a smooth edge cannot be partly transparent, so soft edges become hard and can look jagged on some backgrounds.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s6.p3': 'Animated pictures that you add, such as other GIFs or animated WebP files, contribute only their first frame. To reuse a frame from another animation, export it as a still picture first.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.h': 'Making a GIF smaller',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.p1': 'When the result is too large, try these in order. They are listed from the most effective to the least.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.o1.1': 'Reduce the width.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.o1.2': 'Use fewer frames. Remove frames that add little to the story.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.o1.3': 'Reduce the number of colours.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.o1.4': 'Crop away parts of the picture that do not change.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.o1.5': 'Shorten the loop.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s7.p2': 'The tool warns when a GIF is above about 10 MB, which is too large for most chats and emails. A good target for sharing is under 2 or 3 MB. Our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> explains the ideas of size and quality for still pictures, and most of them apply to frames as well.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s8.h': 'Using drawings and vector art',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s8.p1': 'Logos, icons and diagrams often start as SVG files. Export each state as a PNG at the size you need, then build the GIF from those pictures. Our guide to <post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png>converting SVG to PNG</post_how-to-convert-svg-to-png> explains how to choose the size and keep transparency, and flat vector art is exactly the kind of content GIF handles well.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s8.p2': 'If you want the pictures to travel as a document instead of a loop, <tool_images_to_pdf>Images to PDF</tool_images_to_pdf> turns them into pages of one PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s9.h': 'Accessibility and good manners',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s9.p1': 'Animation can be distracting. Keep loops short and calm, avoid fast flashing and do not put essential information only in a GIF. Add a text description wherever the GIF appears, so that people who cannot see it, or who have motion switched off, still understand what it shows.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s10.l1.1': 'Using frames of different sizes, which makes the animation jump.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s10.l1.2': 'Using a photo sequence with large pictures and wondering why the file is huge.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s10.l1.3': 'Leaving the delay so short that people cannot read the frames.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s10.l1.4': 'Forgetting that soft transparent edges turn hard in a GIF.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s10.l1.5': 'Making a long clip into a GIF when a video would be smaller and sharper.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s11.o1.1': 'Plan three to twenty frames that tell one clear story.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s11.o1.2': 'Make the frames the same size and edit them first.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s11.o1.3': 'Set the order, the delay and the loop.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s11.o1.4': 'Pick a width suited to where the GIF will be used.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s11.o1.5': 'Check the file size, and reduce width, frames or colours if needed.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.s11.o1.6': 'Add a text description where you publish it.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.1.q': 'Why is my GIF so large?',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.1.a': 'GIFs store every frame as a picture limited to 256 colours. Fewer frames, a smaller width and fewer colours all shrink the file. The tool shows the size as soon as the GIF is made.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.2.q': 'Can I keep transparent areas?',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.2.a': 'Yes, turn on keep transparent areas for PNG or WebP pictures with transparency. GIF transparency is on or off per pixel, so soft edges become hard.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.3.q': 'How many pictures can I use?',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.3.a': 'Up to 100 frames. The larger the frames, the more memory your browser needs.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.4.q': 'Can I add an animated GIF as a frame?',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.4.a': 'Animated inputs such as GIF and WebP contribute only their first frame.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.5.q': 'Can I make a GIF from one picture?',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.faq.5.a': 'Yes. A single picture gives a still GIF.',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-make-a-gif-from-images.cta.p': 'Open <tool_gif_maker>GIF Maker</tool_gif_maker>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.title': 'How to Merge PDF Files Online: Order, Size and Limits',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.description': 'Combine several PDFs into one document in the right order, keep the size under control and avoid common problems, all in your browser with no upload.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.excerpt': 'Join contracts, invoices and scans into one PDF in the order you choose, and learn what merging keeps, drops and how to keep the result small.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.imageAlt': 'Three separate PDF pages sliding together into one combined document on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.intro.1': 'Applications, tax returns, invoices, project reports: sooner or later somebody asks you for “everything in one PDF”. You have the cover letter in one file, the form in another, a scan of an ID in a third, and no wish to send three attachments that might be opened in the wrong order.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.intro.2': 'Merging fixes that. This guide shows how to combine PDFs in your browser, how to get the order right the first time, what merging does not carry over, and how to keep the final file small enough to send.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.sum.1': 'Merge PDF joins two or more PDFs into one document, in the order you set with the arrows.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.sum.2': 'The files are combined on your own device and are not uploaded.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.sum.3': 'Page content and links are kept; bookmarks and form fields of the source files are not.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.sum.4': 'Password-protected PDFs must be unlocked first, and the merged file is best compressed once at the end.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s1.h': 'When merging is the right move',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s1.p1': 'Merge when the recipient needs a single document to read, print or archive: a job application pack, a set of supporting documents for a visa or loan, the monthly invoices for your accountant, or chapters of a report written by different people. A single PDF opens in one window, prints in one job and cannot lose a page on the way.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s1.p2': 'Do not merge when the files serve different purposes or will be edited separately. If one part of the pack changes often, keep it as its own file and merge only at the moment you send it.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s2.h': 'How to merge PDFs in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s2.p1': '<tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf> runs inside your browser tab. Your documents are read from your device, joined locally and offered back as a download.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDFs. You can choose several files at once or drag them onto the page. Each file can be up to 100 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s2.o1.2': 'Put the files in the order you want. Use the arrows to move a file up or down. The list is the order of the final document.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s2.o1.3': 'Press Merge and wait a moment. Larger files take longer because your own device does the work.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s2.o1.4': 'Download the combined PDF and open it before you send it.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s3.h': 'Getting the order right',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s3.p1': 'The most common mistake is not the merge itself but the order. The files are joined exactly as listed, so check the list from top to bottom before you press the button. A few habits help:',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s3.l1.1': 'Name your files so they sort naturally, for example 01-cover-letter, 02-form, 03-id-scan. When the names already carry the order, the list is right from the start.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s3.l1.2': 'Put the page that explains the pack first. A short cover page or summary makes a long pack much easier to read.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s3.l1.3': 'Check the page count afterwards. If three files of 2, 4 and 3 pages gave you 9 pages, the merge is complete.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s3.p2': 'If you need to change the order of individual pages after merging, <tool_reorder_pdf_pages>Reorder PDF Pages</tool_reorder_pdf_pages> shows thumbnails you can drag, and <tool_remove_pdf_pages>Remove PDF Pages</tool_remove_pdf_pages> deletes pages you do not need.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s4.h': 'What merging keeps and what it does not',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s4.p1': 'Merging copies the visible content of every page: text, images, vector graphics and links. The merged file is a normal PDF that opens in any reader.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s4.p2': 'Some document-level features do not carry over from the source files:',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s4.l1.1': 'Bookmarks and outlines of the individual files are not merged. The combined file has no table of contents unless you build one elsewhere.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s4.l1.2': 'Form fields are not merged, so a fillable form will be flattened into what it looks like. If a form must stay fillable, complete it before merging or send it as its own file.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s4.l1.3': 'Password-protected PDFs are not supported. If one of your files asks for a password, remove it first with <tool_unlock_pdf>Unlock PDF</tool_unlock_pdf>, which works when you know the password.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s4.n1': 'Tip: merge a copy, not your only version. Keep the originals in a folder so you can rebuild the pack if a page turns out to be missing or in the wrong place.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s5.h': 'Keep the merged file a sensible size',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s5.p1': 'A merged PDF is as large as the sum of its parts, and attachment limits are set by the receiving system. Many email services accept attachments of roughly 20 to 25 MB in total, and online portals often allow much less.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s5.p2': 'If the result is too large, reduce it in this order:',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s5.o1.1': 'Remove pages nobody needs, such as blank pages and duplicates.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s5.o1.2': 'Compress the merged file once with <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>. Our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains the strengths and what to expect.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s5.o1.3': 'Compress before you merge only if a single source file is huge, for example a scan with high-resolution photos.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s5.p3': 'Compressing the combined file once is better than compressing every part, because each round of recompression costs a little image quality.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s6.h': 'Add page numbers after merging',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s6.p1': 'Each source file starts at page 1 on its own, so a merged pack can have several “page 1” labels printed on its pages. If readers will refer to pages by number, number the whole document after merging with <tool_add_page_numbers>Add Page Numbers</tool_add_page_numbers>, so the numbers run from the first page to the last.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s7.h': 'Merging scanned documents',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s7.p1': 'Scans merge exactly like any other PDF, but remember two things. They are heavy, so check the size afterwards. And they contain pictures of text, not text, so the merged file will not be searchable. If you need to search it, add a text layer with OCR PDF; our guide to <post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>making a scanned PDF searchable</post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable> shows how. If your starting point is a pile of photos, <post_convert-images-to-pdf>convert the images to PDF</post_convert-images-to-pdf> first and then merge the result with the rest.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s8.h': 'Merging and splitting go together',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s8.p1': 'Merging is half of a pair. If a pack arrives as one large file and you only need parts of it, our guide to <post_how-to-split-a-pdf>splitting a PDF</post_how-to-split-a-pdf> shows how to cut it by range, page or chunk. A common routine is to split a document, replace one part, and merge the parts again.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s9.h': 'Fixing problems',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s9.l1.1': 'A file will not add: it is probably password-protected, damaged or over 100 MB. Unlock it, split it, or repair it first; our guide to <post_repair-a-corrupted-pdf>repairing a corrupted PDF</post_repair-a-corrupted-pdf> explains what can be recovered.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s9.l1.2': 'The order is wrong after merging: go back, fix the list order and merge again from your originals. For a small fix, use Reorder PDF Pages.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s9.l1.3': 'The merged file looks larger than expected: that is normal. Compress it once at the end.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.s9.l1.4': 'A fillable form no longer fills: form fields are not carried over by merging. Complete the form first, then merge.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.1.q': 'Is it safe to merge confidential PDFs online?',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.1.a': 'Merge PDF runs in your browser, so the files are processed on your device and are not uploaded to a server. As with any sensitive document, avoid doing it on a shared or public computer.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.2.q': 'Is there a limit on how many files I can merge?',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.2.a': 'Each file can be up to 100 MB, and the tool limits how many files you can add at once to keep your browser responsive. For a very large pack, merge it in two stages.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.3.q': 'Will merging reduce the quality of my pages?',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.3.a': 'No. Pages are copied across without being recompressed, so text and images look the same. Quality only drops if you compress the merged file afterwards.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.4.q': 'Can I merge a PDF with images?',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.4.a': 'First turn the images into a PDF with Images to PDF, then merge that PDF with the others. That also lets you choose the page size and margins for the pictures.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.5.q': 'Can I undo a merge?',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.faq.5.a': 'Split PDF can separate the combined file again by page ranges, but it is easier to keep your original files and simply merge again if you need a different version.',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.cta.h': 'Merge your PDFs now',
  'blog.how-to-merge-pdf-files.cta.p': 'Open <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.title': 'How to Redact a PDF Properly So Hidden Text Is Really Gone',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.description': 'Black out names, numbers and areas in a PDF so the text underneath is destroyed, not just covered. Learn the right workflow and the checks to do.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.excerpt': 'Why a black box on top of text is not redaction, how page-to-image redaction removes the content for good and how to find and check every match before sharing.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.imageAlt': 'A PDF page with black redaction bars over lines of text on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.intro.1': 'Every year, organisations publish PDFs with black boxes over names and numbers, only for journalists to select the text under the boxes, copy it and read it. The boxes were just drawings on top of the page. The words were still there.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.intro.2': 'Real redaction destroys the covered content. This guide explains the difference between covering and removing, how to redact a PDF in your browser so the underlying text is gone, and which checks to run before a redacted file leaves your hands.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.sum.1': 'A black rectangle drawn on top of text is not redaction: the text stays selectable underneath.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.sum.2': 'Real redaction removes the content. Kitwell rebuilds every page that has a redaction as a picture with the black boxes painted in.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.sum.3': 'Redacted pages lose selectable text, links and form fields, which is the price of destroying the content.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.sum.4': 'Automatic search finds text inside a single line; always review every page and add boxes by hand where needed.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.sum.5': 'Check the result by trying to select and search for the hidden words, and look at the document properties.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s1.h': 'Covering is not redacting',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s1.p1': 'Many tools offer a rectangle or highlighter in black. In the PDF file, the rectangle is an extra object placed above the text. The text object is still there, so:',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s1.l1.1': 'You can select the area and copy the words.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s1.l1.2': 'Search finds them.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s1.l1.3': 'A screen reader reads them aloud.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s1.l1.4': 'A PDF editor can move or delete the black box.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s1.p2': 'If the information is confidential, this is a data leak waiting to happen. Redaction in the strict sense means the content is removed from the file, so nothing remains to be recovered.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s2.h': 'How redaction works here',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s2.p1': '<tool_redact_pdf>Redact PDF</tool_redact_pdf> destroys content by changing what the page is. Every page that contains at least one redaction is rendered into a picture with the black boxes painted in, and the new file uses that picture instead of the original page. The text, vector objects and images underneath do not exist in the new file. Pages that you did not redact are copied unchanged, so they keep their selectable text.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s2.p2': 'The consequences are straightforward and important:',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s2.l1.1': 'Redacted pages cannot have their text selected, because they are pictures now.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s2.l1.2': 'Links and form fields on redacted pages stop working.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s2.l1.3': 'Document properties such as title and author are removed from the result unless you choose to keep them.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s2.p3': 'If you need searchable text again afterwards, run <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf>; our guide to <post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable>making a scanned PDF searchable</post_ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable> explains what to expect. The recognition only reads what is visible, so the redacted words stay black.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.h': 'How to redact a PDF step by step',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.p1': 'The tool runs in your browser tab, so the file is read from your device.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it has a password.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.o1.2': 'Choose a page and draw boxes over everything that must disappear.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.o1.3': 'Or search for words, email addresses and numbers to mark all matches automatically.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.o1.4': 'Review every page, and add boxes by hand wherever search missed something.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.o1.5': 'Apply the redactions and download the result.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s3.o1.6': 'Check the result before you share it.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s4.h': 'Drawing boxes and searching',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s4.p1': 'Drawing is the most reliable method because you see exactly what will be covered. It is the only method that works for scanned pages and for text that is part of a picture, because those have no selectable words to search.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s4.p2': 'Search is fast for long documents. Type a name, an email address or a number and mark all matches at once. It has limits you should understand:',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s4.l1.1': 'It works on selectable text only.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s4.l1.2': 'It finds matches that sit inside a single run of text. A phrase that the PDF has split into pieces can be missed.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s4.l1.3': 'It cannot read pictures of text.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s4.p3': 'So treat search as a first pass, not as a guarantee. Go through every page afterwards.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.h': 'What to redact',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.p1': 'Think about what could identify a person or reveal a secret, including things that are easy to overlook:',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.l1.1': 'Names, addresses, phone numbers and email addresses.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.l1.2': 'Account, identification and case numbers.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.l1.3': 'Signatures and handwritten notes.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.l1.4': 'Headers and footers that repeat a name on every page.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.l1.5': 'Small print, margin notes and watermarks that contain details.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.l1.6': 'Information visible in pictures, such as a name tag in a photograph.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s5.p2': 'If a number appears in several forms, for example with and without spaces, search for each form, or redact by hand.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s6.h': 'Checks before you share',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s6.p1': 'Never trust a redaction you have not tested:',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s6.o1.1': 'Open the new file and try to select text on the redacted area. Nothing should be selectable on redacted pages.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s6.o1.2': 'Use the viewer’s search function to look for a word you redacted. It must find nothing.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s6.o1.3': 'Scroll through every page, not only those you remember marking.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s6.o1.4': 'Check the document properties. If you kept them, make sure they do not contain the name you hid. <tool_edit_pdf_metadata>Edit PDF Metadata</tool_edit_pdf_metadata> can inspect and clear them, and <post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs>our guide to removing metadata</post_how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs> explains what files hide.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s6.o1.5': 'Compare the original and the redacted file with <tool_compare_pdf>Compare PDF</tool_compare_pdf> to confirm that only the intended places changed; <post_how-to-compare-two-pdf-files>our comparison guide</post_how-to-compare-two-pdf-files> explains how to read the result.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s7.h': 'Redaction versus other tools',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s7.l1.1': 'Cropping only hides content outside a box; it does not delete it. See <post_how-to-crop-pdf-pages>how to crop PDF pages</post_how-to-crop-pdf-pages>.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s7.l1.2': 'Watermarks are drawn on top of the page and can be removed; see <post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf>adding a watermark to a PDF</post_how-to-add-a-watermark-to-a-pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s7.l1.3': 'Passwords protect the whole file, not parts of it. <tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf> is useful for transport, but anybody with the password sees everything unredacted.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s7.p1': 'Use redaction when part of a document must never be seen, and passwords when the whole file must only be seen by the right person. Often you use both.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s8.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s8.l1.1': 'Relying on a black rectangle from a general PDF editor.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s8.l1.2': 'Trusting search to find every match.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s8.l1.3': 'Forgetting repeated headers, footers and metadata.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s8.l1.4': 'Not testing the result.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s8.l1.5': 'Overwriting the only copy. Always keep the original in a safe place.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s9.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s9.o1.1': 'Keep the original safe and work on a copy.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s9.o1.2': 'Mark everything: boxes first for scans, search for text.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s9.o1.3': 'Review every page.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s9.o1.4': 'Apply, then try to select and search for the hidden words.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.s9.o1.5': 'Check the document properties before sharing.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.1.q': 'Is the hidden text really removed?',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.1.a': 'Yes. Every page with a redaction is rebuilt as a picture with the black boxes painted in, so the text and objects underneath do not exist in the new file. Pages you did not redact are copied unchanged.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.2.q': 'Why can I not select text on redacted pages any more?',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.2.a': 'Because those pages are pictures now. That is how the underlying content is destroyed. Run OCR PDF afterwards if you need searchable text; the redacted words stay black.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.3.q': 'Does the search find every match automatically?',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.3.a': 'No. It marks matches that sit inside a single line of text. A phrase split into pieces, or text that is part of an image, may be missed, so review every page.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.4.q': 'What happens to links and form fields on redacted pages?',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.4.a': 'They stop working, because the pages become pictures.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.5.q': 'Are the document properties removed?',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.5.a': 'They are removed from the result unless you choose to keep them.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.6.q': 'Is my PDF uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.faq.6.a': 'No. The tool runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly.cta.p': 'Open <tool_redact_pdf>Redact PDF</tool_redact_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.title': 'How to Remove Metadata From Photos and PDFs Before Sharing',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.description': 'Photos and PDFs carry hidden details such as location, camera, author and dates. Learn what metadata is, what it can reveal and how to remove it in your browser.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.excerpt': 'Find out what hidden data your files carry, how to strip location and author details from photos and PDFs before you share them, and what metadata removal does not cover.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.imageAlt': 'A document and a photo with a tag being peeled off, on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.intro.1': 'Every file you create carries more than you see. A holiday photo knows which phone took it and where you were standing. A report knows who wrote it, in which program and on which dates. This hidden information is called metadata, and most of the time it is harmless. Sometimes it is not: a photo posted online can reveal a home address, and a PDF sent to a client can show the name of a colleague or the program that produced it.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.intro.2': 'This guide explains what metadata is, what it can reveal, how to remove it from photos and PDFs in your browser and what it does not cover.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.sum.1': 'Metadata is information stored inside a file about the file: author, dates, device, software and sometimes location.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.sum.2': 'Photos carry EXIF data, including camera model and GPS location. Re-encoding a picture in the browser removes it.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.sum.3': 'PDFs carry document information and XMP metadata. The metadata tool can edit it or remove it all.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.sum.4': 'Removing metadata does not change the visible content; hidden text, comments and the picture itself stay.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.sum.5': 'Review a file before sharing; do not assume that metadata has been cleaned.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.h': 'What metadata is',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.p1': 'Metadata is data about data. It is stored in the file but is not part of the visible page or picture. Two families matter here.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.p2': 'For photos, the standard is EXIF. It typically records:',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.l1.1': 'The camera or phone model and the lens.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.l1.2': 'Date and time the picture was taken.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.l1.3': 'Exposure settings.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.l1.4': 'GPS coordinates, if location was enabled on the device.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.l1.5': 'Sometimes the editing program used.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.p3': 'For PDFs, there are two layers:',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.l2.1': 'The document information: title, author, subject, keywords, creator program, producer program and creation and modification dates.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s1.l2.2': 'XMP metadata, a more detailed record that carries similar fields and more.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.h': 'Why it matters',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.p1': 'Most metadata is useless to others, but a few situations make it dangerous:',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.l1.1': 'A photo of an item for sale taken at home contains the coordinates of your home.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.l1.2': 'A PDF contract carries the name of the employee who prepared it, or an old title that reveals an earlier client.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.l1.3': 'A file exported from a template includes the template’s original author.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.l1.4': 'Dates can reveal when a document was really made, which may not match the date you claim.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.l1.5': 'The program name can disclose which software and version you use.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s2.p2': 'Metadata is not a secret kept in a vault; anybody with the file can read it with free tools. Removing it before sharing is basic hygiene.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.h': 'Removing metadata from PDFs',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.p1': '<tool_edit_pdf_metadata>Edit PDF Metadata</tool_edit_pdf_metadata> works inside your browser tab, so the PDF is read from your device.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it has a password.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.o1.2': 'The current properties are filled in. To see them without changing anything, <tool_pdf_metadata_viewer>PDF Metadata Viewer</tool_pdf_metadata_viewer> shows them.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.o1.3': 'Edit the title, author, subject and keywords, or choose Remove all metadata.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.o1.4': 'Save and download the updated PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.p2': 'Remove all metadata clears the document information, which covers title, author, subject, keywords, creator, producer and dates, and the embedded XMP metadata. It does not touch the page text, images or comments. If you want to keep a useful title, edit the fields instead and delete only the author and software details.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s3.p3': 'The tool also supports non-Latin text: titles and authors in Urdu, Arabic, Chinese and other scripts are saved correctly.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s4.h': 'Removing metadata from photos',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s4.p1': 'For pictures, the browser tools that re-encode an image through a canvas drop EXIF metadata as a side effect, including camera model and GPS location. That means any of these tools produce a clean copy:',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s4.l1.1': '<tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> re-encodes the picture and shows you the new size.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s4.l1.2': '<tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer> creates a smaller version, which is also what you want for most web uses.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s4.l1.3': '<tool_image_format_converter>Convert Image Format</tool_image_format_converter> changes the format and re-encodes in the same step.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s4.p2': 'Embedded colour profiles are dropped as well, which can shift colours slightly on some screens. For sharing photos on forums, marketplaces and social networks, this trade-off is usually welcome. Our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> explains the resizing routine, and <post_how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg>converting WebP to JPG</post_how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg> shows another way to get a clean copy.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s4.p3': 'Check the result: open the converted picture and look at its properties in your operating system. The GPS fields should be gone.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s5.h': 'What removing metadata does not do',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s5.p1': 'Be clear about the boundary:',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s5.l1.1': 'The visible content is unchanged. A name written in the document text stays.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s5.l1.2': 'Hidden text, comments and annotations in a PDF are not removed by the metadata tool. If parts of the content must not be seen, use <tool_redact_pdf>Redact PDF</tool_redact_pdf>, and read our guide to <post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly>redacting a PDF properly</post_how-to-redact-a-pdf-properly>.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s5.l1.3': 'Photos can still show a landmark, a street sign or a reflection that reveals the place. Metadata is only one of the ways a picture betrays its location.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s5.l1.4': 'Watermarks and visible stamps remain.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s5.l1.5': 'File names can contain names or places as well. Rename the file before sharing.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.h': 'A sensible sharing routine',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.o1.1': 'Decide who will receive the file and what they should know.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.o1.2': 'Look at the metadata. For PDFs, use the viewer; for photos, check the file properties.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.o1.3': 'Remove or edit what is not needed.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.o1.4': 'Check the visible content for names and details.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.o1.5': 'Rename the file so the name does not reveal anything.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.o1.6': 'Keep your original, with all its metadata, in a private place.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s6.p1': 'Metadata can also be useful. Photographers rely on EXIF to sort pictures, and archives rely on titles and dates. Strip it from the copies you publish, not from your master files.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s7.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s7.l1.1': 'Assuming that a file is clean because it looks clean.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s7.l1.2': 'Removing metadata but leaving hidden text or comments.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s7.l1.3': 'Posting photos of valuables or children with GPS data intact.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s7.l1.4': 'Forgetting the file name.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s7.l1.5': 'Deleting the original together with its useful metadata.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s8.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s8.o1.1': 'Look at what metadata the file carries.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s8.o1.2': 'Remove all of it, or edit it to what you want to show.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s8.o1.3': 'For photos, re-encode through a tool that drops EXIF.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s8.o1.4': 'Redact content that must not be visible.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.s8.o1.5': 'Rename the file and keep the original.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.1.q': 'What does Remove all metadata remove from a PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.1.a': 'The document information, which covers title, author, subject, keywords, creator, producer and dates, and the embedded XMP metadata. It does not touch the page text, images or comments.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.2.q': 'Why edit metadata at all?',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.2.a': 'To fix a wrong title shown in browser tabs and search results, to credit the right author, or to strip personal details before sharing a file.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.3.q': 'Do image tools remove GPS location from photos?',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.3.a': 'Re-encoding through a canvas drops EXIF metadata such as camera model and GPS location, so the output of the compressor, resizer and format converter does not carry it.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.4.q': 'Does it support non-Latin text in titles and authors?',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.4.a': 'Yes. Titles and authors in Urdu, Arabic, Chinese and other scripts are saved correctly.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.5.q': 'Can I edit a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.5.a': 'Not directly. Unlock it first, then edit the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.6.q': 'Are my files uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.faq.6.a': 'No. The tools run inside your browser, so the files stay on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-remove-metadata-from-photos-and-pdfs.cta.p': 'Open <tool_edit_pdf_metadata>Edit PDF Metadata</tool_edit_pdf_metadata>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.title': 'How to Resize an Image Without Losing Quality',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.description': 'Resize photos by exact pixels or percentage while keeping the aspect ratio. Learn why shrinking works better than enlarging and how to choose a size.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.excerpt': 'A practical guide to resizing pictures for web, email and print: pixels versus percentage, the aspect-ratio lock, batch resizing and what happens to quality.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.imageAlt': 'A photo being scaled down with arrows pointing inward on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.intro.1': 'Phone cameras take pictures that are far larger than most screens need. A 12-megapixel photo is about 4,000 pixels wide, while a web page might show it at 800 pixels, and an email client at even less. Sending the full picture wastes bandwidth, slows pages and often breaks upload limits. Resizing brings the picture down to the size it will actually be shown at.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.intro.2': 'This guide explains how resizing works, the difference between pixels and percentages, why the aspect-ratio lock matters, how to resize many pictures at once and why shrinking is safe while enlarging is not.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.sum.1': 'Resizing changes the pixel dimensions of a picture, which usually reduces file size a great deal.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.sum.2': 'Shrinking gives the best quality; enlarging cannot add detail and makes pictures look softer.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.sum.3': 'Keep the aspect-ratio lock on, or the picture will be stretched.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.sum.4': 'Enter exact pixels when a form tells you the size, or a percentage when you simply want it smaller.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.sum.5': 'You can resize several pictures at once, up to 20 per batch.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s1.h': 'What resizing really does',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s1.p1': 'A picture is a grid of pixels. Making it smaller means combining groups of pixels into one, which is a form of averaging. Done with good resampling, the result looks clean because the eye cannot see the detail that was thrown away at the smaller size. This is why shrinking is nearly always safe.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s1.p2': 'Making a picture larger works the other way: the program has to invent new pixels between the existing ones. It can only guess, so the result looks smooth but soft. No resizing tool can add real detail that the camera never captured. If you must enlarge, expect softness, and see our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> for the opposite direction. <tool_enlarge_image>Enlarge Image</tool_enlarge_image> uses better resampling for upscaling, but even it cannot invent real detail.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s2.h': 'Pixels or percentage?',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s2.p1': '<tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer> offers two ways to describe the new size:',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s2.l1.1': 'Pixels: you enter the width or the height, and the other follows from the aspect ratio. Use this when a destination specifies a size, such as 1,200 pixels wide for a blog or 600 by 600 for an avatar.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s2.l1.2': 'Percentage: you scale the picture by a share, for example 50 percent. Use this when you do not care about exact numbers and simply want everything smaller.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s2.p2': 'Percentages are handy for batches in which the pictures differ in size: every one is reduced by the same share.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.h': 'How to resize in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.p1': 'The tool works inside your browser tab, so the pictures are read from your device.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add one or more images (up to 20 per batch, each up to 25 MB).',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.2': 'Choose pixels or percentage and enter the new size.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.3': 'Keep the aspect-ratio lock on to avoid distortion.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.4': 'Choose the output quality for JPG or WebP if you need to.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.o1.5': 'Press Resize and download the results, individually or as a ZIP.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s3.p2': 'The result shows the new dimensions in the file name, so you can tell the versions apart.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s4.h': 'The aspect-ratio lock',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s4.p1': 'The aspect ratio is the proportion between width and height. When the lock is on, changing one dimension changes the other automatically so the picture keeps its shape. Turn the lock off only when you really want to change the shape, for example to make a picture fit exactly into a fixed frame. Most of the time, the lock should stay on; a stretched face or a squashed circle is easy to spot and hard to excuse.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s4.p2': 'If the picture needs another shape, crop it rather than stretching it. <tool_image_cropper>Crop Image</tool_image_cropper> lets you choose an aspect ratio and cut to that shape. Our guide to <post_how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size>cropping an image to an exact size</post_how-to-crop-an-image-to-a-specific-size> explains how, and why you should crop before you resize.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.h': 'Choosing a size',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.p1': 'Think about where the picture will be shown:',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.l1.1': 'A blog or article picture rarely needs to be wider than about 1,200 to 2,000 pixels.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.l1.2': 'An email picture can be around 800 to 1,200 pixels wide.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.l1.3': 'A profile picture or avatar needs a few hundred pixels.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.l1.4': 'A print picture needs many more pixels than a screen picture. As a rule, 300 pixels per inch of print size gives a sharp result.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.l1.5': 'A thumbnail needs only a small fraction of the original.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s5.p2': 'If a screen is sharp, pictures on it benefit from about twice the displayed size, because many phones and laptops pack more than one physical pixel into each displayed pixel. For a picture shown 600 pixels wide, a width of 1,200 gives a crisp result on those screens.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s6.h': 'Limits of the tool',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s6.p1': 'Browsers cap the size of the canvas they can work with. The tool limits output to 16,000 pixels on each side and about 100 megapixels. Each file can be up to 25 MB, and a batch holds up to 20 files, which keeps the browser responsive. Animated GIF or WebP files are processed using their first frame.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s7.h': 'Quality, format and metadata',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s7.p1': 'Resizing re-encodes the picture, which has consequences:',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s7.l1.1': 'JPG and WebP outputs have a quality setting. A medium-high value keeps pictures sharp while saving space.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s7.l1.2': 'PNG output is lossless, so it keeps every pixel but can remain large. Use it for graphics with sharp edges.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s7.l1.3': 'Re-encoding drops EXIF metadata, such as the camera model and the GPS location, and colour profiles. That is often what you want before sharing a photo online, but colours can shift slightly on some screens.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s7.p2': 'If you need the picture as another format, for example because a site rejects WebP, our guide to <post_how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg>converting WebP to JPG</post_how-to-convert-webp-to-jpg> helps. To switch the format at the same time, use <tool_image_format_converter>Convert Image Format</tool_image_format_converter>. Our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> explains how to choose. For a smaller file at the same dimensions, <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> is the right tool.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s8.h': 'Orientation first',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s8.p1': 'If a picture lies on its side, rotate it before resizing with <tool_image_rotator>Rotate Image</tool_image_rotator>. Resizing a sideways picture and rotating afterwards works too, but it is easy to end up with the dimensions the wrong way round.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s9.l1.1': 'Enlarging a small picture and expecting it to be sharp.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s9.l1.2': 'Turning the aspect-ratio lock off by accident, so the picture is stretched.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s9.l1.3': 'Resizing the same picture repeatedly. Always start again from the original.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s9.l1.4': 'Using a percentage when a form wants exact pixels.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s9.l1.5': 'Resizing before cropping, which wastes pixels you then throw away.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.1': 'Find out the size the destination needs.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.2': 'Crop and rotate first, then resize.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.3': 'Keep the aspect-ratio lock on.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.4': 'Shrink rather than enlarge whenever possible.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the original file and check the result at the size it will be shown.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.1.q': 'Can I enlarge an image?',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.1.a': 'Yes, but enlarging cannot add detail, so the result will look softer. Downscaling gives the best quality.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.2.q': 'What is the maximum output size?',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.2.a': 'Browsers limit canvas size. This tool caps output at 16,000 px per side and about 100 megapixels.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.3.q': 'Is EXIF or location data kept?',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.3.a': 'No. Re-encoding through a canvas drops EXIF metadata such as camera model and GPS location, which is often what you want before sharing a photo. Colour profiles are also not preserved.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.4.q': 'Can I resize many pictures at once?',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.4.a': 'Yes, up to 20 files per batch, each up to 25 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.5.q': 'Should I use pixels or a percentage?',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.5.a': 'Use pixels when a destination specifies a size and a percentage when you just want everything smaller by the same share.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.6.q': 'Are my pictures uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.faq.6.a': 'No. The tool runs inside your browser, so the files stay on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-resize-an-image-without-losing-quality.cta.p': 'Open <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.title': 'How to Rotate PDF Pages and Save the Change Permanently',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.description': 'Fix sideways or upside-down pages in a PDF, rotate one page or all of them, and save the new orientation so it stays that way in every viewer.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.excerpt': 'Rotate single pages or the whole PDF with thumbnail previews, learn why scans arrive sideways and how to save the rotation so it sticks.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.imageAlt': 'A sideways PDF page being turned upright with a curved arrow on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.intro.1': 'Almost everyone has opened a PDF and found a page lying on its side. It happens with scans from office copiers, with photos turned into documents and with files assembled from several sources. Your viewer lets you rotate the view, but the next person who opens the file sees the same sideways page again, because the rotation was only a viewing setting.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to fix the orientation for good: rotate individual pages or the whole document, save a new PDF, and avoid the small traps that make a rotated page look wrong later.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.sum.1': 'Rotating the view in a PDF reader is temporary. Saving a rotated copy makes the change permanent.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.sum.2': 'You can rotate one page, several pages or all pages, in steps of 90 degrees.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.sum.3': 'The rotation is stored as a page attribute, so the page content is not redrawn and nothing is lost.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.sum.4': 'Mixed portrait and landscape pages are normal; rotate only the ones that are wrong.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.sum.5': 'Always check each page after saving, because a page rotated twice ends up upside down.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s1.h': 'Why pages end up sideways',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s1.p1': 'A PDF page has a size and a rotation setting. Many things can leave the rotation wrong:',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s1.l1.1': 'A scanner or copier feeds the paper in landscape and records it that way.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s1.l1.2': 'A phone photo of a document is stored as a landscape picture and placed on the page without turning it.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s1.l1.3': 'A document is built from files that were created in different orientations.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s1.l1.4': 'Somebody rotated the page only in their viewer and never saved it.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s1.p2': 'None of this affects the text or pictures themselves. Only the angle at which the page is shown is wrong, which is why it can be fixed without any loss of quality.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s2.h': 'How to rotate a PDF in your browser',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s2.p1': '<tool_rotate_pdf>Rotate PDF</tool_rotate_pdf> shows every page as a thumbnail, so you can see the effect before you save. It runs in your browser, so the file is read from your device.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). Unlock it first if it is password-protected.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s2.o1.2': 'Wait for the thumbnails to appear.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s2.o1.3': 'Rotate individual pages with the buttons next to each thumbnail, or rotate all pages at once.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s2.o1.4': 'Look at every thumbnail. A page that was rotated by mistake can be turned back.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s2.o1.5': 'Save the rotated PDF and download it.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s3.h': 'What “permanent” means here',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s3.p1': 'A PDF stores a rotation value for every page, a multiple of 90 degrees. The tool changes that value in the new file. The page content is not redrawn and not converted to pictures, so text stays selectable, links keep working and the file size barely changes.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s3.p2': 'The new file is a copy. Your original is untouched, which is useful if you rotate the wrong pages and want to start again. Save the result under a new name, for example scan-rotated.pdf.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s4.h': 'Rotating one page, some pages or all pages',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s4.l1.1': 'One wrong page: rotate just that thumbnail. This is the most common case in a scanned stack.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s4.l1.2': 'Every page wrong in the same way, for example a whole landscape scan: use the option that rotates all pages at once.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s4.l1.3': 'A mix: rotate the groups separately. It is quicker to rotate all pages first and then turn back the few that were already correct.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s4.p1': 'Direction matters. A page that is lying on its left side needs a quarter turn to the right, and an upside-down page needs a half turn. If you click the wrong way, simply turn the page again until the thumbnail looks right.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s5.h': 'Rotating is not the same as cropping or reordering',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s5.p1': 'Three operations are easy to confuse:',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s5.l1.1': 'Rotate changes the angle of a page.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s5.l1.2': '<tool_crop_pdf>Crop PDF</tool_crop_pdf> changes the visible area of a page, for example to trim a wide white margin (see <post_how-to-crop-pdf-pages>how to crop PDF pages</post_how-to-crop-pdf-pages>).',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s5.l1.3': '<tool_reorder_pdf_pages>Reorder PDF Pages</tool_reorder_pdf_pages> changes the position of pages in the document.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s5.p2': 'A badly scanned page may need more than one of them. Rotate first, so you can see what you are working with, and crop or reorder afterwards. If a document needs only some of its pages, <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> can pick them out.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s6.h': 'Preventing sideways pages in the first place',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s6.l1.1': 'Scan documents in the orientation of the paper, and check the preview before sending the scan.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s6.l1.2': 'When you photograph pages, hold the phone parallel to the paper and use the same orientation for every page.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s6.l1.3': 'When you build a PDF from pictures, use a tool that lets you set the page orientation. Our guide to <post_convert-images-to-pdf>converting images to PDF</post_convert-images-to-pdf> explains how, and <tool_scan_to_pdf>Scan to PDF</tool_scan_to_pdf> offers automatic or fixed orientation.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s6.l1.4': 'When you combine files with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>, check the result page by page. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> lists the other things to look at.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s7.h': 'What to check after saving',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s7.p1': 'Open the new file in a normal viewer and scroll through it, not only the pages you touched:',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s7.l1.1': 'Are all pages upright?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s7.l1.2': 'Did a page that was already correct get rotated by the “all pages” option?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s7.l1.3': 'Are pages that are meant to be landscape, such as a wide table, still landscape?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s7.l1.4': 'Do the page numbers or headers read the right way up?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s7.p2': 'If a page is still wrong, run the tool again on the new file. Rotations add up, so a page turned right twice is upside down.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s8.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s8.l1.1': 'Relying on the viewer’s rotate button and sending the file without saving the change.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s8.l1.2': 'Rotating all pages when only a few were wrong.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s8.l1.3': 'Rotating the same page twice and not noticing.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s8.l1.4': 'Judging the result from the first page only.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s8.l1.5': 'Overwriting the original instead of saving a new copy.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s9.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s9.o1.1': 'Add the PDF and wait for the thumbnails.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s9.o1.2': 'Rotate only the pages that are wrong, or all pages when they all are.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s9.o1.3': 'Check every thumbnail before saving.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s9.o1.4': 'Save, download and scroll through the result.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.s9.o1.5': 'Keep the original until you are sure the new file is right.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.1.q': 'Is the rotation really saved in the file?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.1.a': 'Yes. The tool writes the new rotation into a new PDF. It opens the right way up in any viewer, not only in the one you used.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.2.q': 'Does rotating a PDF reduce its quality?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.2.a': 'No. The page content is not redrawn, only the page’s rotation value changes. Text stays selectable and pictures keep their quality.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.3.q': 'Can I rotate only some pages?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.3.a': 'Yes. Rotate individual thumbnails, or rotate all pages at once when they all need the same change.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.4.q': 'Can I rotate by a small angle to straighten a crooked scan?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.4.a': 'No. Pages turn in steps of 90 degrees. A scan that is slightly crooked needs to be scanned again or straightened in an image editor before it is turned into a PDF.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.5.q': 'Can I rotate a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.5.a': 'Not directly. Unlock it first, then rotate the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.6.q': 'Is my file uploaded?',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.faq.6.a': 'No. The tool works inside your browser, so the PDF stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-rotate-pdf-pages-permanently.cta.p': 'Open <tool_rotate_pdf>Rotate PDF</tool_rotate_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.title': 'How to Split a PDF: Ranges, Single Pages and Chunks',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.description': 'Split a PDF by page ranges, into single pages or into equal parts, or extract just the pages you need, directly in your browser and without uploading the file.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.excerpt': 'Break a large PDF into the parts you need, by range, page by page or in fixed-size chunks, and pick the right tool for each job.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.imageAlt': 'One long PDF document being cut into separate smaller documents, shown on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.intro.1': 'You have a 120-page scan and the person you are writing to needs pages 14 to 19. Or a report where every chapter must go to a different team. Or a bundle of invoices that arrived as one file and has to be filed one by one. Sending the whole document is slow, untidy and sometimes a privacy problem.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.intro.2': 'Splitting solves all three. This guide explains the three ways to split a PDF, how to write page ranges that do what you mean, and when a different tool is a better fit.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.sum.1': 'Split PDF can cut a file by custom ranges such as 1-3 and 4-6, into single pages, or into chunks of a fixed size.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.sum.2': 'Download the parts one by one or all together as a ZIP.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.sum.3': 'To keep only some pages as one new file, Extract PDF Pages is simpler; to delete pages, use Remove PDF Pages.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.sum.4': 'Everything happens in your browser, so the document is never uploaded.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.h': 'Three ways to split a PDF',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.p1': '<tool_split_pdf>Split PDF</tool_split_pdf> offers three modes, and picking the right one saves time.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.h1': 'Custom ranges',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.p2': 'Use this when you know which pages go together. Type ranges separated by commas, for example 1-3, 4-6, 7-12. Each range becomes its own PDF. This is the mode for chapters, sections, and for pulling a single part out of a long document.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.h2': 'Every page',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.p3': 'Use this when every page must be a separate file, such as invoices, tickets, payslips or certificates that were scanned in one run. You get one PDF per page.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.h3': 'Every N pages',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s1.p4': 'Use this to cut a long file into equal pieces, for example every 10 pages. The last part contains whatever is left over, so a 25-page file split every 10 pages gives 10, 10 and 5.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s2.h': 'Step by step',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF. It can be up to 100 MB.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Choose the mode: custom ranges, every page or every N pages.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'For custom ranges, type the ranges. For the other modes, set the number if asked.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Press Split. The parts appear as a list.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s2.o1.5': 'Download each part, or take all of them together as a ZIP file.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s3.h': 'Writing page ranges that work',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s3.p1': 'Ranges are written the way you would write them in a print dialog. A few examples show the pattern:',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s3.l1.1': '1-3 means pages 1, 2 and 3 as one file.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s3.l1.2': '1-3, 4-6 means two files: one from pages 1 to 3 and one from pages 4 to 6.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s3.l1.3': '5 on its own means page 5 as a single-page file.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s3.p2': 'Page numbers are the position of the page in the file, counting from 1, not the number printed on the page. A document with a cover and a roman-numeral preface can have printed number 1 on its fifth page, and the tool counts the fifth page as 5. If a result looks off by a few pages, that is almost always the reason; open the PDF and check the position of the page in the viewer.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s3.n1': 'Tip: do a test with one short range first. Download it and check that it starts and ends on the pages you meant before you split the whole file.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s4.h': 'When Extract PDF Pages is the better tool',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s4.p1': 'Split PDF makes several files. If you want one new file that contains a chosen set of pages, <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> is quicker. You click page thumbnails to select pages, or type a list such as 1-3, 8, and download one new PDF with exactly those pages in order. Use it for sending “just pages 4, 9 and 10” to someone.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s4.p2': 'If you only need to throw pages away, such as blank pages at the end of a scan, <tool_remove_pdf_pages>Remove PDF Pages</tool_remove_pdf_pages> deletes them and keeps everything else in one file.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s5.h': 'Splitting and merging go together',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s5.p1': 'Two jobs come up again and again. The first is to take apart a pack, change one part and put it back together: split the file, edit or replace the part, then join the parts again with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>. Our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> covers the order and limits.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s5.p2': 'The second is to rearrange a document. For a handful of pages, <tool_reorder_pdf_pages>Reorder PDF Pages</tool_reorder_pdf_pages> lets you drag thumbnails into a new order without splitting anything.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s6.h': 'Splitting to send a large PDF',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s6.p1': 'Attachment limits are set by the receiving system. Many email services accept roughly 20 to 25 MB in total, and portals often allow far less. If a PDF is too large, splitting can help: send two files of half the size, or only the pages that matter. If the pages themselves are heavy, for example scans with photos, compressing is usually a better answer. Our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains how to shrink a file while keeping text sharp.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s7.h': 'What to know before you split',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s7.l1.1': 'Password-protected PDFs are not supported. If you know the password, remove it first with <tool_unlock_pdf>Unlock PDF</tool_unlock_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s7.l1.2': 'Very large or complex files depend on your device memory. If a file will not process, split it in two stages, for example first by half, then each half by range.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s7.l1.3': 'The pages of each part keep their look, but a link that pointed to a page now in another part cannot work any more.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s7.l1.4': 'Bookmarks of the original file do not follow the parts, so rebuild them if you need them.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s8.h': 'Common situations',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'A scanned bundle of invoices: use Every page, then rename the files to match your filing system.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'A long report for several readers: use custom ranges, one range per chapter.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'A big file for an upload form with a page limit: use Every N pages, with N set to the form’s limit, and upload the parts one by one.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'Only the signature page: use Extract PDF Pages and select that page.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Does splitting a PDF reduce the quality?',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.1.a': 'No. The pages are copied into the new files as they are, so text and images look the same as in the original.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Can I split a PDF into single pages and get them all at once?',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.2.a': 'Yes. Choose Every page and download the result as a ZIP file that contains one PDF per page.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Is my PDF uploaded to a server when I split it?',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.3.a': 'No. Split PDF works inside your browser tab, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.4.q': 'What is the maximum file size?',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.4.a': 'Up to 100 MB per PDF, although very large or complex files also depend on your device memory.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.5.q': 'How do I split a PDF that asks for a password?',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Remove the password first with Unlock PDF, which works when you know the password, then split the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.cta.h': 'Split your PDF now',
  'blog.how-to-split-a-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_split_pdf>Split PDF</tool_split_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.title': 'How to Test Regular Expressions With Live Matches',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.description': 'Learn the basics of regular expressions, test patterns against sample text, read capture groups and flags, and avoid patterns that freeze your browser.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.excerpt': 'A beginner-friendly guide to regular expressions: patterns, flags, groups and replacements, a short list of useful patterns and how to avoid slow, runaway patterns.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.imageAlt': 'A search pattern highlighting matches inside lines of text on a teal background',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.intro.1': 'A regular expression, often shortened to regex, is a compact way to describe a pattern in text. With one line you can find every date in a document, check that a field looks like a phone number, pull the numbers out of a log file or rewrite a thousand lines in one go.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.intro.2': 'The syntax looks strange at first, and it is easy to get a pattern almost right. Testing it against real sample text, with the matches highlighted, is the fastest way to learn and to avoid mistakes. This guide explains the building blocks, shows how to read matches and groups, and explains how to stay clear of patterns that run forever.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.sum.1': 'A regex describes a pattern. The tool highlights every match in your sample text as you type.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.sum.2': 'Parentheses create groups, and the tool lists what each group captured.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.sum.3': 'Flags change the behaviour: global, ignore case, multiline, dot matches newline and unicode.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.sum.4': 'The tool uses JavaScript regular expressions, which differ in a few details from other languages.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.sum.5': 'Patterns with nested repetition can run for a very long time, so the tool stops them after 1.5 seconds.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s1.h': 'What a regular expression is',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s1.p1': 'A regex is a small program written as text. Most characters match themselves, so the pattern cat matches the letters c, a and t in that order. The power comes from special symbols that match classes of characters, repeat things or mark positions.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s1.p2': 'People use regular expressions in editors, in programming languages, on the command line and in form validation. The same ideas appear everywhere, which is why learning them once pays off for years.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.h': 'The building blocks',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.p1': 'These pieces cover most everyday patterns.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.1': 'A dot matches any single character except a line break.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.2': 'A backslash with d matches a digit, with w a letter, digit or underscore, and with s a space or tab. The capital versions mean the opposite.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.3': 'A plus sign means one or more of the previous item, a star means zero or more, and a question mark means zero or one.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.4': 'Curly braces set an exact count, so the pattern for four digits is a digit symbol followed by braces with the number 4.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.5': 'A caret matches the start of a line and a dollar sign the end of it.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.6': 'A backslash with b matches a word boundary, the edge between a word and the space around it.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.7': 'A vertical bar means or, so the pattern cat or dog is written with a bar between the two words.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.l1.8': 'Parentheses group items together and capture what they matched.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s2.p2': 'Put them together and you can describe a date as four digits, a hyphen, two digits, a hyphen and two digits. Each piece is simple. The skill is in combining them.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.h': 'How to test a pattern',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.p1': '<tool_regex_tester>Regex Tester</tool_regex_tester> works inside your browser tab, so your sample text stays on your device.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.o1.1': 'Type your pattern in the pattern box, without the slashes that some programs write around it.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.o1.2': 'Choose the flags you need.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.o1.3': 'Paste the text to test.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.o1.4': 'Read the highlighted matches and the table that lists every match with its position.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.o1.5': 'Open the replacement preview to see what the text would look like after a find and replace.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s3.p2': 'Start with a small sample that contains both the lines you want to match and some that you do not. A good test set includes the easy cases, the edge cases and a few lines that should be ignored.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.h': 'Flags',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.p1': 'Flags change how the whole pattern behaves. The tool offers these.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.l1.1': 'Global finds every match instead of stopping at the first one. You usually want it on.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.l1.2': 'Ignore case treats capital and small letters as the same.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.l1.3': 'Multiline makes the caret and the dollar sign match at the start and end of every line, not only of the whole text.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.l1.4': 'Dot matches newline lets the dot cross line breaks.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.l1.5': 'Unicode makes the engine understand characters outside the basic range, such as emoji and letters from many alphabets.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s4.p2': 'When a pattern works on one line but fails on a long text, the cause is usually the multiline or the global flag.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s5.h': 'Groups and replacements',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s5.p1': 'Parentheses turn part of a pattern into a group. The table in the tool shows what each group captured in each match, which is the quickest way to check that a pattern splits text the way you intended.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s5.p2': 'Take the pattern with three groups for a year, a month and a day, built from digits and hyphens. On the text 2026-10-25 the first group holds the year, the second the month and the third the day. In a replacement, you can refer to these groups by number, so you can reorder them. A replacement that writes group three, then group two, then group one, separated by slashes, turns the date into day, month and year.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s5.p3': 'The replacement preview shows the result before you use the pattern anywhere else, which saves you from damaging a large file.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.h': 'Patterns worth keeping',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.p1': 'A few patterns appear again and again.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.l1.1': 'Lines that are empty or contain only spaces: a caret, optional spaces and a dollar sign.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.l1.2': 'Two or more spaces in a row: a space followed by a plus sign, which you replace with a single space.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.l1.3': 'A number with optional decimals: digits, then an optional group made of a dot and more digits.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.l1.4': 'A whole word, so that cat does not match inside catalogue: the word with a word boundary on each side.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.l1.5': 'Spaces at the end of a line: spaces followed by a dollar sign.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s6.p2': 'For plain cleanup, you may not need a pattern at all. <tool_text_cleaner>Text Cleaner</tool_text_cleaner> removes extra spaces, empty lines and invisible characters with a few tick boxes, and <tool_remove_duplicate_lines>Remove Duplicate Lines</tool_remove_duplicate_lines> removes repeated lines. Our guide to <post_how-to-count-words-and-characters-online>counting words and characters</post_how-to-count-words-and-characters-online> uses the same tools to prepare text.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s7.h': 'Be careful with validation',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s7.p1': 'Regular expressions are tempting for checking emails, phone numbers and addresses, but the real rules are complicated. A short pattern for an email address will accept some addresses that are wrong and reject some that are right. Use a regex as a quick first check, and rely on a confirmation email or a proper library for anything important.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s7.p2': 'For structured data, a pattern is the wrong tool. Do not try to pull values out of JSON or XML with a regex. Format and read them with a proper tool instead, such as <tool_json_formatter>JSON Formatter</tool_json_formatter>, and see our guide to <post_how-to-format-and-validate-json>formatting and validating JSON</post_how-to-format-and-validate-json>.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s8.h': 'Patterns that can freeze your browser',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s8.p1': 'Some patterns are dangerous. If a pattern has a repetition inside another repetition, and the text almost matches but not quite, the engine can try an enormous number of combinations. This is called catastrophic backtracking. A classic example is a group repeated with a plus sign inside another plus sign.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s8.p2': 'Such patterns can freeze a page for seconds or minutes. The tool runs matching in a background worker and stops after 1.5 seconds, or after 5,000 matches, so a runaway pattern cannot freeze the page. If you see the warning, make the pattern simpler: remove the nested repetition, be more specific about what may repeat or add an anchor.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s8.p3': 'The same advice protects your own programs. A pattern that is safe on short test text can still be slow on long input from users.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s9.h': 'Differences between regex flavours',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s9.p1': 'The tool uses JavaScript regular expressions, as built into your browser. Most everyday syntax is the same in Python, PHP, Java and others, but there are differences: some features exist in one language and not another, and the details of escaping and replacements change. When you move a pattern to another language, test it there too.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s10.h': 'Related tasks',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s10.p1': 'Regex also helps with data you copy between tools. <tool_text_diff_checker>Text Diff Checker</tool_text_diff_checker> compares the text before and after a replacement, so you can confirm that only the intended lines changed. If your pattern works on links, <tool_url_encoder_decoder>URL Encoder / Decoder</tool_url_encoder_decoder> shows how special characters are written in an address.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s10.p2': 'Dates and times are another common target. Instead of cutting dates out with patterns, you may find it easier to convert them to numbers. Our guide to <post_unix-timestamps-explained>Unix timestamps</post_unix-timestamps-explained> explains how.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s11.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s11.l1.1': 'Forgetting the global flag and wondering why only one match is found.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s11.l1.2': 'Using the dot when you mean a literal dot, which has to be escaped with a backslash.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s11.l1.3': 'Making a pattern greedy when you want the shortest match.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s11.l1.4': 'Using one big pattern when two simple ones are clearer.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s11.l1.5': 'Trusting a short email pattern as a complete validation.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s11.l1.6': 'Testing only on the examples that should match and not on those that should not.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s12.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s12.o1.1': 'Write a small test text with matches and non-matches.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s12.o1.2': 'Build the pattern in small steps and watch the highlights.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s12.o1.3': 'Check the groups table and the replacement preview.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s12.o1.4': 'Keep patterns simple and avoid nested repetition.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.s12.o1.5': 'Test the pattern again in the language where you will use it.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.1.q': 'Which regex flavour is used?',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.1.a': 'JavaScript (ECMAScript) regular expressions, as implemented by your browser. PCRE, Python and other flavours differ in some features.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.2.q': 'Why does my page freeze on some patterns?',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.2.a': 'Patterns with nested repetition can backtrack catastrophically. Matching runs in a background worker and is stopped after 1.5 seconds, so a runaway pattern cannot freeze the page, but you should still avoid patterns with a repeated group inside another repetition.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.3.q': 'How many matches does the tool show?',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.3.a': 'Matching stops after 5,000 matches or 1.5 seconds.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.4.q': 'Do I type the slashes around the pattern?',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.4.a': 'No. Enter the pattern without the surrounding slashes and choose the flags separately.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.5.q': 'Can I preview a find and replace?',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.faq.5.a': 'Yes. The replacement preview shows the text after the replacement, and you can use captured groups in it.',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.how-to-test-regular-expressions.cta.p': 'Open <tool_regex_tester>Regex Tester</tool_regex_tester>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.title': 'JPG vs PNG vs WebP: Which Image Format Should You Use?',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.description': 'Compare JPG, PNG and WebP for photos, screenshots, logos and transparency. Learn when each is best and what is lost when you convert between them.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.excerpt': 'Pick the right image format for photos, screenshots and logos, and learn what converting between JPG, PNG and WebP really does.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.imageAlt': 'Three image tiles labelled JPG, PNG and WEBP side by side on a teal background',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.intro.1': 'You save a screenshot and it is a PNG. Your phone saves photos as JPG, or sometimes as formats a website will not accept. A website asks for WebP. Every format has a reason to exist, and picking the wrong one means files that are bigger than needed, blurry text or a transparent logo with an ugly white box around it.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.intro.2': 'This guide compares the three formats you meet most often, in plain language, and shows how to convert between them without nasty surprises.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.sum.1': 'JPG is the universal format for photographs: small files, lossy, no transparency.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.sum.2': 'PNG is lossless and supports transparency: best for screenshots, logos and graphics, but large for photos.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.sum.3': 'WebP gives smaller files than JPG or PNG at similar quality and supports transparency, but needs a browser or service that accepts it.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.sum.4': 'Converting a JPG to PNG does not restore lost detail; it only changes the container.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s1.h': 'JPG: the photo format',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s1.p1': 'JPG, also written JPEG, was designed for photographs. It stores an image by throwing away detail the eye is least likely to notice, which makes files small. The quality setting controls how much is thrown away.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s1.p2': 'Strengths: small files for photos, supported everywhere, from phones to printers to every website and application. Weaknesses: no transparency, and repeated saving at lossy settings slowly degrades the picture. Sharp edges and text inside a JPG can show fuzzy halos at low quality.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s2.h': 'PNG: the lossless format',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s2.p1': 'PNG keeps every pixel exactly as it is. Nothing is thrown away, so saving a PNG again and again causes no further loss. It also supports transparency, so a logo can sit on any background without a box around it.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s2.p2': 'Strengths: perfect for screenshots, diagrams, logos, icons and anything with sharp edges or flat colours. Weaknesses: file sizes for photographs are much larger than JPG, because photos have little of the flat repetition PNG compresses well.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s3.h': 'WebP: the modern all-rounder',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s3.p1': 'WebP was created for the web. It can compress like JPG (lossy) or like PNG (lossless), supports transparency and usually produces smaller files at similar visual quality.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s3.p2': 'Strengths: smaller pages and faster loading, with transparency when you need it. Weaknesses: not every program, upload form or older device accepts it. If an application rejects your file, converting back to JPG or PNG is the fix. Browsers must also support WebP encoding for the tools to create it, and unsupported browsers show an error rather than a broken file.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s4.h': 'A quick way to decide',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s4.l1.1': 'Photo to email, print or upload form: JPG.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s4.l1.2': 'Screenshot or diagram with text: PNG.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s4.l1.3': 'Logo or icon that must have a transparent background: PNG, or WebP if the destination supports it.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s4.l1.4': 'Photo on your own website where speed matters: WebP, with JPG as the fallback if you need broad compatibility.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s4.l1.5': 'Archive or editing copy where you want no loss: PNG.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s5.h': 'Converting between formats in your browser',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s5.p1': 'Kitwell has a converter for each common pair. All of them work in your browser, so your pictures are not uploaded, and they accept up to 20 files of 25 MB each per batch:',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s5.l1.1': '<tool_jpg_to_png>JPG to PNG</tool_jpg_to_png>: when an application needs PNG, or you want to stop further quality loss from re-saving.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s5.l1.2': '<tool_png_to_jpg>PNG to JPG</tool_png_to_jpg>: to make a photo or screenshot smaller.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s5.l1.3': '<tool_jpg_to_webp>JPG to WebP</tool_jpg_to_webp> and <tool_png_to_webp>PNG to WebP</tool_png_to_webp>: for smaller web images.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s5.l1.4': '<tool_webp_to_jpg>WebP to JPG</tool_webp_to_jpg> and <tool_webp_to_png>WebP to PNG</tool_webp_to_png>: when a site or program does not accept WebP.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s6.h': 'What really changes when you convert',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s6.p1': 'Converting changes how the picture is stored, and each direction has a consequence:',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s6.l1.1': 'JPG to PNG: the file usually becomes larger, and the picture looks identical. It does not recover detail that the JPG compression already removed. PNG simply stops further loss.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s6.l1.2': 'PNG to JPG: the file becomes smaller, but transparent areas are filled with white, because JPG cannot store transparency. Fine detail can soften slightly.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s6.l1.3': 'Anything to WebP: smaller file, same look at a sensible quality. Transparency is kept when converting from PNG.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s6.l1.4': 'WebP to JPG or PNG: for compatibility. Going to JPG flattens transparency onto a colour; going to PNG keeps it.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s6.n1': 'Tip: always convert from the best copy you have. A JPG made from another JPG, or from a screenshot of a JPG, is worse than one made from the original.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s7.h': 'What is not preserved',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s7.p1': 'The converters read the pixels and write a new file. The camera information stored with a photo, such as EXIF data (date, location and camera model) and embedded colour profiles, is not carried over. That is good for privacy, but colours may look slightly different on some screens. Animated GIF or WebP files are converted using their first frame only.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s8.h': 'Make files smaller after converting',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s8.p1': 'A new file in the right format can still be larger than you need. Run it through <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> or lower the dimensions. Our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> explains the order that gives the least visible damage. If the images are going into a document, <post_convert-images-to-pdf>turn them into a PDF</post_convert-images-to-pdf> afterwards.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s9.h': 'Common mistakes',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s9.l1.1': 'Using PNG for photographs on a website, which makes pages slow for no visible gain.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s9.l1.2': 'Saving a logo as JPG and getting a white box on a coloured background.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s9.l1.3': 'Re-saving a JPG many times, which adds a little damage each time.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s9.l1.4': 'Converting JPG to PNG to “improve quality”. It does not.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.s9.l1.5': 'Sending WebP to someone whose program cannot open it, without a JPG copy.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.1.q': 'Which is better, JPG or PNG?',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.1.a': 'Neither is better in general. JPG is better for photographs because the files are much smaller. PNG is better for screenshots, logos and anything with sharp edges or transparency.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.2.q': 'Is WebP better than JPG?',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.2.a': 'WebP files are usually smaller at similar quality and WebP supports transparency. JPG is accepted by more programs and upload forms, so choose by where the image will be used.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.3.q': 'Does converting JPG to PNG improve the quality?',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.3.a': 'No. The picture looks the same. PNG just prevents further loss when you save it again, and the file becomes larger.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.4.q': 'Will my transparent PNG keep its transparency as a JPG?',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.4.a': 'No. JPG cannot store transparency, so transparent areas are filled with white.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.5.q': 'Are my pictures uploaded when I convert them?',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.faq.5.a': 'No. The converters run in your browser, so the files stay on your device.',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.cta.h': 'Convert an image now',
  'blog.jpg-vs-png-vs-webp.cta.p': 'Open <tool_png_to_jpg>PNG to JPG</tool_png_to_jpg>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.title': 'How to Make a Scanned PDF Searchable with OCR',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.description': 'Learn what OCR does, how to tell whether a PDF is a scan, and how to add a searchable text layer to scanned pages in your browser, with tips for better accuracy.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.excerpt': 'A scanned PDF is a stack of pictures. See how OCR turns it into text you can search and copy, and how to get the best results.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.imageAlt': 'A scanned page with a magnifying glass revealing selectable text lines, on a teal background',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.intro.1': 'You open a PDF, press Ctrl+F to look for a name, and nothing is found, even though you can see the name on the page. Or you try to copy a paragraph and the selection tool grabs the whole page as a picture. That PDF is a scan: every page is an image of text, not text itself.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.intro.2': 'Optical character recognition, or OCR, reads the letters in those pictures and gives you real text back. This guide explains how it works, how to run it in your browser without uploading the document, and what to do when the results are not perfect.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.sum.1': 'A scanned PDF is a stack of pictures; OCR reads the letters and adds a text layer you can search and copy.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.sum.2': 'The recognition runs in your browser, so the scanned document is never uploaded.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.sum.3': 'It reads English printed text; handwriting and other languages are unreliable.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.sum.4': 'Scan quality matters more than any setting: 200 to 300 DPI, straight pages and good contrast.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s1.h': 'First, check whether your PDF is really a scan',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s1.p1': 'Not every PDF needs OCR. A quick test: try to select a single word with the mouse, or search for a word you can see. If the word highlights, the PDF already contains text. If you can only drag a box over a whole region, or the search finds nothing, it is a scan.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s1.p2': '<tool_extract_pdf_text>Extract PDF Text</tool_extract_pdf_text> gives a clear answer as well. If a file has no selectable text, it says so and explains that reading it needs OCR.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s2.h': 'What OCR actually produces',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s2.p1': 'The OCR tool recognises the text on each page and adds it as an invisible layer behind the picture. The pages look exactly as before, but you can now search them, select words and copy text. You can also download the recognised text as a plain text file.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s2.p2': 'Because the original page images are kept untouched, nothing about the look of your document changes. The text layer is only as good as the recognition, which is why checking the result matters.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s3.h': 'How to run OCR in your browser',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s3.o1.1': 'Open <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf> and add your scanned PDF (up to 100 MB).',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s3.o1.2': 'Choose the quality: Fast (150 DPI), Recommended (200 DPI), or Best for small print (300 DPI). Higher settings read smaller text but take longer.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s3.o1.3': 'Optionally limit the pages, for example 1-3, 5. Leave it empty to read every page. The option “Skip pages that already have selectable text” saves time on mixed documents.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s3.o1.4': 'Start recognition. Each page takes a few seconds, and you can cancel at any time.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s3.o1.5': 'Review the recognised text, then download the searchable PDF or the text file.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s4.h': 'What to know before you start',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s4.l1.1': 'English only for now. Text in other languages is misread, and the tool says so up front rather than pretending otherwise.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s4.l1.2': 'The recognition engine (Tesseract, compiled to WebAssembly) and its English data are served from this website and run inside your browser. The first run loads about 3 MB, and your document is never uploaded.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s4.l1.3': 'Large documents are slow, because your own device does the work. For a long scan, process a page range at a time.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.h': 'Getting more accurate results',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.p1': 'OCR quality depends far more on the scan than on the settings. These habits make the biggest difference:',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.l1.1': 'Scan at 200 to 300 DPI. Lower resolutions blur small letters together; much higher ones only add file size and processing time.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.l1.2': 'Keep the page straight. A skewed page produces more errors, so re-scan if the text slopes noticeably.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.l1.3': 'Use good contrast. Dark text on a plain light background works best; shadows, folds and coloured backgrounds hurt.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.l1.4': 'Expect trouble with handwriting, very small print and decorative fonts. Recognition of these is unreliable.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.l1.5': 'Always check important numbers, such as amounts, dates and reference codes, against the page. A single misread digit can change the meaning.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s5.n1': 'Tip: if your scan came from photographs of pages, build it from straight, well-lit pictures with <tool_images_to_pdf>Images to PDF</tool_images_to_pdf> first. Our guide to <post_convert-images-to-pdf>converting images to PDF</post_convert-images-to-pdf> explains the options.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s6.h': 'Which quality setting should you choose?',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s6.p1': 'The three settings trade speed against how small a letter the engine can still read. Fast (150 DPI) is fine for a quick look at a clean page of normal-sized text. Recommended (200 DPI) is the right starting point for most documents: letters, contracts, forms and book pages. Best for small print (300 DPI) is for fine print, footnotes, receipts and anything photographed from a distance, and it takes noticeably longer per page because there is more image to read.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s6.p2': 'If you are unsure, run Recommended on a page range of two or three pages first. Check those pages carefully, and only switch to Best if small characters are being misread. Re-running a whole long document at a higher setting is slow, so a short test saves time.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s7.h': 'How to check the result',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s7.p1': 'OCR is never perfect, so build a short review into your routine instead of trusting the output blindly:',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s7.o1.1': 'Search the finished PDF for a few words you can see on the page. If they are found, the text layer is aligned and working.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s7.o1.2': 'Copy a paragraph into a text editor and read it. Look for letters swapped with similar shapes, such as l, 1 and I, or 0 and O.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s7.o1.3': 'Check every number that matters: amounts, dates, account or reference codes, and phone numbers.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s7.o1.4': 'For contracts or legal documents, treat the text layer as a search aid and keep the original page images as the authoritative version.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s8.h': 'Scanned from a phone? Prepare the pages first',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s8.p1': 'Photos of pages work, but they give weaker results than flat scans, because of perspective, shadows and uneven light. Straighten and crop each picture, then turn the set into a PDF with <tool_scan_to_pdf>Scan to PDF</tool_scan_to_pdf>. Its black and white document style evens out shadows and makes printed text darker, which gives the OCR engine cleaner letters to read. Run <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf> on the result.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s9.h': 'What to do next',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s9.l1.1': 'Make the file smaller with <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>. Scans are often large; see <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>how to compress a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s9.l1.2': 'Compare two versions of a document with <tool_compare_pdf>Compare PDF</tool_compare_pdf>. Comparing text works once both files have a text layer.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.s9.l1.3': 'Hide private details before sharing with <tool_redact_pdf>Redact PDF</tool_redact_pdf>.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.1.q': 'Is my scanned document sent to an OCR service?',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.1.a': 'No. The recognition engine and its data run inside your browser, so the document stays on your device.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.2.q': 'Does OCR change how my pages look?',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.2.a': 'No. The original page images are kept as they are, and the recognised text is added as an invisible layer so that it can be searched and copied.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.3.q': 'How long does OCR take?',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.3.a': 'A few seconds per page on a typical computer, longer at the highest quality setting and on large pages. Because your own device does the work, a slow phone or a very long scan will take more time. You can cancel at any time.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.4.q': 'Will the file get bigger after OCR?',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.4.a': 'Slightly, because the text layer is added to the existing page images. If size matters, run Compress PDF afterwards.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.5.q': 'What if only some pages are scans?',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.5.a': 'Use the option to skip pages that already have selectable text. Those pages are left untouched and only the scanned pages are processed.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.6.q': 'Can it read handwriting or languages other than English?',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.faq.6.a': 'Not reliably. The tool currently recognises English printed text. Handwriting and other languages produce many mistakes, so treat any result for them with caution.',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.ocr-scanned-pdf-searchable.cta.p': 'Open <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.title': 'How to Password-Protect and Unlock a PDF Safely',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.description': 'Lock a PDF with AES-256 encryption, choose what readers may do, and unlock a file you own. Learn how passwords and permissions differ and what the limits are.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.excerpt': 'Learn how to protect a PDF with a strong password, how permissions differ from passwords, and how to unlock a file you have the password for.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.imageAlt': 'A PDF document with a padlock being closed, on a teal background',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.intro.1': 'Payslips, medical letters, contracts, scans of identity documents: some PDFs should not be readable by whoever happens to find the attachment. A password is the simplest protection there is, and it travels with the file wherever it goes.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.intro.2': 'This guide explains how to protect a PDF with a password in your browser, how a password differs from permissions such as “no printing”, how to choose a password worth having, and how to remove protection from a file you own.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.sum.1': 'A document password stops anyone without it from opening the PDF; permissions only restrict actions such as printing or copying.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.sum.2': 'Protect PDF uses AES-256 encryption, and everything runs in your browser, so the file and the password are not uploaded.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.sum.3': 'Choose a long, unique password and share it through a different channel than the file.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.sum.4': 'Unlock PDF removes a password only when you provide the correct one; it never guesses passwords.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s1.h': 'Passwords and permissions are different things',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s1.p1': 'A PDF can have two kinds of protection, and people often mix them up:',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s1.l1.1': 'A password to open. Without it the file cannot be read at all. This is real protection: the content is encrypted.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s1.l1.2': 'Permissions. The file opens freely, but the reader program is asked not to allow printing, copying text or editing. These restrictions depend on the program obeying them, so treat them as a polite request, not as a lock.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s1.p2': 'If the content must stay private, you need a password to open. Permissions are for convenience, for example to discourage casual copying of a document you are sharing widely.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.h': 'How to protect a PDF in your browser',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.p1': '<tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf> runs inside your browser tab. The document is read from your device, encrypted there and returned as a download.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). If it already has a password, unlock it first.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Type the password twice to avoid typing mistakes.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Choose what readers may do: print, copy or edit.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'Press Protect and download the encrypted copy.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.o1.5': 'Open the new file and check that it asks for the password before you send it.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s2.p2': 'Passwords can be up to 127 standard characters, made of letters, digits and symbols.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s3.h': 'Choosing a password that is worth having',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s3.p1': 'Encryption is only as strong as the password. A short or guessable password can be tried automatically at enormous speed, however good the encryption.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s3.l1.1': 'Length beats cleverness. A password of 14 or more characters is far harder to guess than a short one with symbols.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s3.l1.2': 'Do not use names, birthdays, phone numbers or common words on their own.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s3.l1.3': 'Never reuse a password you use elsewhere.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s3.l1.4': 'Use a password manager to store it, instead of writing it in the email that carries the file.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s3.p2': 'If you want a strong random password, <tool_password_generator>Password Generator</tool_password_generator> creates one in your browser using a cryptographic random source, and does not store or send it.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s4.h': 'How to send a protected PDF safely',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s4.p1': 'The protection is only useful if the password does not travel next to the file. Send the PDF by email and the password by a different channel, such as a phone call or a messaging app. If the recipient needs it later, do not resend both together.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s4.p2': 'Tell the recipient that they need a reader that supports modern encryption. AES-256 files open in current PDF programs and browsers, but very old readers, from before about 2008, may not open them.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s5.h': 'How to unlock a PDF',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s5.p1': 'If you know the password and want a copy that opens without it, use <tool_unlock_pdf>Unlock PDF</tool_unlock_pdf>:',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s5.o1.1': 'Add the protected PDF.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s5.o1.2': 'Enter its password if asked. A PDF that only restricts printing or copying needs no password to open.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s5.o1.3': 'Download the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s5.p2': 'It supports standard PDF protection, including RC4 and AES encryption.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s6.h': 'What Unlock PDF cannot do',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s6.p1': 'Be clear about the limits so you do not waste time:',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s6.l1.1': 'It does not crack or guess passwords. Without the correct password, a PDF that is encrypted for opening stays closed.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s6.l1.2': 'It does not support certificate-based or rights-managed files.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s6.l1.3': 'If a file carried a digital signature, re-saving it invalidates the signature, because the document has changed.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s6.p2': 'Unlocking is meant for your own files, for example when you changed your mind about a password or must merge, edit or compress a protected PDF. Always respect the rights of whoever created the document.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s7.h': 'When you need to unlock before another step',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s7.p1': 'Many tools cannot read an encrypted PDF. If you want to join protected files, remove the password first with Unlock PDF, then use <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>; our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> covers the rest. The same goes for compressing: unlock, compress, and protect again at the end. Our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains the compression step.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s7.p2': 'The best order for a document you send is: edit, merge or compress first, sign if needed, and protect last. If you need a signature on the document, see <post_sign-a-pdf-without-printing>how to sign a PDF without printing</post_sign-a-pdf-without-printing>, because Sign PDF cannot open a password-protected file.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s8.h': 'Common mistakes',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'Sending the password in the same email as the file.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'Using a short, guessable password such as a name plus a year.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'Forgetting the password. There is no recovery: keep it in a password manager.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'Protecting a file and then deleting the unprotected original without checking that the protected one opens.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.s8.l1.5': 'Assuming “no copying” permissions stop a determined person. Only a password to open protects the content.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Is AES-256 encryption secure?',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.1.a': 'AES-256 is a strong, widely used standard. In practice, the password is the weak point, so the strength of your password decides how safe the file is.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Is my PDF or password uploaded when I protect it?',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.2.a': 'No. Protect PDF runs in your browser, so the file and the password stay on your device.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Can I remove a password from a PDF if I forgot it?',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.3.a': 'No. Unlock PDF needs the correct password and does not guess passwords. If you have lost it, the file cannot be opened by this tool.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.4.q': 'Why will my protected PDF not open on an old device?',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.4.a': 'AES-256 requires a reasonably modern PDF reader. Very old programs, from before about 2008, may not support it. Use a current browser or reader.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.5.q': 'What is the difference between protecting and signing a PDF?',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Protecting controls who can open or use the file. Signing places a signature on it. They are separate steps, and signing is best done before you protect the file.',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.cta.h': 'Protect your PDF now',
  'blog.password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.title': 'How to Reduce Image Size in KB Without Blurry Results',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.description': 'Get a photo under an upload limit such as 200 KB or 1 MB. Learn how dimensions, format and quality work together, and how to check the result.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.excerpt': 'Shrink a photo to a target file size by combining the right format, quality and dimensions, without turning it into a blurry mess.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.imageAlt': 'A large photo shrinking into a small file size badge on a teal background',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.intro.1': 'An application form that accepts photos “up to 200 KB”. A shop that rejects product images over 1 MB. A website that slows down because every picture is 4 MB. Phone cameras produce files far larger than most websites and forms want, and “make it smaller” is one of the most common image jobs there is.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.intro.2': 'The problem is doing it without wrecking the picture. This guide explains the three things that decide file size, in the order you should adjust them, so you reach your target with the least visible damage.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.sum.1': 'File size depends on three things: dimensions (pixels), format and quality. Change dimensions first; the compressor uses a fixed, balanced quality.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.sum.2': 'JPG and WebP compress photos far better than PNG, which is lossless.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.sum.3': 'Most upload limits do not need a full-size photo: a width of 1,200 to 2,000 pixels is usually plenty on screen.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.sum.4': 'Check the result at the size it will be used, and keep your original.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s1.h': 'What makes an image file large',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s1.p1': 'A photo is a grid of pixels, and each pixel needs data. A 12-megapixel phone photo has about 12 million pixels, so even compressed it often weighs 3 to 6 MB. Three things control the final size:',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s1.o1.1': 'Dimensions. Halving the width and height leaves a quarter of the pixels, which cuts the size by a lot. This is the most powerful setting and usually the least visible one, because screens rarely show a photo at full camera resolution.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s1.o1.2': 'Format. JPG and WebP use lossy compression made for photographs. PNG is lossless and keeps every pixel exactly, which is ideal for logos and screenshots but gives large files for photos.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s1.o1.3': 'Quality. For lossy formats, quality trades detail for size. Lowering it removes fine detail, and below a certain point blocky artefacts and blur appear. Kitwell’s Compress Image uses one balanced quality and has no quality slider, so you control the size with the dimensions and the format.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s2.h': 'The order that works best',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s2.p1': 'Most people go straight to a quality slider and lower it until the file fits, which is why their pictures end up blurry. A better order is:',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s2.o1.1': 'Reduce the dimensions to the size you actually need.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s2.o1.2': 'Switch to a better format for photos, such as JPG or WebP.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s2.o1.3': 'Only if the file is still too large, reduce the dimensions a little more.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s2.p2': 'Each step costs less visible quality than lowering the quality itself, so you only use the later ones if the earlier ones were not enough.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.h': 'How to do it in your browser',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.p1': '<tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor> works inside your browser, so your pictures are not uploaded. You can add up to 20 images at a time, each up to 25 MB.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.o1.1': 'Add your images.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.o1.2': 'Choose the output format. For photos, JPG or WebP is a good start.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.o1.3': 'Optionally set a maximum width or height. This is the setting that does the heavy lifting.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.o1.4': 'Press Compress. The tool shows the size before and after, so you can see the saving for every image.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.o1.5': 'Download the results, and keep your original files unchanged.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s3.p2': 'If the file is still too large, lower the maximum width a little and run it again from the original, not from the compressed copy. If the tool reports that the result was not smaller, it keeps your original file; reduce the dimensions to get a smaller one.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s4.h': 'Choosing dimensions',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s4.p1': 'Pick the size from where the image will be shown, not from what the camera produced:',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s4.l1.1': 'A picture inside a web page or blog post rarely needs to be wider than about 1,200 to 2,000 pixels.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s4.l1.2': 'A profile picture or avatar often needs only a few hundred pixels on each side.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s4.l1.3': 'An image that will be printed needs more pixels than one that will only be seen on a screen.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s4.l1.4': 'Many forms and portals state exact pixel sizes next to the file size limit. Follow them.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s4.p2': 'To set an exact width and height, or a percentage, use <tool_image_resizer>Resize Image</tool_image_resizer>. Keep the aspect-ratio lock on so the picture is not stretched. Resizing and compressing together gives the best result for the least quality loss.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s5.h': 'Choosing the format',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s5.l1.1': 'Photographs: JPG is the safe, universal choice. WebP gives smaller files at similar quality, but the receiving system must accept it.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s5.l1.2': 'Screenshots, logos and graphics with sharp edges or flat colours: PNG keeps them crisp. If the file is too large, convert it to JPG or WebP, accepting some softness around edges.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s5.l1.3': 'Images with a transparent background: PNG and WebP keep transparency. JPG cannot, so transparent areas are filled with white.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s5.p1': 'If you need a specific format, <tool_png_to_jpg>PNG to JPG</tool_png_to_jpg> and <tool_jpg_to_webp>JPG to WebP</tool_jpg_to_webp> convert with a balanced fixed quality. Our guide to <post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp>JPG, PNG and WebP</post_jpg-vs-png-vs-webp> explains when each is the right choice. If a site does not accept WebP, <tool_webp_to_jpg>WebP to JPG</tool_webp_to_jpg> converts it back.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s6.h': 'Aiming for a specific size, such as 100 KB or 200 KB',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s6.p1': 'There is no way to say “make this exactly 200 KB” in one move, but a short routine gets you there:',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s6.o1.1': 'Set the maximum width to about 1,600 pixels and the format to JPG.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s6.o1.2': 'Compress and read the new size.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s6.o1.3': 'If it is still over, drop the width to 1,200 and compress again from the original.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s6.o1.4': 'If it is far under the limit, raise the width a little for a sharper picture.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s6.o1.5': 'Stop as soon as the file fits. Anything smaller than necessary just costs quality.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s7.h': 'How to judge the result',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s7.p1': 'Open the compressed image at the size it will actually be used. A picture that looks perfect when shrunk to a thumbnail can show blocky patches or smeared edges at full size. Look at fine detail: faces, text in the picture and areas of smooth gradient, such as a sky, which show compression first.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s7.p2': 'If you see banding or blocks, choose a larger maximum width, or keep the original dimensions and accept a bigger file.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s8.h': 'Things to keep in mind',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s8.l1.1': 'Compression removes the camera information stored in the file, such as the location and date, and colour profiles. This is useful for privacy, but it also means colours can shift slightly on some screens.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s8.l1.2': 'Do not compress an image repeatedly. Every lossy round loses detail, so always start from the original.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s8.l1.3': 'Animated GIF or WebP files are converted using their first frame only.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s8.l1.4': 'Output from JPG or WebP shrinks best. PNG output is lossless and may not shrink at all.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s9.h': 'Pictures that end up in a PDF',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.s9.p1': 'If your images are going into a document, compress them before you build it, or compress the finished file. <tool_images_to_pdf>Images to PDF</tool_images_to_pdf> builds the PDF, and <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> shrinks it afterwards. Our guide to <post_convert-images-to-pdf>converting images to PDF</post_convert-images-to-pdf> covers the page size and order.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.1.q': 'Does compressing an image reduce its quality?',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.1.a': 'With JPG and WebP, yes, a little: you trade fine detail for a smaller file. With the tool’s balanced quality and a sensible size, the difference is rarely visible at the size the picture is used.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.2.q': 'What is the best format for a small file size?',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.2.a': 'For photos, WebP or JPG. PNG is lossless and usually the largest. Use PNG only when you need sharp edges or transparency.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.3.q': 'Are my images uploaded when I compress them?',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.3.a': 'No. The tool works inside your browser, so the pictures stay on your device.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.4.q': 'Why did my PNG not get smaller?',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.4.a': 'PNG is lossless, so compressing it with PNG output may save little or nothing. Choose JPG or WebP as the output, or reduce the dimensions. When a file cannot be made smaller, the tool keeps your original.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.5.q': 'How many images can I compress at once?',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.faq.5.a': 'Up to 20 per batch, each up to 25 MB, to keep your browser responsive.',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.cta.h': 'Compress an image now',
  'blog.reduce-image-size-in-kb.cta.p': 'Open <tool_image_compressor>Compress Image</tool_image_compressor>, or browse all of the <all_image>image tools</all_image>.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.title': 'How to Repair a Corrupted PDF That Will Not Open',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.description': 'Learn why PDFs get damaged, which problems a rebuild can fix and which it cannot, and how to repair a broken PDF in your browser without uploading the file.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.excerpt': 'A PDF that will not open is not always lost. See what damage can be repaired, how to try it safely and what to do when it cannot be saved.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.imageAlt': 'A cracked PDF document being mended with a wrench and a green check mark on a teal background',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.intro.1': 'You double-click a PDF and get “the file is damaged and could not be repaired”, a blank window, or a document that opens with half its pages missing. It might be an important report, a download that was interrupted, or an attachment that arrived broken. Before you ask for the file again, it is worth knowing that many of these cases can be repaired.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.intro.2': 'This guide explains how a PDF is built, why it breaks, what a repair can and cannot recover, and how to try it safely.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.sum.1': 'A PDF is a set of objects plus an index at the end; most “corrupted” files have a damaged or missing index, and the pages themselves are often intact.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.sum.2': 'Repair PDF rebuilds the index and page structure in your browser and keeps the pages it can read.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.sum.3': 'It cannot bring back content that is missing from the file, and damage inside compressed object streams cannot be rebuilt.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.sum.4': 'If a file cannot be repaired, the tool says so and does not give you a download it did not create.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.h': 'Why a PDF stops opening',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.p1': 'A PDF file is a collection of objects, such as pages, fonts and images, followed by a cross-reference table that lists where each object is, and a small trailer that points to the table. A viewer reads the trailer first, then uses the table to find everything else.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.p2': 'If the end of the file is damaged, the viewer cannot find the table, and it reports a corrupted file even when every page is still inside. The usual causes:',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.l1.1': 'A download that stopped early, so the file is cut off and the end is missing.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.l1.2': 'An email gateway, a flaky USB drive or a cloud sync that wrote only part of the file.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.l1.3': 'A program that crashed while saving or exporting.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.l1.4': 'Extra data added before the PDF header, for example by a faulty export or transfer tool.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s1.l1.5': 'Real storage errors on a failing disk, which damage the content itself.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s2.h': 'First, quick checks',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s2.p1': 'Before you try a repair, rule out the simple causes:',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s2.o1.1': 'Open the file in a different program, such as your browser or another PDF reader. Some viewers are stricter than others.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s2.o1.2': 'Check the file size. A file that is only a few kilobytes, or far smaller than you expect, was probably cut off.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s2.o1.3': 'Download or copy it again from the source. A second transfer often fixes a damaged one.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s2.o1.4': 'If it is password-protected, make sure you are entering the password correctly. A protected file that fails to open is not necessarily corrupted. With the password, <tool_unlock_pdf>Unlock PDF</tool_unlock_pdf> removes the protection first.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s3.h': 'How to repair a PDF in your browser',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s3.p1': '<tool_repair_pdf>Repair PDF</tool_repair_pdf> works inside your browser tab, so the damaged file is not uploaded anywhere.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s3.o1.1': 'Open the tool and choose the PDF (up to 100 MB). A password-protected file must be unlocked first.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s3.o1.2': 'Start the repair. The tool looks for the objects in the file, rebuilds the cross-reference table and trailer, and when needed gathers the pages it can read into a new page structure.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s3.o1.3': 'Read the report under the result. It lists the problems found, what was done and how many pages are in the repaired file.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s3.o1.4': 'If a file was produced, open it and check the pages before you rely on it, then download it.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.h': 'What a repair can fix',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.p1': 'Repair works on the structure of the file:',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.l1.1': 'A missing or wrong object list (the cross-reference table).',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.l1.2': 'A missing trailer or end-of-file marker.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.l1.3': 'Junk data before the PDF header.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.l1.4': 'A broken page list.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.l1.5': 'A file that was cut off: the pages that were saved before the cut can often be recovered.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s4.p2': 'When the file opens fine but only had small structural problems, the tool says so and gives you a cleaned copy.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s5.h': 'What a repair cannot fix',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s5.p1': 'Be realistic about the limits, because they are physical, not a matter of effort:',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s5.l1.1': 'Content that is no longer in the file. If the end of a long document was never downloaded, those pages do not exist and cannot be reconstructed.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s5.l1.2': 'Damaged pages, images or fonts. A page whose data is broken cannot be brought back, so it may be left out of the repaired file.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s5.l1.3': 'Damage inside compressed object streams, which this tool cannot rebuild.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s5.l1.4': 'Some files are too damaged to save at all. In that case the tool tells you that nothing could be recovered; it never hands you a download that it did not create.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s5.p2': 'A repaired file may therefore have fewer pages than the original, and after a heavy repair the order of the pages can change. Always compare the page count and a few pages with what you expected.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s6.h': 'Checking a repaired file',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s6.p1': 'A repaired PDF needs a quick inspection:',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s6.o1.1': 'Open it and scroll through. Look for blank pages, garbled text and missing images.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s6.o1.2': 'Check the page count against what you expected.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s6.o1.3': 'Search for a word you know is inside. If the text is found, the text layer survived.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s6.o1.4': 'For important documents, ask the sender for a fresh copy as soon as possible, and treat the repaired file as a stop-gap.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s6.p2': 'If you only need the words from a document that opens badly, <tool_extract_pdf_text>Extract Text from PDF</tool_extract_pdf_text> can copy the selectable text out of a readable file, and <tool_pdf_to_jpg>PDF to JPG</tool_pdf_to_jpg> renders the pages as pictures.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s7.h': 'After the repair',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s7.l1.1': 'A damaged file is often larger than it needs to be, so run <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> on the repaired copy if size matters. Our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> covers the options.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s7.l1.2': 'If you were about to combine files, repair the broken one first and then use <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>; <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>how to merge PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains the order and limits.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s8.h': 'How to avoid damaged PDFs',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s8.l1.1': 'Let downloads finish and compare the file size with what the source states.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s8.l1.2': 'Do not unplug a drive while a file is being saved or copied.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s8.l1.3': 'Keep a second copy of important documents in another place.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s8.l1.4': 'Avoid renaming a file’s extension to change its type; a file with the wrong extension is a common cause of “cannot open”.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.s8.l1.5': 'Export from the program again if you can. A fresh export is better than any repair.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.1.q': 'Can every corrupted PDF be repaired?',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.1.a': 'No. Structural damage, such as a missing index or a cut-off end, can often be repaired. Missing or broken content cannot be recreated, and some files are too damaged to save. The tool tells you when it cannot produce a usable file.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.2.q': 'Is my PDF uploaded when I repair it?',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.2.a': 'No. Repair PDF works inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.3.q': 'Why does the repaired PDF have fewer pages?',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.3.a': 'Pages whose data was missing or damaged cannot be recovered, so they are left out. The report under the result states how many pages were kept.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.4.q': 'What if my PDF opens but looks wrong?',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.4.a': 'Then it may not be corrupted at all. The tool will report that no repair was needed and give you a cleaned copy. Wrong-looking text or missing fonts usually come from the program that created the file.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.5.q': 'Can it repair a password-protected PDF?',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.faq.5.a': 'Not directly. Unlock the file first with the password, then repair the unlocked copy.',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.cta.h': 'Try to repair your PDF',
  'blog.repair-a-corrupted-pdf.cta.p': 'Open <tool_repair_pdf>Repair PDF</tool_repair_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.title': 'How to Sign a PDF Without Printing (and What It Means)',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.description': 'Draw, type or upload a signature and place it on a PDF in your browser. Learn how a visual signature differs from a digital signature.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.excerpt': 'Sign a PDF on your computer or phone without printing or scanning, and understand what a visual signature does and does not prove.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.imageAlt': 'A pen drawing a signature line onto a PDF page on a teal background',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.intro.1': 'The classic routine is silly: print the form, sign it with a pen, scan it again, and email the scan. It wastes paper and time, and the scan often comes out crooked and grey. Placing your signature on the PDF directly is faster and looks cleaner.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.intro.2': 'But there is something to know before you do it. “Signing a PDF” can mean two different things, and which one you need depends on who asked for the signature. This guide shows how to add a signature in your browser, and explains the difference so you do not send the wrong kind.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.sum.1': 'Sign PDF places a picture of your signature on the pages you choose; you can draw it, type your name or upload an image.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.sum.2': 'It is a visual signature: no certificate, no timestamp and no tamper detection.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.sum.3': 'Many everyday forms accept it; contracts that require a digital or qualified signature do not.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.sum.4': 'The file is processed in your browser and not uploaded; password-protected PDFs must be unlocked first.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s1.h': 'Two kinds of PDF signature',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s1.p1': 'A visual signature is an image of your handwritten signature, or of your typed name, placed on the page. It looks like a signature and is accepted wherever a scanned, hand-signed copy is accepted: many internal forms, school and club documents, delivery receipts, consent slips and simple agreements.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s1.p2': 'A digital signature is a different technology. It is created with a certificate that identifies you, it records the time, and it makes the document show a warning if anyone changes it afterwards. It is used where the law or a contract requires proof of who signed and that the document did not change.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s1.p3': 'Sign PDF creates the first kind. It does not create certificates, timestamps or tamper-evident signatures, and it will not fill a signature form field. If the person asking for your signature needs a digital or qualified signature, ask which service or format they accept before you sign.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s2.h': 'How to sign a PDF in your browser',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s2.p1': '<tool_sign_pdf>Sign PDF</tool_sign_pdf> runs on your own device. The document and your signature never leave your browser.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s2.o1.1': 'Add the PDF you need to sign (up to 100 MB). If it is password-protected, unlock it first.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s2.o1.2': 'Create your signature. Draw it with the mouse, a trackpad or your finger, type your name, or upload a picture of a signature.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s2.o1.3': 'Drag the signature to the right place on the page. Resize it so it fits the signature line.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s2.o1.4': 'Choose which pages get the signature. Many documents need it on one page; some need initials on every page.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s2.o1.5': 'Download the signed PDF and open it to check the position.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s3.h': 'Making a signature that looks good',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s3.l1.1': 'Draw on a touch screen if you can. A finger or stylus gives a more natural line than a mouse.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s3.l1.2': 'If you upload a picture, sign with a dark pen on plain white paper, take a straight, well-lit photo and crop it tightly. A messy photo leaves grey edges around the signature.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s3.l1.3': 'Keep it a sensible size. A signature that overlaps the printed line or other text looks careless.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s3.l1.4': 'Use the same style each time, so your signature stays recognisable.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s4.h': 'What the signature is, technically',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s4.p1': 'The signature is placed as a picture on top of the page. That has two practical consequences. It does not become part of a signature field in the document, and anyone with the right tools could remove or move it, because nothing protects it. The tool is honest about this limit so that you can choose the right method for important documents.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s4.n1': 'Tip: for anything that matters, keep the unsigned original and the signed copy together, and note the date you signed.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s5.h': 'When a visual signature is enough, and when it is not',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s5.p1': 'Usually enough: forms inside a company or school, rental or club applications where a scanned signature is accepted, documents the recipient will print anyway, and receipts or acknowledgements with no legal weight.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s5.p2': 'Ask first: loans, property documents, contracts of employment, tax or government filings. Some require an electronic signature service or a certificate. Others say explicitly that a scan of a hand-signed page is fine. The sender knows, so ask before you sign.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s6.h': 'Before and after signing',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s6.p1': 'Prepare the file first. If the document is a Word file, convert it to PDF before signing; our guide to <post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting>converting Word to PDF</post_word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting> explains what to expect. If it is a bundle, join the pieces with <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>; our guide to <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>merging PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files> explains the order. If only one page needs a signature, <tool_extract_pdf_pages>Extract PDF Pages</tool_extract_pdf_pages> can pull that page out as its own file.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s6.p2': 'After signing, check the file size, since a document with scans can be large. <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf> makes it smaller; our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains the options.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s6.p3': 'If the signed document should only be read by one person, add a password last with <tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf>. Our guide to <post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf>protecting and unlocking a PDF</post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf> explains why the order matters: sign first, protect last. If the file already has a password, remove it with <tool_unlock_pdf>Unlock PDF</tool_unlock_pdf> before signing.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s7.h': 'Keeping your signature safe',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.s7.p1': 'Your signature image is a piece of personal information. Do not leave copies on shared computers, and do not paste it into documents you do not control. This tool does not store your signature, because it never leaves your browser; close the tab and it is gone.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.1.q': 'Is a signature added with Sign PDF legally valid?',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.1.a': 'It depends on the document and the country. It is a visual signature, comparable to a scanned hand-signed copy. It has no certificate or timestamp. For contracts that need a digital or qualified signature, ask the other party what they accept.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.2.q': 'Can I sign on my phone?',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.2.a': 'Yes. The tool works in a mobile browser, and drawing with a finger works well. Large documents are slower on phones because your device does the work.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.3.q': 'Is my signature or document uploaded anywhere?',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.3.a': 'No. Everything happens in your browser, and nothing is sent to a server.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.4.q': 'Can I put the signature on every page?',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.4.a': 'Yes. You choose which pages receive it, which is useful for initials on every page.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.5.q': 'Why can I not open my PDF in the tool?',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.faq.5.a': 'It may be password-protected. Remove the password with Unlock PDF, sign the unlocked copy and protect it again afterwards if needed.',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.cta.h': 'Sign your PDF now',
  'blog.sign-a-pdf-without-printing.cta.p': 'Open <tool_sign_pdf>Sign PDF</tool_sign_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.title': 'Unix Timestamps Explained: Convert Them to Dates and Back',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.description': 'Understand Unix timestamps in seconds and milliseconds, convert them to UTC, local time and ISO 8601 dates, and avoid the usual time zone mistakes.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.excerpt': 'What a Unix timestamp is, how to tell seconds from milliseconds, how to read one as a date in any time zone and how to avoid the mistakes that make events appear at the wrong hour.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.imageAlt': 'A clock face next to a long number representing a Unix timestamp on a teal background',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.intro.1': 'If you have ever looked at a log file, a database export or an API response and seen a number like 1700000000 where a date should be, you have met a Unix timestamp. It is one of the most common ways for computers to store a moment in time, and it is short, easy to sort and the same all over the world.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.intro.2': 'The number is not friendly for people. This guide explains what it means, how to tell whether it counts seconds or milliseconds, how to turn it into a readable date in any time zone and how to avoid the mistakes that place an event at the wrong hour.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.sum.1': 'A Unix timestamp counts the seconds since 1 January 1970 at 00:00 UTC. It does not depend on a time zone.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.sum.2': 'Timestamps with 13 or more digits are normally milliseconds, and shorter ones are seconds.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.sum.3': 'The same timestamp shows different clock times in different time zones, but it is always the same moment.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.sum.4': 'ISO 8601 is the clear way to write a date and time as text, for example 2023-11-14T22:13:20Z.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.sum.5': 'Always store and send times in UTC, and convert to local time only when you show them to people.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s1.h': 'What a Unix timestamp is',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s1.p1': 'A Unix timestamp, also called epoch time or POSIX time, is the number of seconds that have passed since a fixed starting point: midnight UTC on 1 January 1970. That starting point is called the epoch. The timestamp 0 is that exact moment, and 86400, the number of seconds in a day, is one day later.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s1.p2': 'Because it is a single number, a timestamp is easy to store, to compare and to subtract. To know how long something took, subtract the start from the end. To sort events, sort the numbers. There are no months of different lengths, no daylight saving changes and no time zones to worry about.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s2.h': 'Seconds or milliseconds',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s2.p1': 'Many systems count in seconds, and many others, including JavaScript and several databases, count in milliseconds. A timestamp in milliseconds is a thousand times larger.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s2.p2': 'A simple rule works for dates in the present era. A timestamp of 10 digits, such as 1700000000, is in seconds. A timestamp of 13 digits, such as 1700000000000, is in milliseconds. <tool_timestamp_converter>Timestamp Converter</tool_timestamp_converter> uses this rule automatically: values with 13 or more digits are treated as milliseconds and shorter ones as seconds. You can also choose seconds or milliseconds yourself when the automatic choice is wrong, for example for dates very far in the past or future.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s2.p3': 'If a date shows up in 1970, you have almost certainly fed milliseconds to something that expects seconds, or the other way round. If a date shows up thousands of years in the future, you have done the opposite.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s3.h': 'Reading a timestamp as a date',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s3.p1': 'The converter turns a timestamp into three readable forms.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s3.o1.1': 'UTC, the universal reference time.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s3.o1.2': 'Your local time zone, taken from your device, or any other time zone that you choose from the list.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s3.o1.3': 'An ISO 8601 text, which is a standard way to write date and time.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s3.p2': 'As an example, the timestamp 1700000000 is 22:13:20 on 14 November 2023 in UTC. In a time zone five hours ahead of UTC, the same moment is 03:13:20 on 15 November, which is a different day on the calendar. Both are the same instant.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s3.p3': 'The tool also shows the current Unix time in seconds and milliseconds, which is handy when you need a timestamp for "now" in a test.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s4.h': 'Turning a date into a timestamp',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s4.p1': 'The reverse works too. Pick a date and time, choose the time zone in which you mean it and read the timestamp. The time zone matters: 9 o\'clock in one city is not 9 o\'clock in another, so the same wall-clock time gives different timestamps.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s4.p2': 'This is useful when you need to set an expiry time, schedule something through an API or write a query that selects rows between two moments.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s5.h': 'ISO 8601, the clear text format',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s5.p1': 'When a time has to be written as text, use ISO 8601. The date comes first with the year, then the month and the day, followed by a T and the time. A Z at the end means UTC, while an offset such as plus 05:00 shows the distance from UTC.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s5.p2': 'The order of year, month and day sorts correctly as plain text, and it avoids the confusion between formats such as 03/04/2026, which is the third of April in some countries and the fourth of March in others. Use it in file names, logs and data exchange.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s6.h': 'Time zones and daylight saving',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s6.p1': 'Most mistakes with time come from time zones and daylight saving. A few rules prevent them.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s6.l1.1': 'Store times in UTC or as timestamps, and convert only for display.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s6.l1.2': 'Never store a local time without its zone. A time without a zone is ambiguous, especially during the hour that repeats when clocks go back.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s6.l1.3': 'Use zone names such as Asia/Karachi or Europe/Berlin, not fixed offsets, when you need to show local time, because offsets change with daylight saving.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s6.l1.4': 'Remember that some days have 23 or 25 hours in places that change their clocks.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s6.p2': 'The converter lets you view the same timestamp in any zone, which makes it a quick way to check what time an event happens for people in another country.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s7.h': 'Limits and the year 2038',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s7.p1': 'Timestamps in JavaScript can describe dates from roughly the year minus 271821 to the year 275760, which is the range this converter supports. That is far more than anyone needs.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s7.p2': 'Older systems store the timestamp in a 32-bit signed number, which runs out on 19 January 2038 at 03:14:07 UTC. This is called the year 2038 problem. Modern software uses 64-bit numbers, so it is not a concern for new work, but you should know that old databases and embedded devices may still be affected.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s8.h': 'Where you meet timestamps',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s8.p1': 'Timestamps appear in many places:',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s8.l1.1': 'API responses and JSON files, where fields such as created and expires hold numbers. <tool_json_formatter>JSON Formatter</tool_json_formatter> makes these files readable, and our guide to <post_how-to-format-and-validate-json>formatting and validating JSON</post_how-to-format-and-validate-json> explains how.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s8.l1.2': 'Log files, where each line begins with a time.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s8.l1.3': 'Login tokens, which often carry an expiry time. If you decode one, <tool_base64_encoder_decoder>Base64 Encoder / Decoder</tool_base64_encoder_decoder> helps you read its parts, and our guide to <post_what-is-base64-encoding>Base64 encoding</post_what-is-base64-encoding> explains why they look the way they do.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s8.l1.4': 'Databases and spreadsheets, where a column of numbers can be read as dates.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s8.p2': 'If you have to cut timestamps out of a long log, <tool_regex_tester>Regex Tester</tool_regex_tester> helps you build a pattern for them. Timestamps are not the same as unique identifiers: if you need an identifier for a record, <tool_uuid_generator>UUID Generator</tool_uuid_generator> makes random ones.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s9.h': 'Checking your data',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s9.p1': 'When a timestamp field lives inside a JSON file, check the file first. <tool_json_validator>JSON Validator</tool_json_validator> tells you the line and column of any error, so you can fix the file before you hunt for a time problem that is really a syntax problem.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s9.p2': 'Timestamps also appear in XML feeds and sitemaps, where dates are written as ISO 8601 text. Our guide to <post_how-to-format-and-validate-xml>formatting and validating XML</post_how-to-format-and-validate-xml> shows how to read those files.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s9.p3': 'Large numbers can cause trouble too. JavaScript stores numbers in a form that loses precision above a very large size, so a timestamp in nanoseconds can lose its last digits when parsed. Keep such values as text if exactness matters.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s10.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s10.l1.1': 'Mixing up seconds and milliseconds.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s10.l1.2': 'Storing local times without a time zone.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s10.l1.3': 'Using a fixed offset instead of a zone name for a place that changes its clocks.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s10.l1.4': 'Reading a timestamp on a computer with the wrong time zone and trusting the result.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s10.l1.5': 'Writing dates in an ambiguous format instead of ISO 8601.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s11.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s11.o1.1': 'Count the digits: 10 means seconds and 13 means milliseconds.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s11.o1.2': 'Convert and check UTC first, then the local zone.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s11.o1.3': 'Write times as ISO 8601 text, with a Z or an offset.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s11.o1.4': 'Store UTC, and convert only when you show the time.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.s11.o1.5': 'Test with a date near a daylight saving change.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.1.q': 'Seconds or milliseconds?',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.1.a': 'Timestamps with 13 or more digits are treated as milliseconds, shorter ones as seconds. You can override this manually.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.2.q': 'What is the range of dates the converter supports?',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.2.a': 'The range of JavaScript dates: roughly years minus 271821 to 275760.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.3.q': 'Does a Unix timestamp depend on the time zone?',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.3.a': 'No. It counts seconds since 1970 in UTC. Only the way it is shown depends on the time zone.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.4.q': 'What is the year 2038 problem?',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.4.a': 'Systems that store the timestamp in a 32-bit signed number run out on 19 January 2038. Modern systems use 64-bit numbers.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.5.q': 'Can I see the current Unix time?',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.faq.5.a': 'Yes. The tool shows the current time in seconds and in milliseconds.',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.unix-timestamps-explained.cta.p': 'Open <tool_timestamp_converter>Timestamp Converter</tool_timestamp_converter>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.title': 'What Is Base64 Encoding? Uses, Limits and Examples',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.description': 'Learn what Base64 is, why it makes data a third larger, where it is used and why it is not encryption, and how to encode and decode in your browser.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.excerpt': 'Base64 turns any data into plain text characters. Learn where it is used, what it costs and what it is not: a way to keep secrets.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.imageAlt': 'Binary ones and zeros turning into a string of letters and digits on a teal background',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.intro.1': 'You open an email source, an API response or a CSS file and meet a long run of letters and digits ending in one or two equals signs. That is probably Base64. It shows up in web pages, tokens, configuration files and email attachments, usually without anyone explaining what it is.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.intro.2': 'This guide explains Base64 in plain language: what problem it solves, how it works, what it costs, and the most important thing to remember about it, which is that it is encoding, not encryption.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.sum.1': 'Base64 represents any data using only 64 safe text characters, so it can travel through systems built for text.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.sum.2': 'The encoded text is about 33% larger than the original data.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.sum.3': 'It is not encryption: anyone can decode it, so never use it to hide a secret.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.sum.4': 'Text, and images through the dedicated tools, can be encoded and decoded in your browser without uploading anything.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s1.h': 'The problem Base64 solves',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s1.p1': 'Many systems were designed to carry text, not raw bytes. Email, parts of URLs, JSON and HTML can break, or alter data, when they meet arbitrary binary values such as an image or a file. Control characters get stripped, line breaks get changed, and bytes that are not valid text get mangled.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s1.p2': 'Base64 avoids the problem by translating any sequence of bytes into a limited alphabet of 64 characters that every text system handles safely: the capital letters, the lowercase letters, the digits, plus and slash. The result is plain text that can be copied, pasted, stored in JSON or placed in an email without damage, and later turned back into the exact original bytes.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s2.h': 'How it works, in short',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s2.p1': 'Base64 takes the data three bytes at a time. Three bytes are 24 bits, which it splits into four groups of 6 bits. Each 6-bit group is a number from 0 to 63, and each number is mapped to one character of the alphabet. Three bytes in, four characters out.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s2.p2': 'When the data does not divide evenly by three, the end is padded with one or two equals signs. That is why many Base64 strings finish with = or ==. A variant called URL-safe Base64 swaps plus and slash for minus and underscore, so the text can sit inside a web address, and often leaves out the padding.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s3.h': 'Why the result is bigger',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s3.p1': 'Four characters for every three bytes means the output is about one third larger than the input, plus a little for padding and any line breaks. A 300 KB image becomes roughly 400 KB of text. This is the price of using only safe characters, and it is the main reason not to Base64-encode large files when you can avoid it.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s4.h': 'Where you meet Base64',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s4.l1.1': 'Images and fonts embedded directly in HTML or CSS as data URIs, which start with data: followed by a type and base64.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s4.l1.2': 'Email attachments, which are encoded so they can travel inside text messages.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s4.l1.3': 'Web tokens such as JWTs, whose parts use the URL-safe variant.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s4.l1.4': 'HTTP basic authentication headers, where the username and password are encoded, not protected.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s4.l1.5': 'Binary values stored inside JSON or XML, which cannot hold raw bytes.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s4.l1.6': 'Certificates and keys in text formats.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s5.h': 'Base64 is not encryption',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s5.p1': 'This point causes real security mistakes. Base64 is a public, reversible way of writing data. Anyone can decode it in a second, with no key or password. Encoding a password or a token in Base64 hides nothing.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s5.p2': 'If something must stay secret, it needs real encryption, or better, it should never be put in the page, URL or message at all. If you see Base64 in a header or a configuration file, assume that whoever can read the file can read the value.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s6.h': 'How to encode and decode text in your browser',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s6.p1': '<tool_base64_encoder_decoder>Base64 Encoder / Decoder</tool_base64_encoder_decoder> works entirely in your browser, so what you paste is not sent to a server. It handles the full range of text, including accented letters, scripts such as Arabic, Chinese or Cyrillic, and emoji, because it encodes text as UTF-8 first.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s6.o1.1': 'Choose Encode or Decode.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s6.o1.2': 'Paste your text.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s6.o1.3': 'Copy the result.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s6.p2': 'If decoding fails, the input is not valid Base64. Common causes are stray spaces or line breaks, a missing character, or text that is actually URL-safe Base64 using minus and underscore. Remove surrounding whitespace and check the ending first.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s7.h': 'Images and Base64',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s7.p1': 'An image can be turned into a Base64 string and back. <tool_image_to_base64>Image to Base64</tool_image_to_base64> produces the text you can place in HTML or CSS, and <tool_base64_to_image>Base64 to Image</tool_base64_to_image> turns such a string back into a picture you can save. This is handy for tiny icons you want to embed to save a request. For anything larger than a few kilobytes, a normal image file is better: it loads separately, can be cached by the browser and does not add a third to its size inside your page. If the picture is too heavy to embed, shrink it first; our guide to <post_reduce-image-size-in-kb>reducing image size in KB</post_reduce-image-size-in-kb> shows how.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s8.h': 'Base64, URL encoding and JSON',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s8.p1': 'These three are often confused, but they solve different problems:',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s8.l1.1': 'Base64 turns arbitrary bytes into safe text.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s8.l1.2': 'URL encoding, also called percent-encoding, makes text safe to place inside a web address, replacing unsafe characters with a percent sign and two digits. <tool_url_encoder_decoder>URL Encoder / Decoder</tool_url_encoder_decoder> does it.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s8.l1.3': 'JSON is a structure for data. A JSON string can hold a Base64 value, and our guide to <post_how-to-format-and-validate-json>formatting and validating JSON</post_how-to-format-and-validate-json> shows how to keep it valid.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s8.p2': 'Text that contains HTML special characters is a fourth case, handled by <tool_html_encoder_decoder>HTML Encoder / Decoder</tool_html_encoder_decoder>.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s9.h': 'Common mistakes',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s9.l1.1': 'Treating Base64 as a way to keep a password or token private.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s9.l1.2': 'Embedding large images as Base64 in a page, which bloats it and cannot be cached separately.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s9.l1.3': 'Forgetting that the data grows by about a third when you store or send it.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s9.l1.4': 'Copying a string with a line break or space in the middle, which makes decoding fail.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.s9.l1.5': 'Mixing the standard and URL-safe alphabets and then wondering why a decoder rejects the text.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.1.q': 'Is Base64 a form of encryption?',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.1.a': 'No. It is an encoding that anyone can reverse without a key. Never use it to protect a secret.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.2.q': 'Why does Base64 make data bigger?',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.2.a': 'It uses four text characters for every three bytes of data, so the output is about 33% larger than the input.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.3.q': 'What do the equals signs at the end mean?',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.3.a': 'They are padding. They fill the last group when the data length is not a multiple of three bytes, so you will see one or two of them at the end of many strings.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.4.q': 'Can Base64 handle non-English text and emoji?',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.4.a': 'Yes. The text is converted to UTF-8 bytes before it is encoded, so any language and emoji can be encoded and decoded correctly.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.5.q': 'Is my text uploaded when I use the tool?',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.faq.5.a': 'No. The encoding and decoding happen in your browser and nothing is sent to a server.',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.cta.h': 'Encode or decode now',
  'blog.what-is-base64-encoding.cta.p': 'Open <tool_base64_encoder_decoder>Base64 Encoder / Decoder</tool_base64_encoder_decoder>, or browse all of the <all_developer>developer tools</all_developer>.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.title': 'What Is PDF/A? Versions, Uses and How to Convert Safely',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.description': 'PDF/A is the archival version of PDF. Learn what 1b, 2b, 2u, 3b, 3u and 4 mean, when you need it and why a real converter must never fake compliance.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.excerpt': 'Understand PDF/A for long-term archiving: what the standard guarantees, how the versions differ, why fonts and colours can block a conversion and how to validate the result.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.imageAlt': 'A PDF document with an archive badge and a checkmark on a teal background',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.intro.1': 'Documents have a way of outliving the software that made them. A contract signed in 2026 might need to be opened in 2046, by a different program on a different computer. Ordinary PDFs usually survive, but they are allowed to depend on things outside the file: fonts installed on your machine, links to external content, even scripts. PDF/A exists to remove those dependencies.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.intro.2': 'This guide explains what PDF/A is, what its version names mean, when you really need it and how to convert a file without pretending that it complies when it does not.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.sum.1': 'PDF/A is a restricted form of PDF made for long-term archiving: everything needed to display the file must be inside it.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.sum.2': 'The names such as 1b, 2b, 2u, 3b, 3u and 4 describe the generation of the standard and the level of conformance.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.sum.3': 'A real conversion either meets the rules or says clearly why it cannot; it never relabels an ordinary PDF.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.sum.4': 'Fonts that are not embedded, CMYK colour without a profile and some compression methods can block a conversion that keeps the text.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.sum.5': 'Check important files with an independent validator such as veraPDF.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s1.h': 'What PDF/A guarantees',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s1.p1': 'PDF/A is an ISO standard family for archiving. A conforming file is self-contained and predictable:',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s1.l1.1': 'All fonts used on the pages are embedded, so the text looks the same everywhere.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s1.l1.2': 'Colours are defined in a way that does not depend on the display device.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s1.l1.3': 'There is no JavaScript, no launching of external programs and no encryption.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s1.l1.4': 'Metadata is stored in a standard form, so software can identify the file for what it is.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s1.p2': 'The result is a document designed to look the same decades from now. The price is that some PDF features are not allowed, and the files can be larger.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.h': 'Understanding the version names',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.p1': 'A name such as PDF/A-2b has two parts: a number and a letter.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.p2': 'The number is the generation of the standard:',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.l1.1': 'PDF/A-1 is the oldest, based on PDF 1.4. It is strict: no transparency and no layers.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.l1.2': 'PDF/A-2 is based on PDF 1.7 and allows more features, such as transparency and compressed object structures.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.l1.3': 'PDF/A-3 has the same rules as 2 but also allows files embedded as attachments.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.l1.4': 'PDF/A-4 is the newest, based on PDF 2.0.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.p3': 'The letter is the level of conformance:',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.l2.1': 'b (basic) means the file will look the same.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.l2.2': 'u (Unicode) adds a guarantee that the text can be copied and searched reliably, because every font maps its characters to Unicode.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.l2.3': 'a (accessible) adds a tagged document structure for accessibility. A tagged structure cannot be created automatically from an arbitrary PDF, so it is not offered here.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s2.p4': 'PDF/A-4 also has variants for 3D content and attachments, called 4e and 4f, which are not offered either.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.h': 'Which version should you choose?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.p1': 'Start from the requirement. If an authority, archive or client names a version, use that one. If not:',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.l1.1': 'PDF/A-2b is the most widely accepted and the sensible default.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.l1.2': 'PDF/A-1b is for systems that insist on the oldest version.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.l1.3': 'The u versions, 2u and 3u, are a good choice when text must remain searchable and copyable.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.l1.4': 'PDF/A-3b matches 2b and exists for workflows that attach source files.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.l1.5': 'PDF/A-4 is for organisations that have moved to the newest standard.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s3.p2': 'If you do not know, choose 2b.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s4.h': 'How to convert in your browser',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s4.p1': '<tool_pdf_to_pdfa>PDF to PDF/A</tool_pdf_to_pdfa> works in your browser tab, so the file is read from your device.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s4.o1.1': 'Open the tool and add your PDF (up to 100 MB). A password-protected file must be unlocked first with <tool_unlock_pdf>Unlock PDF</tool_unlock_pdf>, because a PDF/A file cannot be encrypted (see <post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf>protecting and unlocking a PDF</post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf>).',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s4.o1.2': 'Choose the PDF/A version.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s4.o1.3': 'Choose how to convert: keep the text and fonts, or turn the pages into pictures.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s4.o1.4': 'Convert, read the list of what was done and download the file.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s5.h': 'Keep text, or turn pages into pictures?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s5.p1': 'The first mode changes only the structure and metadata of the file. The text stays selectable and the file stays small. It works only when the file already meets the content rules: every font embedded, no CMYK colour without a profile, no LZW compression, and annotations with an appearance. For the oldest version, there must also be no transparency, no layers and no JPEG 2000 images, and for the u versions and PDF/A-4 every font needs a text mapping.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s5.p2': 'The second mode renders each page as an image and builds a new PDF/A from the pictures. It always works, up to 200 pages, but the text can no longer be selected and the file becomes larger. If you need searchable text again, you can run <tool_ocr_pdf>OCR PDF</tool_ocr_pdf> on a copy, but the result is then no longer the exact PDF/A you validated.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s6.h': 'Why a real converter says no',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s6.p1': 'Many online converters promise PDF/A and simply change the label inside the file. That is dangerous: the file claims a guarantee it does not meet. A proper tool refuses instead. When Kitwell cannot keep your text and still meet the rules, it lists the reasons, for example the names of fonts that are not embedded, and offers the picture mode.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s6.p2': 'If a file seems damaged rather than non-compliant, try <tool_repair_pdf>Repair PDF</tool_repair_pdf> first. Our guide to <post_repair-a-corrupted-pdf>repairing a corrupted PDF</post_repair-a-corrupted-pdf> explains what can be recovered.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s7.h': 'What the tool changes',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s7.p1': 'To make a file conform, the tool adds the PDF/A identification and XMP metadata, an sRGB colour profile as the output intent and a file identifier. It removes things that the standard forbids: JavaScript, forbidden actions, attachments, extra document information and annotation settings that are not allowed. Before it hands you the file, it checks its own work against the rules it applies.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s8.h': 'Validation: do not just take our word for it',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s8.p1': 'The browser tool does not include a full PDF/A validator, so it cannot certify its result. During development, files produced in each of the offered versions were checked with veraPDF, a widely used open-source validator, and passed. If you must prove compliance, run your own file through veraPDF or another validator. This is standard practice for archives and legal deposits.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s9.h': 'Common mistakes to avoid',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s9.l1.1': 'Assuming that a PDF/A label means the file was validated.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s9.l1.2': 'Choosing a version the receiving system does not accept.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s9.l1.3': 'Converting a password-protected file without unlocking it first.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s9.l1.4': 'Throwing away the original after turning pages into pictures.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s9.l1.5': 'Archiving a huge file without compressing it first; <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains the trade-offs.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s9.l1.6': 'Ignoring the document properties. <tool_edit_pdf_metadata>Edit PDF Metadata</tool_edit_pdf_metadata> lets you review the title and author before archiving.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s10.h': 'A quick checklist',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s10.o1.1': 'Find out which PDF/A version the recipient requires.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s10.o1.2': 'Unlock the file if needed.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s10.o1.3': 'Try keep-text mode first; use picture mode only if it is refused and the text can be given up.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s10.o1.4': 'Validate important files with veraPDF.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.s10.o1.5': 'Keep the original PDF as well as the archive copy.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.1.q': 'Is the result a certified PDF/A file?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.1.a': 'No. Kitwell prepares your PDF in the version you choose and checks its own work against the rules it applies. Test files passed the veraPDF validator, but the browser tool itself does not include a full PDF/A validator.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.2.q': 'Which PDF/A version should I choose?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.2.a': 'PDF/A-2b is the most widely accepted and the default. Choose another version only if the receiving system asks for it.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.3.q': 'Why was my PDF refused when I chose to keep the text?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.3.a': 'PDF/A needs every font embedded and does not allow CMYK colour without a profile, LZW compression or annotations without an appearance. These cannot be fixed without changing the pages, so the tool lists what it found.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.4.q': 'Does converting to PDF/A make the file larger?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.4.a': 'Usually a little, because fonts and a colour profile are embedded. Picture mode makes files much larger.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.5.q': 'Can a PDF/A file be password-protected?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.5.a': 'No. Encryption is not allowed in PDF/A, so remove the password first.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.6.q': 'Is my PDF uploaded?',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.faq.6.a': 'No. The tool runs inside your browser, so the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.cta.h': 'Try it yourself',
  'blog.what-is-pdf-a-and-when-to-use-it.cta.p': 'Open <tool_pdf_to_pdfa>PDF to PDF/A</tool_pdf_to_pdfa>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.title': 'Word to PDF Without Losing Formatting: What to Expect',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.description': 'Convert a DOCX file to PDF in your browser. See what is kept, what is approximated, and how to prepare your document so the PDF comes out clean.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.excerpt': 'An honest look at converting Word documents to PDF in the browser: what carries over, what changes and how to prepare the file.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.imageAlt': 'A Word document page transforming into a PDF page with a red PDF label on a teal background',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.intro.1': 'A PDF looks the same on every device, which is why people ask for one when you send a CV, a contract, an assignment or an invoice. A Word file can look different on another computer, with different fonts and shifted page breaks. Converting to PDF fixes the look in place.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.intro.2': 'The catch is that no converter reproduces a Word document exactly unless it is Word itself. This guide explains what a browser-based conversion keeps, what it only approximates, and how to prepare your document so that the PDF comes out as close as possible to what you expect.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.sum.1': 'Word to PDF converts DOCX files; old .doc files and password-protected documents are not supported.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.sum.2': 'Text formatting, headings, lists, tables, links and JPEG and PNG pictures are kept.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.sum.3': 'Headers, footers, footnotes, text boxes, equations, charts and shapes are not reproduced, and fonts are replaced by Noto Sans.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.sum.4': 'The tool lists what it could not convert, so check the result before you send it.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s1.h': 'If you have Word, use its own export first',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s1.p1': 'If Microsoft Word or a compatible office program is installed on your computer, its built-in Save as PDF (or Export) gives the most faithful result, because the same program that laid out the document writes the PDF. Nothing else can do better.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s1.p2': 'A browser tool is for the other situations: you are on a computer or phone without Word, you only have the file, you do not want to install anything, or you need a quick conversion of a simple document. In those cases <tool_word_to_pdf>Word to PDF</tool_word_to_pdf> does the job, as long as you know its limits.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s2.h': 'How to convert a Word file to PDF',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s2.p1': 'The conversion runs in your browser, so the document is not uploaded anywhere.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s2.o1.1': 'Open the tool and choose your DOCX file (up to 50 MB).',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s2.o1.2': 'Pick the page size. You can keep the size set in the document or choose another one.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s2.o1.3': 'Press Convert. The tool reads the document, lays out the pages and builds the PDF.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s2.o1.4': 'Read the notes under the result. If something could not be converted, they tell you what and how many.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s2.o1.5': 'Download the PDF and open it before you send it.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.h': 'What is kept',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.p1': 'The converter reads the content and structure of the document and lays it out again. These parts carry over:',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.l1.1': 'Bold, italic, underlined text and hyperlinks.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.l1.2': 'Headings and paragraph alignment.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.l1.3': 'Bullet and numbered lists.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.l1.4': 'Tables.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.l1.5': 'Pictures in JPEG and PNG format.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.l1.6': 'Line breaks, page breaks and the page orientation.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s3.p2': 'Because the text stays real text, you can select, search and copy it in the PDF.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.h': 'What is approximated or left out',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.p1': 'Be realistic about these differences, and check them in the result:',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.l1.1': 'Fonts. The original fonts are replaced by Noto Sans, so line breaks and page breaks will fall in different places than in Word.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.l1.2': 'Layout features such as columns and text that wraps around a picture are not reproduced.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.l1.3': 'Headers, footers and footnotes are not included.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.l1.4': 'Text boxes, equations, charts, shapes and pictures in formats other than JPEG and PNG are skipped.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.l1.5': 'Scripts that Noto Sans does not cover, such as Arabic, Hebrew, Thai, Chinese, Japanese and Korean, appear as question marks. The tool tells you how many characters were affected.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s4.p2': 'The tool counts what it skipped and says so under the result, so you are never left guessing why a table or a picture is missing.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.h': 'Prepare the document for a cleaner PDF',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.p1': 'A few minutes of preparation in Word makes the PDF better:',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.o1.1': 'Use real headings and lists instead of manual spacing and typed bullets. Structure converts well; hand-drawn spacing does not.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.o1.2': 'Put pictures in line with the text, and save them as JPEG or PNG. Floating pictures and other formats may not carry over.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.o1.3': 'Move important information out of headers, footers and text boxes into the main text.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.o1.4': 'Replace special equations or charts with a picture saved as PNG or JPG.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.o1.5': 'Save the file in the current DOCX format. If your file is an old .doc, open it in Word and use Save As, then choose DOCX.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s5.n1': 'Tip: convert a short test first. If the first page looks right, the rest almost always will too.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s6.h': 'Page size: keep the document’s or choose one',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s6.p1': 'By default the PDF uses the page size stored in the document, such as A4 or Letter. If you are sending the file to a country or office that prints on the other standard, choose it explicitly. Remember that a different page size also changes where lines and pages break.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s7.h': 'After the PDF exists',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s7.l1.1': 'If it is too large, run <tool_compress_pdf>Compress PDF</tool_compress_pdf>. Our guide to <post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality>compressing a PDF without losing quality</post_compress-pdf-without-losing-quality> explains the options.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s7.l1.2': 'To combine several converted documents, use <tool_merge_pdf>Merge PDF</tool_merge_pdf>; see <post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>how to merge PDF files</post_how-to-merge-pdf-files>.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s7.l1.3': 'To restrict who can open the file, add a password with <tool_protect_pdf>Protect PDF</tool_protect_pdf>. Our guide to <post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf>protecting and unlocking a PDF</post_password-protect-and-unlock-a-pdf> covers it in detail.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s7.p1': 'The same approach works for other Office files. <tool_excel_to_pdf>Excel to PDF</tool_excel_to_pdf> turns workbooks into tables, and <tool_powerpoint_to_pdf>PowerPoint to PDF</tool_powerpoint_to_pdf> gives one page per slide. <tool_html_to_pdf>HTML to PDF</tool_html_to_pdf> handles web content you have saved as a file.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s8.h': 'Troubleshooting',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s8.l1.1': 'The tool says the file is not a valid document: check that it is really a DOCX and not an old .doc renamed, and that it is not password-protected.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s8.l1.2': 'Pages break in different places than in Word: that is expected, because the fonts differ. Adjust the page size or spacing in Word and convert again.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s8.l1.3': 'A picture is missing: it was probably in an unsupported format or inside a text box. Re-insert it in line with the text as a JPEG or PNG.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.s8.l1.4': 'Text shows question marks: the characters are in a script the tool does not support yet.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.h': 'Frequently asked questions',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.1.q': 'Will the PDF look exactly like my Word document?',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.1.a': 'Not exactly. The tool does not use Microsoft Word, so fonts, spacing and page breaks differ. It reproduces the content and structure, not the exact layout, so check the result before you send it.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.2.q': 'Can I convert an old .doc file?',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.2.a': 'Not directly. Open it in Word, save it as a DOCX file and convert that.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.3.q': 'Is my document uploaded to a server?',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.3.a': 'No. The conversion runs in your browser tab and the file stays on your device.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.4.q': 'Can I convert a password-protected document?',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.4.a': 'No. Remove the protection in Word first, then convert the document.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.5.q': 'Why is the text in my PDF a different font?',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.faq.5.a': 'The original fonts are replaced by Noto Sans so that the PDF can be built in the browser. This is also why line and page breaks can move.',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.cta.h': 'Convert a document now',
  'blog.word-to-pdf-without-losing-formatting.cta.p': 'Open <tool_word_to_pdf>Word to PDF</tool_word_to_pdf>, or browse all of the <all_pdf>PDF tools</all_pdf>.',
} as const;
